Research article 
Determinants and action paths of transboundary water pollution 
collaborative governance: A case study of the Yangtze River Basin, China 
Feifei Zhao a, Xinyi Shu a, Xu Zhao a,b,*, Meiwei Guo a 
a College of Economics and Management, China Three Gorges University, Yichang, 443000, China 
b Research Center for Reservoir Resettlement, China Three Gorges University, Yichang, 443000, China   
A R T I C L E  I N F O   
Keywords: 
Action path 
Collaborative governance 
Dynamic qualitative comparative analysis 
(QCA) 
Transboundary water pollution governance 
A B S T R A C T   
With rapid economic growth, the issue of water pollution has become increasingly prominent, and there is a 
consensus that river basin management systems and cross-regional management coordination mechanisms need 
improving. In this study, 171 transboundary sections of the Yangtze River Basin were matched with the data of 
57 cities to construct panel data from 2015 to 2021. Based on the four-dimensional framework of environ­
ment–determination–process–resources, the dynamic qualitative comparative analysis (QCA) method is used to 
identify the key influencing factors and action paths of water pollution collaborative governance effects. The 
results show that a single antecedent condition is not necessary to achieve efficient collaborative governance 
effects, and only the “number of collaborative governance” and “scale of collaboration” conditions played 
important roles. There are five paths that can achieve efficient collaborative governance effects: economy- 
oriented, ecology-oriented, technology-oriented, government-oriented, and all-oriented. Additionally, hetero­
geneous results show that the impact of the regional governance intention on efficient collaborative governance 
effect is limited in the middle and upstream sections of the Yangtze River Basin, while the downstream sections 
are more dependent on the basic condition of the basin. The results can help promote effective cross-regional 
collaboration in the Yangtze River Basin, provide scientific basis for regions to formulate targeted governance 
measures, and provide models for governance in other regions.   
1. Introduction 
With the acceleration of China’s industrialization and urbanization, 
water pollution problems have become more serious. From 2000 to 
2014, the total amount of wastewater discharged in China increased 
from 41.5 billion tons to 71.62 billion tons, and nearly one-third of lakes 
and rivers were polluted to some degree, according to the annual sta­
tistical report of China’s Ministry of Ecology and Environment. The 
Yangtze River basin flows through 19 provinces, municipalities, and 
autonomous regions in China. And the Yangtze River basin covers 18.8% 
of China’s total land area, making it the largest river basin in China. 
According to data released by the Yangtze River Water Resources 
Commission of the Ministry of Water Resources, more than 30 billion 
tons of wastewater are discharged into the river every year due to 
human activities, and the situation for water pollution prevention and 
governance is very serious. 
The Chinese government has made active efforts to control water 
pollution in the Yangtze River basin, promulgating a series of policy 
documents, such as the Water Ecology Monitoring Plan for the Yangtze 
River Basin (Trial) and the Action Plan for the Yangtze River Protection 
and Restoration, and innovating the river chief system management 
model (Xiao et al., 2021; Zhan, 2018; Zhang, 2018). However, the effect 
of this governance has not been satisfactory. The governance of river 
basins has requirements that extend beyond those of general environ­
mental governance, as river basins have unique attributes, such as 
mobility, transboundary, integrity, synergy, and negative externalities 
(Chen and Gong, 2023). Therefore, each administrative unit in the basin 
actively manages water pollution in its respective region However, when 
the scope of governance reaches the boundary of the administrative unit, 
the “beggar-thy-neighbor” phenomenon occurs due to poorly defined 
responsibilities and poor communication (Wang, 2023), which reduces 
the overall effect of governance (Wang and Wang, 2023). 
To solve the problem of ineffective governance of water pollution, 
the Chinese government has promoted cooperation and coordination 
among transboundary regions by holding local government meetings 
and signing many agreements to jointly solve the problem of water 
