pollution (Liao and Du, 2021; Xiao et al., 2021). However, trans­
boundary water pollution control requires a large amount of capital 
investment and technical support (Cao and Long, 2019). Due to differ­
ences in economic development levels, some regions cannot afford high 
governance costs, which prevents neighboring regions from forming a 
common governance concept. Based on this, the central government has 
adopted economic incentives, such as transboundary ecological 
compensation mechanisms (Wang, 2023; Xu and Liu, 2024), but there 
are obvious benefit distribution problems between upstream and 
downstream regions, resulting in little effect. The above problems mean 
that the government lacks sufficient motivation for cooperation, which 
makes it difficult to form an effective governance force. In this case, 
transboundary collaborative water pollution treatment is particularly 
urgent, and a consensus has been reached to promote collaborative 
regional water pollution treatment (Zhang and Wang, 2023). It is 
necessary to strengthen the mechanisms of communication and coor­
dination between governments and upstream and downstream regions 
to promote the participation of all parties, thus promoting the 
improvement and protection of the water environment. 
This study selects the Yangtze River Basin as the research area and 
analyzes in depth the main influencing factors and action paths that 
promote the transboundary collaborative governance of water pollution 
in the basin. This work will provide theoretical support and an experi­
ence reference to effectively improve the efficiency of the collaborative 
governance of water pollution and to innovate collaborative governance 
models. 
This study makes the following three contributions: First, it builds a 
comprehensive theoretical framework for cross-border collaborative 
governance. Research on the driving mechanism of collaborative 
transboundary water pollution treatment is still fragmented and lacks an 
integrated theoretical framework. Based on the four-dimensional 
framework of “environment-resolution-process-resources,” this study 
systematically identifies and analyzes the key antecedents affecting the 
transboundary collaborative governance of water pollution and estab­
lishes a comprehensive framework to evaluate the effect of this gover­
nance. This theoretical framework not only integrates existing research 
results but also provides a clear theoretical direction for future research 
on cross-border collaborative governance. Second, this study deepens 
the application and understanding of the dynamic qualitative compar­
ative analysis (QCA) method. When analyzing such complex social sci­
ence issues as cross-border collaborative governance, previous research 
has often adopted the traditional regression method, focusing on the 
inference of linear causality and ignoring the interaction of multiple 
factors that may exist in the actual situation. The dynamic QCA method 
adopted in this study not only helps reveal the complex action path of 
the transboundary collaborative governance of water pollution in the 
Yangtze River Basin from the perspective of configuration, but it also 
provides a more comprehensive, in-depth understanding of the causal 
mechanism by reducing the sensitivity of the relationship between 
variables. This provides a new perspective and approach for studying 
complex social issues similar to transboundary collaborative gover­
nance. Third, the concept of regional heterogeneity is introduced to the 
study of the collaborative governance of water pollution. Based on the 
regional heterogeneity of the water pollution collaborative governance 
effect, this study explores the differential model of regional collabora­
tive governance in the upstream, middle, and downstream regions of the 
Yangtze River. This analysis not only provides specific and targeted 
strategy suggestions for watershed transboundary water pollution con­
trol but also provides a new perspective for understanding the 
complexity and diversity of inter-regional collaborative governance. 
This study makes significant contributions at multiple levels, such as 
methodology, theoretical framework, and practical application; these 
contributions not only promote the depth of knowledge in collaborative 
transboundary water pollution treatment but also provide new ideas and 
directions for future research. 
2. Literature review 
As an important environmental management measure, trans­
boundary water pollution collaborative governance has gradually 
attracted academic attention (see Table 1). Some scholars have laid a 
theoretical foundation for transboundary water pollution collaborative 
governance. First, they have conducted in-depth research on the theo­
retical framework and practical models of collaborative governance, 
exploring the mechanisms for promoting water pollution governance 
between different regions and countries (Hassler et al., 2018; Shakthi 
et al., 2021). These include the development of policies and regulatory 
frameworks applicable to transboundary environmental issues (Johns 
and Vannijnatten, 2021; Lv and Liu, 2021) and discuss key issues such as 
interest coordination and resource allocation in collaborative gover­
nance (He and Ren, 2024; Yao et al., 2024). Second, scholars have 
focused on the establishment and maintenance of cooperative partner­
ships (Gao et al., 2019), emphasizing the importance of overcoming 
political, economic, and cultural barriers to establish effective cooper­
ative partnerships in transboundary water pollution governance (Zhan, 
2018). Concurrently, information sharing and technological collabora­
tive innovation were also considered key factors of collaborative 
governance, which can improve the efficiency and effectiveness of 
collaborative governance by promoting information flow and technol­
ogy exchange (Xie et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2023). 
These studies provide theoretical support for in-depth understanding 
of transboundary collaborative governance, and the effect evaluation of 
collaborative governance is a key link to testing governance results. 
Effective evaluation can not only reveal the actual effect of collaborative 
governance but also provide reference for future policy formulation. On 
this basis, scholars have fervently discussed the effect of water pollution 
collaborative governance. At present, evaluation of the effect of water 
pollution collaborative governance mainly focuses on the economic, 
social, and environmental dimensions. It usually adopts index system 
evaluations, mathematical model simulations, and case studies (Rui and 
Zhou, 2020; Shakthi et al., 2021), and proposes to build a government 
collaborative governance model for transboundary water environment 
based on synergetics (Wang and Shen, 2016). Additionally, scholars 
have explored the multiple influencing factors of collaborative gover­
nance from multiple dimensions such as interest status, social capital, 
system, information technology, resources, assessment, and trust (Jia 
and Li, 2021; Rao and Zhao, 2022; Scott, 2021; Wu and Zhuang, 2013). 
However, there are still some deficiencies in the existing research. 
First, there is no unified evaluation standard and index system for 
collaborative governance. Evaluation indicators and methods used by 
different regions and research institutions may vary, which leads to the 
incomparability of evaluation results. Second, the scope of research 
lacks universality. The current research consists mostly of theoretical 
discussions from a single perspective or is limited to factor analysis of a 
few cases. 
This study selected 171 transboundary sections in the Yangtze River 
Basin as the research object. Starting from the dimensions of regional 
governance environment, governance determination, governance pro­
cess, governance resources, and with a dynamic QCA method, it explores 
the antecedent conditions that have a significant impact on the effect of 
collaborative governance. By distinguishing the core and edge condi­
tions that influence the effect of transboundary water pollution collab­
orative governance, the study identifies the key driving factors leading 
to efficient regional collaborative governance effect and their configu­
ration mode selection, and clarifies the action path. Based on this, it 
provides a theoretical basis and practical support for the formulation of 
more effective environmental governance policies. 
3. Theoretical background and mechanism analysis 
Sustainable development theory emphasizes meeting current human 
needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet 
F. Zhao et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    
Journal of Environmental Management 360 (2024) 121217
3
their needs (Gao et al., 2020; Lv, 2023; Nguyen et al., 2023). On the one 
hand, as a concrete manifestation of environmental problems, trans­
boundary water pollution violates the equity advocated by sustainable 
development theory (Nguyen et al., 2023). The long-term and complex 
characteristics of water pollution control make it difficult to solve water 
pollution in a short period of time, which poses a threat to the living 
environment of future generations. On the other hand, based on the 
commonality principle of sustainable development theory (Nguyen 
et al., 2023), inter-regional cooperation can more effectively solve the 
problem of transboundary water pollution and promote the sustainable 
development of water resources (Hu et al., 2022; Yao et al., 2024). 
Furthermore, with the improvement of water quality and the rational 
utilization of water resources, the ecological environment in the basin 
will be restored and promoted (Ma et al., 2024; Ren and Zou, 2021). This 
will help provide sustainable support for the ecological environment and 
reliable water resources for future generations and achieve coordinated 
economic, social, and environmental development (Ahmad et al., 2024; 
Habib and Mourad, 2023). Therefore, this study believes that sustain­
able development theory provides appropriate theoretical guidance and 
direction for the treatment of transboundary water pollution. Only 
through comprehensive consideration of pollution control, ecological 
protection, and other factors in the Yangtze River basin can continuous 
improvement of the water environment in the Yangtze River basin be 
achieved while protecting the interests of future generations. 
Synergy theory emphasizes cooperation and coordination among 
different organizations, departments, or stakeholders to achieve com­
mon goals and solve common problems (Camarero and Tamarit, 1995; 
Li et al., 2023; Lu et al., 2022; Wang and Shen, 2016). In transboundary 
water pollution control in the Yangtze River basin, local governments 
must form a collaborative network to jointly develop and implement 
feasible solutions. Such collaboration not only integrates the resources 
and expertise of all parties but also promotes information sharing, risk 
sharing, and responsibility sharing, thereby improving the efficiency 
and effectiveness of governance (Wang and Shen, 2016; Xie et al., 2021; 
Zhang et al., 2023). In addition, regional cooperation theory advocates 
cooperation between different administrative units or stakeholders and 
emphasizes the importance of cooperation between administrative re­
gions in solving cross-border problems (Ernst, 1958; Li, 2017). In the 
Yangtze River basin, local governments must collaborate across 
administrative boundaries to effectively address transboundary water 
pollution (Liao and Du, 2021; Zhan, 2018) and to achieve continuous 
improvement of the water environment and the ecological health of the 
Yangtze River basin. 
Transboundary water pollution collaborative governance is a man­
agement mechanism designed for water pollution problems in the basin 
(Scott, 2021). It focuses on consultation, collaboration, and cooperation 
based on the common interests of the basin, and promotes local gov­
ernments to cooperate and ensure that all social stakeholders participate 
in water pollution governance through the coercive force of multi-party 
participation (Jia and Li, 2021). 
Based on the above theories and a comprehensive analysis of the 
transboundary collaborative governance of water pollution in the 
Yangtze River basin, this study categorizes the antecedent conditions of 
effective collaborative governance into four dimensions: the regional 
governance environment, regional governance determination, regional 
governance processes, and regional governance resources. 
3.1. Regional governance environment 
The Yangtze River covers a rich diversity of ecosystems and 
geographical features, and different areas within the basin face very 
different pollution conditions, forming stark regional differences. Some 
places have serious water pollution problems and poor water quality, 
while others have relatively clean water bodies and healthy ecological 
environments. This difference is due to many factors, including the level 
of industrial development, population density, and agricultural 
activities. First, areas of intensive industry are the main source of water 
pollution due to the release of large quantities of industrial wastewater 
(Jiao et al., 2022). Second, densely populated areas are also an impor­
tant source of water pollution due to domestic sewage and waste (J. Guo 
et al., 2018). Additionally, agricultural non-point source pollution is a 
major problem and frequent, excessive application of pesticides and 
fertilizers directly lead to water pollution (Shortle and Horan, 2017). 
Taking these factors into consideration, the difference in the basic 
conditions throughout the basin’s area have an important impact on the 
feasibility and effect of water pollution collaborative governance. 
3.2. Regional governance determination 
A healthy environment is a public asset, and the externality of 
environmental pollution determines that the government plays an 
indispensable role in environmental governance (Li, 2014). The Yangtze 
River Basin is vast and complex, involving many local governments. 
Regional governments must cooperate in water pollution governance to 
solve transboundary water pollution problems. However, different re­
gions have different governance needs and development strategies, and 
different governments may have different interest demands and policy 
preferences, which may lead to difficulties in collaborative governance 
(Xu et al., 2020). Concurrently, for promotion and political achieve­
ments, some local officials may adopt a beggar-thy-neighbor approach 
and ignore the negative impact on downstream regions (Zhou, 2004). 
Such behavior will not only influence the effect of collaborative gover­
nance but also aggravate regional contradictions and conflicts. There­
fore, the government’s governance determination influences the effect 
of water pollution collaborative governance, and the government must 
actively seek cooperation to ensure all parties have the same will for 
water pollution governance, so they can jointly reach agreements and 
decisions (Liao and Du, 2021). 
3.3. Regional governance process 
In the process of regional governance, the number and scale of 
governance are key factors influencing the effect of water pollution 
collaborative governance, which directly determine the effectiveness 
and sustainability of collaborative cooperation between governments. 
The amount of collaborative governance refers to the frequency of 
solving water pollution problems through cooperation and coordina­
tion. Water pollution problems are often complex and require long-term 
monitoring, assessment, and improved governance measures (Dai and 
Li, 2018). The frequency of collaborative governance plays an important 
role in the governance effect, and multiple collaborative governance 
actors can better adapt to the dynamic and continuous nature of water 
pollution problems. Through collaborative governance for many times, 
all parties can adjust strategies in time when problems are found and 
take effective measures to ensure the continuous improvement of 
governance effects. Moreover, since water pollution governance usually 
involves a balance of interests and consultation of multiple parties, this 
iterative and cooperative process helps the government and institutions 
build a more stable relationship of trust and collaboration and reduce 
misunderstandings and conflicts (Shajara et al., 2023). This will help 
promote the smooth progress of collaborative governance and improve 
the effect of governance. 
The scale of collaborative governance refers to the scope of gov­
ernments participating in collaborative governance. Large-scale collab­
orative governance can pool more resources, knowledge, and expertise, 
and provide more experience and perspective to better address complex 
water pollution problems. When multiple levels of government are 
involved, they can more comprehensively identify problems, formulate 
policies, and take actions to drive overall improvement in water pollu­
tion governance (Li and Lu, 2022). Additionally, water pollution prob­
lems are usually not limited to a specific region, but have transboundary 
and knock-on effects elsewhere (Wang and Li, 2023), so large-scale 
F. Zhao et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    
Journal of Environmental Management 360 (2024) 121217
4
collaborative governance can better address these challenges. Cooper­
ation between different governments and institutions also helps share 
resources and technologies, reduce cost, and improve governance effi­
ciency (Qin and Wang, 2022). 
3.4. Regional governance resources 
The economic status and technical level of different regions in­
fluences the allocation and utilization efficiency of regional resources 
(Li, 2023), which in turn influences the effect of water pollution 
collaborative governance. 
The level of economic development has a significant influence on the 
effect of water pollution collaborative treatment. Regions with a high 
level of economic development usually have more financial resources 
and can invest more money in environmental protection and water 
pollution governance (Sun et al., 2018). These funds can be used to build 
sewage treatment plants, improve sewage networks, promote cleaner 
production technologies, and so on (Huang and Cheng, 2017), thereby 
reducing water pollution. Additionally, highly economically developed 
regions have stronger management and regulatory capabilities (Whit­
ford and Helland, 2003), so they can more effectively implement envi­
ronmental laws and regulations, promote enterprise, and encourage 
residents to improve their production and lifestyles, which help reduce 
water pollution. 
Levels of technological use is also crucial to the effect of water 
pollution collaborative governance. Advanced sewage treatment and 
monitoring technology can improve the efficiency and precision of 
water pollution governance. Regions with a high level of technological 
implementation are often able to improve production methods and 
environmental protection technologies, improve the efficiency of 
sewage treatment, and the rate of water quality compliance (Zeng and 
Chen, 2022). Moreover, advanced monitoring technology can promptly 
and accurately monitor the pollution status of water bodies, provide 
scientific bases for decision makers, and guide the implementation of 
collaborative governance (Wang et al., 2020). Regions with high tech­
nical levels can also conduct environmental risk assessment and emer­
gency plan formulation to improve their ability to respond to water 
pollution events (Song et al., 2021). 
In summary, multiple factors—such as unbalanced development 
between regions and the diversity of pollution problems—jointly in­
fluences the complexity and effectiveness of environmental governance. 
