panel data from 2015 to 2021. The data sources include the China Urban 
Statistical Yearbook, the Ministry of Ecology and Environment, the 
provincial Department of Ecology and Environment, and the Regional 
Bureau of Ecology and Environment. In terms of data collection and 
processing, this study ensures that accurate and reliable data is collected 
and that appropriate data preprocessing is carried out to eliminate the 
impact of outliers, missing values, and other problems on data analysis 
results. The specific research objects are shown in Fig. 2. 
4.3. Measurement of variables 
4.3.1. Result variable 
Collaborative governance effect (GE). This study aims to explore the 
influencing factors and action paths of the transboundary water pollu­
tion collaborative governance effect in the Yangtze River Basin, so the 
transboundary collaborative governance effect is taken as the result 
variable. At present, scholars usually evaluate the effect of trans­
boundary environmental pollution governance in the following two 
ways. One is the subjective perception of environmental quality change 
(Nicola, 2015). The other is the change trend of specific environmental 
parameters (Tomas et al., 2006). When relying on subjective perception 
to evaluate the governance effect of transboundary environmental 
pollution, it is easy to produce deviation (Rao and Zhao, 2022), so this 
study makes evaluations based on the change trend of objective specific 
environmental parameters. The Ministry of Ecology and Environment of 
China adopted a single-factor evaluation method for the water quality 
category of evaluations of river sections, that is, according to the highest 
category of the index of the section in the evaluation period. When 
describing the water quality of the sections, we use the phrases “in line 
with” or “worse than,” according to the water quality of the section 
(grade I–V and poor V). The specific indicators are as follows. Based on 
data from the Ministry of Ecology and Environment of China, this study 
takes 2015 as the observation starting point, and the water quality 
compliance rate is recorded to evaluate the transboundary water 
pollution collaborative governance effect. 
4.3.2. Antecedent conditions 
Considering the mechanism of water pollution collaborative gover­
nance, this study selects six important antecedent conditions around the 
four dimensions of environment, determination, process, and resources. 
This study also discusses in depth the relationships between these 
antecedent conditions and how they jointly influence the effect of the 
collaborative governance of water pollution. The variable measure of 
each antecedent condition is described in the following subsections.  
(1) Regional governance environment 
Basic condition of the basin (BC). Water pollution usually indicates 
that human or natural factors are causing pollutants to enter the water 
body, resulting in a decline in water quality, which results in the loss of 
use value or normal function of the water (Liu et al., 2024). Water 
pollution in the Yangtze River basin is caused mainly by industrial 
wastewater discharge, agricultural non-point source pollution, urban 
domestic sewage discharge, and centralized discharge of the chemical 
industry along the Yangtze River. Therefore, this study takes regional 
industrial wastewater discharge as the index to measure the basic con­
ditions of the basin.  
(2) Regional governance determination 
Will dimension of regional governance (WD). The number and fre­
quency of water pollution policy documents issued by the government 
reflect its concern and determination to solve the problem of water 
pollution. In addition, government work reports differ from general 
texts; they are policy texts with the programmatic nature of governance, 
and as formal expressions of the government’s administrative will, they 
have a high degree of authority and directivity (Wen and Zhao， 2015; 
Wang and Li, 2017; Wen and Zhao，2015). Therefore, in reference to 
Teng and Wen (2018), this study takes water pollution-related docu­
ments issued by the government as an indicator for measuring the will 
dimension of regional water pollution governance. For the consideration 
of the authority, coverage, and timeliness of the text selection, the word 
frequency counted in this study mainly comes from the annual gov­
ernment work report of each city. “Water pollution governance,” “basin 
governance,” and “basin ecological protection” were search terms, and 
the texts were screened to select those related to water environment 
during the study period for statistics.  
(3) Regional governance process 
The number of collaborative governance (NG). News reporting is 
usually one of the main channels reflecting government actions and 
policies (Wang and Chen, 2023; Satinsky, 2017). When conducting 
collaborative governance activities, the government often releases 
relevant information through press conferences, press releases, official 
websites, and other means to announce its plans and achievements and 
the progress of its activities. An analysis of the number of government 
news reports can indicate, to some extent, the frequency and persistence 
of collaborative governance activities. Therefore, referring to Hu et al. 
(2022), this study used the search terms “collaborative,” “joint,” 
“cooperation,” and “communication” in the Ministry of Ecology and 
Environment and the ecological and environmental protection offices of 
each city, as well as related news reports, to count the number of 
transboundary water pollution collaborative governance. When the 
collaborative governance plan or agreement was effective, all collabo­
rative governance activities that occurred in that year were counted. The 
total number of effective water pollution collaborative governance 
agreements and water pollution collaborative governance activities 
conducted in each city during the study period were counted. To ensure 
the accuracy and reliability of the data, this study set strict screening 
criteria: only when the collaborative governance scheme or agreement 
was actually implemented in the current year and had achieved signif­
icant results was it included in the cooperative governance activities of 
that current year. In this way, the study counted the total number of 
effective joint water pollution governance agreements in each city 
during the study period, as well as the total number of actual joint water 
control activities. 
Scale of collaboration (SC). The scale of collaboration directly im­
pacts the effect and influence of water pollution control (Wu and Wu, 
2023). With reference to the practice of transboundary collaborative 
governance of environmental pollution in China, the transboundary 
collaboration can be divided into inter-provincial and intra-provincial 
collaboration. Inter-provincial collaboration will involve more vertical 
and horizontal levels of government, and its coordination and cooper­
ation will be more complex, thus influencing the collaborative gover­
nance effect. This study refers to Rao and Zhao (2022), and assigns the 
value of inter-provincial collaboration as 1 and intra-provincial collab­
oration as 0.  
(4) Regional governance resources 
The level of economic development (PG). Economically developed 
regions invest more money in water pollution collaborative governance, 
and the collaborative regions have enough material and market support. 
Some studies use GDP to measure a region’s economic development 
level (Xia et al., 2024; Zeng et al., 2023), but GDP may not reflect res­
idents’ real living standards. Per capita gross domestic product (GDP) 
can better reflect the impact of changes in real income level on envi­
ronmental quality. Therefore, this study refers to Hu et al. (2022) to 
measure the level of regional economic development with per capita 
GDP. 
The level of technology (RD). Some studies use the number of patent 
