thus reducing energy consumption and pollution emissions. This study 
refers to Godil et al. (2021) to measure the level of technology by the 
R&D investment of each region. 
4.4. Variable calibration 
Referring to the existing research (Guedes et al., 2016), this study 
used the direct calibration method to set 95%, 50%, and 5% of the case 
sample as the calibration anchors of the result variable and antecedent 
conditions with full membership, crossover, and no membership, 
respectively. When the membership value of the fuzzy set after cali­
bration is 0.5, it was adjusted to 0.5001, according to the existing 
research (Xiao et al., 2022). The specific calibration results are shown in 
Table 2. 
5. Empirical results 
5.1. Necessary conditions analysis 
Before the configuration analysis of the influencing factors of 
collaborative governance effect, necessity tests should be conducted on 
each antecedent condition to determine whether the single condition 
(including its non-set) is a necessary condition for influencing the 
collaborative governance effect (Zhang et al., 2023). The criterion of 
necessary condition analysis is that if the consistency level is higher than 
0.9, the condition variable can be regarded as a necessary condition of 
the result variable (Schneider and Wagemann, 2012). Table 3 shows the 
necessary condition analysis results of each antecedent variable. The 
consistency of the necessity analysis of a single condition are all lower 
than 0.9, indicating that none of the six antecedent conditions is 
necessary for the result variable. Therefore, the six conditions must work 
together to form a comprehensive water quality management strategy. 
Collaborative governance can make up for the deficiency of a single 
factor and improve the effect of water quality governance by integrating 
various resources. 
5.2. Configuration analysis 
As the core of the QCA method, configuration analysis aims to 
investigate how different combinations of antecedent conditions affect 
results. Following Zhang et al. (2023), this study set the consistency 
threshold to 0.8 during the construction of the truth table. To retain 
more than 75% of the case data in the truth table, the case number 
threshold was set to 1 and the PRI threshold to 0.75. According to the 
simple solution and intermediate solution of configuration analysis, the 
core and edge conditions in each configuration were identified, and the 
configurations of the result variable were obtained, as shown in Table 4. 
As seen in Table 3, there are five groups of configurations conforming to 
producing the efficient collaborative governance effect, with consis­
tencies of 0.897, 0.905, 0.893, 0.899 and 0.903, respectively; the overall 
consistency reaches 0.867. Both the consistency and the overall solution 
consistency are higher than 0.8, indicating that these five configurations 
can be regarded as sufficient conditions for efficient collaborative 
governance effect. According to the configuration theorization process, 
and referring to Tan et al. (2019), the configurations that produce effi­
cient collaborative governance effect are “economy-oriented,” “ecolo­
gy-oriented,” 
“technology-oriented,” 
“government-oriented,” 
and 
“all-oriented.”(see Fig. 3). 
