where the section is located are not necessary to produce efficient 
collaborative governance effects. These areas include Bazhong, Ganz­
hou, and Enshi, for example. 
Configuration A2: Ecology-oriented. Configuration A2 shows that in 
the case of good basic basin conditions, high governance will dimension 
and a large number of collaborative governance initiatives, combined 
with a large collaborative scale, can improve the collaborative gover­
nance effect. A possible reason is that the good basic conditions of the 
basin provide a more stable and reliable foundation for collaborative 
governance, and the high governance will dimension means there is a 
stronger common goal and cooperation desire, which can enhance the 
governance effect. In configuration A2, in cities with a high governance 
will dimension, the technical level and economic development level of 
the region are not necessary to produce an efficient collaborative 
governance effect. These areas include Xiaogan, Jingzhou, and Yichun, 
for example. 
Configuration A3: Technology-oriented. Configuration A3 indicates 
that with good basic conditions, more collaborative governance times, 
and low technical levels, combined with a large collaborative scale, the 
collaborative governance effect can be better. A possible reason is that 
when the number of collaborative governance initiatives is large, it is 
easier for all parties to establish cooperative relationships and work 
together, accumulating rich experiences in collaborative governance, 
which helps solve water pollution problems more effectively. However, 
as technological advances are accompanied by greater demand for water 
resources—including for production, cooling, and other industrial 
processes—large-scale development and management of water re­
sources can have a negative impact on water quality, thereby reducing 
the effectiveness of water pollution collaborative governance. In 
configuration A3, in cities with low technical levels, the collaborative 
governance will dimension of the area is not necessary to produce an 
efficient collaborative governance effect. These areas include Huaihua, 
Suining, and Chizhou, for example. 
Configuration A4: Government-oriented. Configuration A4 shows 
that when the governance will dimension is high, the number of 
collaborative governance initiatives is large, and the technical level is 
low, combined with a large collaborative scale, collaborative gover­
nance can achieve better results. A possible reason is that a higher 
governance will dimension may mean more stringent environmental 
regulations and policies to strengthen the supervision and management 
of water quality. Concurrently, multiple collaborative governance ini­
tiatives can promote information sharing, resource integration, and joint 
prevention and control of problems, and promote the collaborative 
governance effect. In configuration A4, in cities with a high governance 
will dimension and a large number of collaborative governance initia­
tives, the basin’s basic condition and the economic development level of 
the area are not necessary to produce an efficient collaborative gover­
nance effect. These areas include Ji’an, Shiyan, and Zhaotong, for 
example. 
Configuration A5: All-oriented. Configuration A5 indicates that 
when the basic condition of the basin is good, the governance will 
dimension is high, the technical level is low, and the economic devel­
opment level is high, the collaborative governance effect can be better 
combined with the larger collaborative scale. The possible reason is that 
areas with high governance will dimensions and high economic levels 
