5.3. Robustness test 
Referring to Li and Rao (2023), this study tests the robustness of the 
configuration that produces an efficient collaborative governance effect. 
First, the configuration result is unchanged after the consistency 
threshold is raised from 0.8 to 0.9. Second, the configuration obtained 
after raising the case threshold from 1 to 2 is the existing path. Finally, 
the consistency threshold is increased to 0.9 and the case threshold is 
increased to 2, and the obtained configuration path is still the original 
configuration result. Therefore, the results of this study are robust. 
5.4. Inter-group analysis 
To further investigate the temporal effects of the five configurations, 
this study discusses the inter-group consistency changes. Fig. 4 shows 
the inter-group consistency changes. The consistency levels of the five 
configurations mostly fluctuate above 0.77, but the consistency of con­
figurations 1 and 5 were lower than 0.77 in 2016, or 0.766 and 0.752, 
respectively. However, such fluctuations were isolated to 2016, not 
randomly distributed, and were not a benign deviation (Garcia-Castro 
and Ari˜no, 2016). On the one hand, the results of inter-group analysis in 
this study compensate for the lack of time-series configuration in the 
past; on the other, the results show that all five configurations have good 
explanatory power between 2015 and 2017–2021. The reason for the 
sudden decrease in 2016 may be that a series of documents and policies 
related to water pollution governance were introduced then, but insuf­
ficient experience in policy implementation or poor implementation 
efficiency led to the weak effectiveness of collaborative governance. In 
contrast, this result does not affect the overall explanatory power. 
6. Heterogeneity analysis 
In the real situation, due to the influence of resources endowment, 
geographical location, and economic levels, the water pollution 
collaborative governance effect shows obvious regional heterogeneity. 
These may be due to differences in the governance environment, 
determination, process, and resources in different regions. The down­
stream regions have developed economically, and heavy industry has 
generally shifted to the middle and upstream regions after the trans­
formation of industrial structures. Environmental pollution is relatively 
alleviated in these places, and the governance direction tends toward 
ecological protection. In contrast, economic development in the middle 
and upstream regions of the Yangtze River is ramping up, total growth is 
still the focus of development, and the heavy and chemical industries are 
pillars of the economy. Environmental pollution is relatively serious 
here, and pollution governance strategies are required. Therefore, the 
research samples in this study were divided into the middle and up­
stream regions and the downstream regions of the Yangtze River, and 
the original data set was calibrated and modeled respectively. The re­
sults are shown in Table 5; they explore the location effect of each 
dimension on the water pollution collaborative governance effect. 
For the middle and upstream regions of the Yangtze River, there are 
three configuration paths to producing an efficient collaborative 
governance effect. Among them, configuration B1 indicates that cities 
with excellent basic conditions and much collaborative governance can 
achieve an efficient collaborative governance effect on a large scale. The 
possible reason is that the climates of the middle and upstream regions 
of the Yangtze River are complex and diverse, including subtropical, 
temperate, and plateau climates, which are conducive to the 
