11
maintenance of biodiversity and the self-purification of the water 
ecosystem. Suitable climatic conditions also provide good conditions for 
vegetation growth, which is conducive to maintaining the stability and 
purification function of water and soil, and greatly reduces the difficulty 
of water pollution governance. Typical cases are found in Enshi, Xiao­
gan, and Huaihua. Configuration B2 indicates that cities with a large 
amount of collaborative governance and low technology levels can 
achieve efficient collaborative governance effects with the help of a 
larger collaborative scale. The possible reason is that some industrial 
enterprises in the middle and upstream regions rely on advanced 
pollution-control technologies. However, poor maintenance or improper 
operation may occur, leading to the leakage and discharge of pollutants, 
making the treatment effect and the development and use of technology 
show as a “reverse effect.” Typical cases are found in Luzhou, Pan­
zhihua, and Shiyan. Configuration B3 indicates that cities with large 
amount of collaborative governance and low levels of economic devel­
opment can achieve efficient collaborative governance effects by 
assisting with a larger collaborative scale and optimizing policy envi­
ronment. A possible reason is that the middle and upstream regions of 
the Yangtze River issued a large number of policy documents in response 
to the call for water governance, resulting in fragmentation and complex 
policies, which made it difficult for lower-level departments to under­
stand and implement the policies and hindered effective water pollution 
governance. The increased number of policy documents may also have 
led to the fragmentation of resources for regulators, making it difficult to 
effectively supervise and enforce the law, limiting its positive impact on 
the collaborative governance effect. Typical can be found in Bazhong, 
Hengyang, and Yongzhou. 
For the downstream regions of the Yangtze River, there are three 
configuration paths to producing efficient collaborative governance ef­
fect. Among them, configuration B4 indicates that cities with good basic 
conditions, much collaborative governance, and low levels of economic 
development, combined with high governance will and large collabo­
rative scale, can achieve an efficient collaborative governance effect. 
This may be because the downstream regions of the Yangtze Riv­
er—among the most economically important and densely populated 
regions of China—have long faced serious water pollution problems. 
Government departments have promulgated many laws and regulations 
on water pollution prevention, and governance has helped regulate the 
impact of various activities on the water environment, stipulating rele­
vant responsibilities and punishments to strengthen water pollution 
collaborative governance. Concurrently, government and widespread 
social understanding and support has promoted the smooth progress of 
collaborative governance. Typical cases can be found in Ji’an and 
Jingdezhen. Configuration B5 indicates that cities with a high gover­
nance will, large amounts of collaborative governance, and low tech­
nical levels can achieve efficient collaborative governance effect with 
good basic basin conditions and a large collaborative scale. This may be 
because downstream cities produce chemicals via manufacturing and 
operating high-tech products, leading to direct discharge into rivers, 
exacerbating water quality problems and failing to effectively improve 
the collaborative governance effect. The application of high-tech agri­
culture and biotechnology may also pose a threat to water bodies and 
have a negative impact on the collaborative governance effect. Typical 
cases can be found in Yingtan and Chizhou. Configuration B6 indicates 
that cities with good basic conditions, more instances of collaborative 
governance, and low levels of economic development can achieve effi­
cient collaborative governance effects assisted by a large collaborative 
scale and low technical levels. This may be because industrial, com­
mercial, and residential water consumption is relatively high in cities 
with high economic development, resulting in a large amount of 
wastewater discharge. Cities with high economic development are 
usually accompanied by accelerated urbanization, urban expansion, and 
changing land use, which makes it difficult to effectively control water 
pollution. Typical cases are Jingdezhen and Pingxiang. Additionally, the 
amount of collaborative governance is the core condition, and its scale is 
the edge condition in all six paths, which indicates that the amount of 
collaborative governance is the key factor in promoting a collaborative 
governance effect. 
7. Discussion 
With China’s acceleration in industrialization and urbanization, 
water pollution has become an increasingly serious problem. As one of 
the largest river basins in China, the Yangtze River Basin faces serious 
water quality challenges. Based on the four-dimensional framework of 
environment–determination–process–resources, this study used the dy­
namic QCA method to deeply explore the influencing factors and action 
paths of the transboundary water pollution collaborative governance 
effect in the Yangtze River Basin. The results not only provide a scientific 
basis for transboundary collaboration in the Yangtze River Basin but also 
provide a model for governance in other regions. 
According to the research results, the single antecedent condition is 
not the necessary condition to produce efficient collaborative gover­
nance effects. This conclusion is similar to the views of Cao and Long 
(2019) and Yao et al. (2024). It emphasizes the diversity and complexity 
of the collaborative governance effect. In practice, collaborative 
governance should not over-rely on a specific factor but should 
comprehensively consider the joint action of multiple factors. Concur­
rently, the frequency and scale of collaborative governance play a 
relatively universal role, which means that frequent collaboration and 
extensive participation are the key factors to improving the governance 
effect. This finding is consistent with previous literature reviews (Jia and 
Li, 2021; Wang and Wang, 2023; Wang and Zhang, 2023). By 
strengthening communication and collaboration among various sub­
jects, the scale and scope of collaborative governance can be expanded 
to achieve a more efficient collaborative governance effect. This 
conclusion also confirms the synergy theory and regional cooperation 
theory mentioned above (Camarero and Tamarit, 1995; Ernst, 1958) and 
aligns with the literature reviews that found cooperation between 
different administrative regions is very important for solving trans­
boundary problems (Chen and Gong, 2023; Li, 2017; Liao and Du, 2021; 
Yang et al., 2023a,b). 
This study found that there are five functional paths that can achieve 
efficient collaborative governance effects: “economy-oriented,” “ecol­
ogy-oriented,” “technology-oriented,” “government-oriented,” and “all- 
oriented” paths. However, in the “technology-oriented,” “government- 
oriented,” and “all-oriented” paths, there are areas with low technology 
levels, and the more efficient the collaborative governance effect is. This 
finding is contrary to the results of Zeng and Chen (2022) and Song et al. 
(2021), who found that the higher the technical level, the better the 
collaborative governance effect. This means that in areas with high 
levels of technology, there may be an over-reliance on technology at the 
expense of other elements of governance. In fact, previous studies have 
found that the development of technology has a double-edged sword 
