effect on environmental governance (Du and Deng, 2024; Yue et al., 
2024). While improving treatment technology, technological develop­
ment also creates new pollution sources or pollution methods. For 
example, the acceleration of industrialization and urbanization and the 
continuous emergence of new pollutants and pollution pathways, such 
as new chemical pollution and microplastic pollution (Xu and Yin, 
2021), bring new challenges to this governance work. Moreover, the 
actual promotion and application of technology are limited by capital, 
human resources, and the institutional environment (Guo et al., 2024; 
Habib, 2023), resulting in the inability to fully apply the technology. 
This study also found differences in the collaborative governance 
effect between the middle and upstream and the downstream regions of 
the Yangtze River Basin. The impact of the degree of regional gover­
nance intention on the efficiency of collaborative governance is limited 
in the middle and upstream regions, which may be related to their level 
of economic development and the allocation of governance resources in 
the middle and upstream regions. In the downstream regions, the 
effectiveness of collaborative governance is more dependent on the basic 
conditions of the basin. This result is consistent with the views of Wu and 
Zhuang (2013). In accordance with sustainable development theory (Lv, 
2023; Nguyen et al., 2023), when formulating governance strategies in 
the Yangtze River basin, the characteristics of and differences among 
regions should be fully considered to achieve local conditions and form 
long-term benefits (Ahmad et al., 2024; Ma et al., 2024; Yang et al., 
2023a,b; Yao et al., 2024). 
The research method and theoretical framework of this study pro­
vide a reference for transboundary water pollution collaborative 
governance in other regions. With the dynamic QCA method, we can 
more fully identify the key factors that influence the collaborative 
governance effect and the interaction paths between these factors. 
Moreover, 
the 
four-dimensional 
framework 
of 
environ­
ment–determination–process–resources provides a comprehensive and 
systematic analysis perspective, which is conducive to a deeper under­
standing of the internal mechanism of collaborative governance. 
However, this study had some limitations that should be further 
studied in future studies. First, the scope of this study was limited to the 
municipal level, which is of limited significance for the guidance of 
transboundary water pollution governance in lower-level administrative 
units, such as counties and villages. The main consideration is that there 
are relatively few innovation subjects in lower-level administrative 
units. Their governance capacity and resources are relatively limited, 
relying mainly on financial subsidies or project funds from central or 
higher-level governments. In contrast, cities have stronger technical, 
economic, and human resource advantages in water pollution gover­
nance, and they are more able to assume a leading role in transboundary 
water pollution governance. Future studies may consider refining data 
sources and exploring ways to influence transboundary collaborative 
governance effects at the county or village level to improve the uni­
versality and applicability of this study. Second, this study focused 
mainly on the influencing factors and action paths of the collaborative 
governance effect. It did not conduct in-depth analysis of the specific 
performance of the governance effect due to the difficulty of obtaining 
some water quality disclosure data. Therefore, potential avenues for 
future research can be optimized by strengthening the analysis of spe­
cific manifestations of governance effects. For example, a quantitative or 
qualitative assessment of the effectiveness of governance in a particular 
area or case could be conducted. This would help to assess the effec­
tiveness of collaborative governance more accurately and would provide 
more specific practical guidance for policy formulation and practice. 
8. Conclusions and policies 
8.1. Conclusions 
Using the dynamic QCA method, this study took 171 transboundary 
sections in the Yangtze River Basin as cases, integrated six antecedent 
conditions affecting the water pollution collaborative governance effect 
around 
the 
four-dimensional 
framework 
of 
environ­
ment–determination–process–resources, and explored the multiple 
concurrent causal mechanisms affecting water pollution collaborative 
governance from 2015 to 2021. Combined with the specific situation of 
the Yangtze River basin, it analyzed the configuration differences of 
different locations and dimensions driving water pollution collaborative 
governance. The main research conclusions are as follows: (1) A single 
antecedent condition is not a necessary condition for efficient collabo­
rative governance, and only the “number of collaborative governance” 
and “scale of collaboration” conditions played an important role in 
achieving an efficient collaborative governance effect. (2) There are five 
paths to achieving efficient collaborative governance, namely “econ­
omy-oriented,” “ecology-oriented,” “technology-oriented,” “govern­
ment-oriented,” and “all-oriented.” (3) Heterogeneity analysis shows 
that for the middle and upstream regions of the Yangtze River Basin, the 
configuration paths that produce an efficient collaborative governance 
effect are greatly affected by the basin’s basic conditions, technical 
levels, and economic development levels, respectively, while the 
downstream regions are more dependent on the basin’s basic conditions. 
8.2. Policies 
Based on the above research, this study proposes the following pol­
icies for promoting transboundary water pollution collaborative 
governance:  
(1) Integrating multi-party collaborative governance mechanisms, 
the key policy directions include increasing the frequency and 
scale of collaborative governance. Governments have a key role 
to play in this regard and should take proactive measures to 
promote collaborative governance. All stakeholders, including 
government departments, non-governmental organizations, en­
terprises, and social groups, are encouraged to actively partici­
pate in collaborative governance and form cooperative 
partnerships. Concurrently, a collaborative governance platform 
should be established to promote information sharing, collabo­
ration, and decision-making among all parties. Through an 
effective platform, various stakeholders can better coordinate 
their actions and ensure the proper integration of governance 
actions and resources to promote a comprehensive and efficient 
governance result.  
(2) Adopt diversified policy paths, including ecological, economic, 
and technological support. The ecology-oriented policy path fo­
cuses on preserving natural ecosystems and protecting water 
sources such as wetlands, forests, and rivers to reduce the input of 
pollutants and deterioration of water quality. Governments can 
do this by instituting regulations, establishing ecological re­
serves, and promoting sustainable land use. The economy- 
oriented policy path emphasizes the impact of economic activ­
ities on water pollution, and the government can guide industries 
and individuals to improve their production and consumption 
patterns through incentives to reduce the consumption and 
pollution of water resources. The technology-oriented policy 
approach emphasizes the importance of scientific research and 
technological innovation, investing more resources to support 
technological innovation, and developing new technologies 
applicable to environmental governance, which can help improve 
governance effect. It also reduces over-dependence on resources 
and promotes sustainable and innovative governance.  
(3) Formulate strategies based on local conditions to meet their 
specific governance environment and development needs. For the 
middle and upstream regions, the local governments can 
strengthen the construction of water pollution monitoring and 
early warning systems, and promote industrial restructuring to 
reduce pollutant emissions. It is also necessary to strengthen 
F. Zhao et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    
Journal of Environmental Management 360 (2024) 121217
13
awareness of water resources protection and the publicity and 
enforcement of regulations. Additionally, to adapt to the gover­
nance environment in the middle and upstream regions, the local 
governments can encourage technological innovation and pro­
mote the application of environmental protection technology in 
the local area to improve the water pollution governance effect. 
The downstream regions usually bear the impact of pollutants 
from upper regions, and governance is relatively more complex. 
The government should pay more attention to regional gover­
nance will, strengthen cooperation and communication with the 
upper regions, and jointly formulate effective water pollution 
prevention and governance measures to ensure the effective 
implementation of collaborative governance. Additionally, the 
government should strengthen their assessment of the environ­
mental carrying capacity of downstream regions to improve the 
water pollution governance collaborative effect. 
