Representative quotations: 
Michaels, [83]: “The second paradigm, the 
‘technology’ paradigm, reflects how Israel and Jordan 
increasingly emphasize innovative technological 
solutions to water scarcity, including unilaterally 
developing unconventional water sources to augment 
supply [56]. As above, the overriding concern is still 
focused on maintaining security of freshwater supply 
for urban populations, however, this can now be 
ensured through technological advancement rather 
than military or diplomatic efforts.” (p. 292)  
Wegerich [127]: “…water supply security could be 
defined as a resilient system capable of coping with 
shocks, abuses and threats through direct security 
measures (surveillance and guards) and indirect or 
more passive measures through increasing 
maintenance and additional or alternative water 
supply sources, duplication of or less reliance on 
critical infrastructure to better cope with temporary 
shortages in water source availability as well as water 
rights or allocations to cope with competitions” (p. 
4661)  
Wilson et al. [131]: “This perspective of a resource 
bounded by its natural limits and, in this case, 
threatened by climate change is reflected in how water 
security is framed not as a social problem but as a 
hydrologic one requiring the capture of upstream 
abundance.” (p. 468) 
Resource Governance (n =
17): 
e.g., [119,39,85,86,77,99,129,4,63,67] 
Description: 
management, policy, institutions 
Representative quotations: 
Koff et al. [67]: “Water security, defined as the 
capacity of a population to safeguard sustainable 
access to adequate quantities of acceptable quality 
water for sustaining livelihoods, human well-being, 
and socio-economic development, for ensuring 
protection against waterborne pollution and water- 
related disasters, and for preserving ecosystems in a 
climate of peace and political stability[50].“ (p. 281)  
Magsig [77]: “…this paper follows a broader 
understanding of security and regards a community to 
be water secure when it has sustainable access to 
freshwater of sufficient quantity and quality, or to the 
benefits derived there from; and the ability to 
minimize water-related risk and its various 
repercussions to an acceptable level – without 
compromising the supporting ecosystems [76]"" (p. 
342)  
UNESCO, 2013 [118]: Water security is “the 
capacity of a population to safeguard access to 
adequate quantities of water of acceptable quality for 
sustaining human and ecosystem health on a 
watershed basis, and to ensure efficient protection of 
life and property against water-related hazards – 
floods, landslides, land subsidence, and droughts.” (p. 
12)  
Scott et al. [108]: “We thus propose a unifying 
definition: Water security constitutes the sustainable 
availability of adequate quantities and qualities of 
water for resilient societies and ecosystems in the face 
of uncertain global change.“ (p. 281) 
