identiﬁed (see part D in Fig. 1). Such a misﬁt might take diﬀerent forms,
which in turn call for distinct solutions. On the one side, polluters can
be located in an area of regulatory vacuum where no or non-suﬃcient
policies addressing the particular pollution are introduced. Where
polluters remain unregulated, the only policy option at hand is to react
further downstream through curative measures of puriﬁcation.
On the other side, a misﬁt might occur when a water supplier is
aﬀected by pollution coming from outside the own jurisdiction or
competence area. In such a case, direct as well as indirect collaboration
with other actors or stakeholders seems crucial (see also Sayles and
Baggio, 2017). For instance, it might be of importance that public au-
thorities of the jurisdiction the water supplier is part of are members of
transboundary
waterbody
associations
which
regulate the
entire
catchment area. Likewise, the water supplier might wish to establish
direct collaborative ties with other suppliers or international water
supply associations in order to set-up joint alarm systems in case of
emergency.
The aim of this paper is to conduct a ﬁrst and preliminary analytical
step in this context to help develop an interdisciplinary research ap-
proach which identiﬁes social-ecological misﬁts through spatial visua-
lization. We argue that science and practice both beneﬁt from such an
identiﬁcation and visualization of social-ecological misﬁts as these vi-
sualizations can be a supporting tool for the design of further methods
and the development of new management or regulatory strategies.
2.2. Challenges in transboundary drinking water management
For the case of micropollutants, their spread in rivers and their
management and regulation in the Rhine River catchment area, we can
identify three major characteristics that particularly challenge the so-
cial-ecological ﬁt:
The ﬁrst challenge concerns the transboundary nature of persistent
pollutants transcending borders and jurisdictions. The ﬁnding of an
appropriate solution is complicated by the cutting across intra- and
international borders and by the fact that downstream entities depend
on the action of the upstream riparian (Newig and Fritsch, 2009). Im-
pacts on water resources in the upstream part of the river, e.g. sewage
water
discharges, hydropower
or irrigation, create
negative ex-
ternalities to downstream countries (Nesheim et al., 2010). From a
proﬁt maximization perspective upstream countries behave rationally if
they use water resources without paying for pollution abatement, thus
having a high incentive to do so (Ostrom, 2005). Downstream coun-
tries, on the other hand, often depend on the river’s water resources and
therefore have to engage in water treatment as a response. In our case,
the Rhine serves as drinking water reservoir for some parts of Swit-
zerland, the Netherlands and Germany, which thus depend on a good
raw water quality. A dilemma is created when upstream countries re-
fuse to abate pollution, fearing that downstream countries will free-ride
on the measures of the upstream country (Willett and Sharda, 1997).
The second and third challenges are created through the cross-sec-
toral and multi-level nature of water quality and micropollutant issues:
The resource water is strongly aﬀected by other domains (e.g. land
use, agriculture, climate change) and its uses are of interdependent
nature (e.g. ﬂoods, water supply, hydropower, etc.) which in turn are
managed by a variety of actors belonging to diﬀerent decisional levels
and entities of implementation (Lubell and Edelenbos, 2013). The cross-
sectoral nature of the problem is further enhanced by the fact that
micropollutants can stem from a variety of activities such as pesticide
use in agriculture, consumption of cleaning products in households or
the industrial production of pharmaceuticals. All those activities can
enhance the entry of substances into waters, be it through point or
diﬀuse sources. So the physical extent of the pollution can take various
shapes and cover diﬀerent areas which makes it diﬃcult to adapt
management and regulatory areas to it. Furthermore, the multi-level
nature of the issue is enforced by the fact that some of the countries
within the Rhine catchment area are members of the European Union,
while Switzerland is not. That means that the jurisdictions located
along the Rhine sometimes implement the same, sometimes diﬀerent
policies to tackle the issue of micropollutants. This policy diversity
requires coordination across jurisdictions
To assess transboundary vertical (multi-level) and horizontal (cross-
sectoral) dynamics in the governance system, social network analysis
has been prominently applied in research (Sayles and Baggio, 2017;
Berardo and Lubell, 2016; Bodin et al., 2014; Crona and Bodin, 2006).
Adopting a social network analysis perspective allows for a broad, non-
hierarchical actors' integration: it permits us to grasp tie creation
among a variety of stakeholders or actors responsible for, contributing
to or aﬀected by natural resource degradation (Berardo and Scholz,
2010; Prell et al., 2009). This can result in a network where actors share
diﬀerent attributes such as representing local, regional, national or
international levels and diﬀerent sectors (see part A in Fig. 1).
Social network analysis applied to apprehend relational dimensions
in the governance system enables us to see if the transboundary, multi-
level and cross-sectoral dynamics challenge or improve the ﬁt. When
the actors involved in micropollutants’ management and regulation in
the Rhine catchment area manage to overcome transboundary, multi-
level or cross-sectoral conﬂict lines, then this reduces the potential
social-ecological misﬁt. The underlying assumption is that the colla-
borative tackling of a common pool resource problem helps aligning the
area of management and regulation to the transboundary extent of
pollution. To test this assumption we will compare the area of pollution
to the areas of management and regulation respectively.
3. Methods and material
Although a variety of disciplines mention social-ecological misﬁt, stu-
dies rarely adopt a de facto interdisciplinary approach in which manage-
ment and regulation issues are brought together with a natural-scientiﬁc
analysis (for exceptions, see Sayles and Baggio, 2017; Barnes et al., 2016).
The research presented here addresses this gap and combines mass ﬂow
analysis and thus insights from chemistry, hydrology and physics with
policy and social network analysis. The ﬁrst step in our research design
consists of identifying cases where drinking water suppliers are aﬀected by
the issue of aquatic micropollutants. The major selection criteria is that
drinking water providers use Rhine water through river bank ﬁltration that
might potentially be impacted by the occurrence of micropollutants. Using
this criterion we selected two of the most important water suppliers along
the Rhine, based in the cities of Basel in Switzerland and Düsseldorf in
Germany, as ideal case studies. Basel lies in the tri-point border area where
Switzerland, Germany and France meet, whereas Düsseldorf is embedded
within a strongly populated and industrialized area within Germany. In
Basel, two water suppliers, the Hardwasser AG (here Water Works Basel:
WWB) and Industrial Waterworks Basel (IWB), are responsible for providing
the entire area of the city of Basel (and some neighboring municipalities)
with drinking water; in Düsseldorf this task is provided by Stadtwerke
Düsseldorf AG (here Water Works Düsseldorf: WWD).
3.1. Micropollutants and the selection of compounds
For the following analysis, we selected micropollutants entering
waters via diﬀuse sources (stemming from agricultural practices) and
point sources (pharmaceuticals originating from households entering
via WWTP). Given the large number of compounds (Petrie et al., 2015;
Schymanski et al., 2014), we selected four model compounds re-
presentative of the spatial patterns of point and diﬀuse sources of mi-
cropollutants. To that end, two widely used herbicides and two human
pharmaceuticals were selected according to the following criteria:
K. Ingold et al.
Global Environm
ental Change 48 (2018) 136–150
138
• Each compound must be representative for the use classes in the
Rhine River basin which implies that it is approved for application
or consumption in all riparian countries.
• Data about the use of the compound as well as water quality mea-
surements must be available.
• The compound must be relevant for drinking water production and
health concerns. Generally, this is the case if the compound occurs
in relatively high concentrations and has a high persistency in
passing the river bank ﬁltration systems.
On the basis of these criteria, the herbicides isoproturon (IPU) and
S-metolachlor (MEC) were selected. IPU is widely used against grasses,
mainly on cereals. IPU is measured in large parts of the river network
with concentrations varying according to spring and fall application
(Wittmer et al., 2014). MEC is used against grass on maize, sugarbeet,
pulses and soya ﬁelds. The selected pharmaceuticals include the psy-
chiatric drug carbamazepine (CMZ) and the antibiotic sulfamethox-
azole (SMX). Both are widely found in streams in comparably high
concentrations. All four compounds have been shown to be persistent
during river bank ﬁltration (Bertelkamp et al., 2014; Herberer et al.,
2011; Huntscha et al., 2013; Storck et al., 2010; Tuxen et al., 2000).
Table 1 summarizes the selected model compounds and their char-
acteristics.
3.2. Spatially distributed mass ﬂow model
Via mass ﬂow analysis we modelled the physical extent of the
problem related to micropollutants. To that end, the model calculates
for any given point, X, along the river network the annual load of each
of the four model compounds, i, discharged from the entire upstream
catchments. The river network is divided into river segments. For each
segment, we calculated the local load contributions. Three consecutive
steps helped us identifying the physical extent of pollution (see part B in
Fig. 1):
In a ﬁrst step, the source maps of the four compounds were calcu-
lated. Source maps show the areas or points where the substances enter
the stream network. For herbicides, the maps display the applied mass
of herbicides per unit area; for pharmaceuticals source maps represent
the consumed mass by the population connected to the WWTPs. The
respective consumption data was derived from sales statistics and
consumer studies respectively (see Appendix A in Supplementary ma-
terial). The amounts sold are assumed to be applied or consumed within
the statistics region and during the reported year. Hence, the regional
or national sales statistics of herbicides were scaled to the substance
application area which corresponds to the agricultural area of crops
possibly treated with the herbicide. For pharmaceuticals, the con-
sumption per capita was calculated by dividing the sales data from
country n with the corresponding population. Details of the down-
scaling procedure are described in Appendix A in Supplementary ma-
terial (see also Bergmann et al., 2011; Besse et al. 2008; Clara et al.,
2005; Göbel et al., 2005; Leip et al., 2008; Lienert et al., 2007; Maurer
and Herlyn, 2006; Miege et al., 2009; Moschet et al., 2013; Ort et al.,
2009; Singer et al., 2016; Vogt et al., 2007).
In a second step, we estimated the loads that are locally discharged
into the stream network by means of a use class-speciﬁc approach. The
agricultural load Lherb is estimated from the locally applied herbicide
mass Mherb, upstream and an empirical loss rate which describes the
fraction of the herbicide mass lost to surface waters. For the sake of
simplicity, we used a constant, average ε, as an approximation resulting
in the following equation for agricultural load:
