In a third step, the concrete area of the Rhine River catchment area
covered by the model compounds from their source until the point of
interest (that is the two cities) was identiﬁed. This physical extent of
pollution is deﬁned as physical impact zone: we calculated the cumula-
tive load of a compound entering the river network as a function of
increasing distance, beginning from the point of interest (here Basel or
Düsseldorf) and moving from there further upstream along the river
network. This allowed us to delineate the areas which contribute, for
example, 25% or 50% of the load aﬀecting the water supplier in the city
of Düsseldorf.
3.3. Networks of management and regulation embedded in the governance
system
To assess dynamics in the governance system that tackles the issue
of micropollutants, we distinguished between two diﬀerent types of
steering, i.e. management and regulation (see Gerber et al., 2009;
Knoepfel 2007). We argue that both are important when it comes to
address water quality issues. By regulation, we understand the design
and implementation of public policies that set pollution mitigation and
adaptation targets and deﬁne instruments to achieve those targets (Knill
and Tosun, 2012). Management is conceived as the more im-
plementation-oriented, technical side of water supply. It embraces
strategies aiming at the reduction of micropollutants in waters, which
are directly implemented during water collection, treatment and dis-
tribution to the larger population.
To study regulation and management in water quality in general,
and of micropollutants in particular, we apply social network analysis.
As mentioned above, social network analysis was prominently applied
to research on common pool resource dilemmas and natural resource
governance (Bodin et al., 2006; Prell et al., 2009; Sayles and Baggio,
2017). Social network analysis grasps the structural organization of
actors when it comes to regulating or to implementing water protection.
The regulation networks comprise public and private actors involved
in Swiss and German national-level policymaking regarding micro-
pollutants. In both countries, the issue is ﬁrst and foremost tackled at
the national level. Nevertheless, sub-national and regional actors are
network members as they participated in decision-making processes
(see subsequent paragraph; and also Sayles and Baggio, 2017 who
analysed multi-level collaborative structures between local and regional
actors).
In the Swiss case, the network includes all actors participating in the
amendment of the Waters Protection Act and Ordinance between 2007
and 2013. The adoption process of the German Surface Water
Ordinance between 2008 and 2011 deﬁnes the members of the German
regulation network. In order to identify actors participating in the
