reputational approach (Knoke, 1994). We thereby capture actors who
a) took part in the speciﬁed policymaking processes; b) hold key roles in
the political (sub)system; and c) are important in micropollutant reg-
ulation following the opinion of knowledgeable observers. We surveyed
the identiﬁed Swiss actors between April and July 2013. With a re-
sponse rate of 89% (AAPOR, 2011), the Swiss regulation network in-
cludes 52 policy actors. The German survey, conducted between Jan-
uary and April 2014, resulted in a response rate of 68% and a network
comprising 39 actors. We surveyed network members’ connections by
asking with whom their organizations closely collaborated during the
Swiss and German policy processes on micropollutants.
The management networks are ego-networks of the three water
suppliers in Düsseldorf (WWD) and Basel (IWB and WWB) who we had
interviewed in fall and winter of 2015. In an open question, we asked
them to name all possible actors they are in direct regular contact with
when managing the issue of micropollutants (i.e. strategies aiming at
the reduction of micropollutants implemented during water collection,
treatment and distribution). This resulted in a network with 40 actors in
Basel and 15 in Düsseldorf (see Supplementary material for full actors’
list
