Supplementary material). Additionally to water suppliers’ ego net-
works, the management networks include “within-ego-network” ties.
The latter are “institutional ties” between actors who exchange on
water pollution due to their institutionally given mandate, e.g. ties that
link local authorities to their regional or national counterparts.
By considering both, the regulation and the management networks,
we have a rather complete picture of the multi-level, cross-sectoral and
transboundary governance system. In this governance system, the two
networks are entangled. For instance, water suppliers, the key players
in management, are one type of target group of regulation and therefore
also part of the regulation network. Likewise, regional authorities are
embedded in both types of networks: in the management network, they
act as implementers of water protection policies; and in the regulation
network, they take part to represent regional interests in national-level
policy processes on water. In our overall sample, 8 organizations par-
ticipated in both, the management and the regulation network. Actors
who are active in both networks play a crucial role, because they link
implementation and regulation, e.g. by forwarding relevant informa-
tion. Actors involved in the management network might not directly
receive information from national authorities, but indirectly from those
gatekeepers. To account for such gatekeeping and mediator activities,
we did thus not only consider direct, but also indirect relations among
actors (analogue to the “institutional ties” in the management net-
works).
3.4. Spatial representation of misﬁt
By combining mass ﬂow and social network analysis, we seek to
identify and visualize the physical extent of pollution and the respective
areas of management and regulation.
To do so, we ﬁrst geo-referenced the actors of the management and
regulation networks in space, based on the coordinates of their postal
addresses. We then visualized the actors’ location and their ties with
each other and superposed this depiction on the physical extent of
pollution (e.g. the impact zones; see 4.3.1). The generated map shows
whether water suppliers are able (1) to connect to other actors in the
impact zones; (2) to reach out to the boundaries of the most distant
parts of the impact zone; and (3) to show how transboundary their
network is.
Network members have an area of competence in which they set up
(regulation) or implement (management) policies to tackle micro-
pollutants. Some actors are only competent or active in their munici-
pality or region, whereas others are competent or active in the whole
nation-state (typically national environmental agencies or other na-
tional authorities). Thus, the coverage of the Rhine basin by actors’
areas of competences varies spatially. We coded actors’ competence or
activity per administrative unit or jurisdictions (nation, state, and
municipality). The resulting map illustrates the types of jurisdiction
that the actors of the management and regulation networks of micro-
pollutants along the Rhine are part of. We superposed those jurisdic-
tional competence areas with the impact zones in which pollution
spreads in order to identify spatial overlaps and social-ecological misﬁts
(Figs. 12 and 13).
4. Results
4.1. Source maps and physical impact zones
Depending on the compound, the spatial distribution of the source
areas across the Rhine basin is quite diﬀerent (Fig. 2). First, there is a
separation between heavily urbanized areas and regions dominated by
agricultural activities such as the Alsace and the eastern Main basin.
