strategies are implemented to control micropollutants. The network
analysis allows us to study how water suppliers connect to upstream
actors where the pollution originates from.
In a ﬁrst step, we only considered direct contact relations (e.g. in-
formation exchange and joint strategies to manage micropollutants)
that water suppliers have when executing their public task. The man-
agement networks show that the suppliers seem to be most frequently
related to public authorities (Fig. 5). The networks of the two Swiss
suppliers entail over 58% of public authorities, mostly at the regional
(i.e. cantonal) level (32% and 40% respectively). The situation for the
German water supplier of Düsseldorf is slightly diﬀerent. It is less
prominently related to public authorities (29%) with no ties to national
authorities and most connections towards local authorities (18%).
Most interestingly, all water suppliers are poorly connected to pol-
luter organizations. No ties exist towards agriculture organizations
which represent actors accounting for most herbicide discharge into
waters. Swiss Water Works Basel (WWB) is not connected to any pol-
luter organization, whereas the other two have approximately 10% of
their connections to wastewater treatment plants through which phar-
maceuticals consumed in households ﬁnd their entry path into waters.
At this point, we can conclude that the drinking water providers
seem to reach towards public authorities, other drinking water provi-
ders and scientiﬁc actors, but they are not or only weakly connected to
polluter organizations.
As shown in Figs. 6 and 7, the three water suppliers are only in-
directly – that is through actors of their management network – linked
to the respective regulation network, mostly through links to national
or regional public authorities (orange or yellow nodes) or scientiﬁc
institutions and environmental NGOs (green nodes). The Swiss supplier
IWB and the German supplier WWD are both linked to polluter orga-
nizations (red nodes), but only to very few. They are only indirectly
linked to all other polluters and the majority of actors involved in policy
design (regulation networks) (for the composition of the management
and regulation networks by actor type, see Table C.1 in Appendix C in
Supplementary material).
Despite their rather peripheral position in the overall network, IWB
and WWB are still connected to important, central actors such as the
Federal Oﬃce for the Environment (Water Division; FOEN_W). By
contrast, the German network is generally more fragmented and WWD
only has a few selected ties that connect it to the regulation network.
Unlike the Swiss case, those public actors belong to the regional or local
level.
4.3. Spatial representation and misﬁt identiﬁcation
Concentrating on the location of an actor, and thus the point in
space representing the oﬃcial address of an organization, makes much
sense for point source polluters such as industries or wastewater
treatment plants and for local authorities such as municipalities or ci-
ties. But as most actors in our networks are active within an area rather
than just in one location – such as regional or national authorities,
transboundary associations or interest groups – we conceived in a
second step each actor's area of competence and action related to po-
licies and strategies tackling micropollutants. As several actors are ac-
tive within the same area, this results in a map of jurisdictions involved
in the management and regulation of micropollutants along the Rhine.
Finally, this allowed us to visualize the overlap and misﬁt of the area of
competences or action of actors and the impact zones of substances.
We only focus on the impact zone of isoproturon (IPU) here (ana-
lyses of the other three substances are illustrated in the Supplementary
material), because impact zones for the four selected compounds are
overall very similar (see Figs. 3 and 4). The impact zones deviate only
slightly, such that isoproturon has a bigger impact zone than the two
pharmaceuticals (CMZ, SMX) but a smaller one than the other herbicide
(MEC).
4.3.1. Geo-referencing networks in space
Fig. 8 shows the two water suppliers in Basel (IWB and WWB) which
are both integrated in what can be called the “Basel Cluster”. This
cluster includes all network members located within and in the region
near Basel. The water suppliers further have strong relations to two
other clusters, one representing the capital of the neighboring canton
(Liestal Cluster) and the other representing the largest Swiss city
(Zurich Cluster). All clusters are still integrated in the red impact zone
which is contributing 25% of the load of micropollutants to the water
suppliers in Basel. The two Basel suppliers do not seem to be well
connected to actors located in the area which contributes up to 50%
(yellow impact zone), but they are well-connected to the public au-
thorities and organizations in the Swiss capital (Bern Cluster) and even
to actors outside their impact zone (for example EPFL, UNIL, CIPEL).
One can see that there is a tendency of the two Basel suppliers to cover
the western area more than the eastern part of their impact zone.
The water supplier of the city of Düsseldorf (WWD) is similarly well
connected to actors in the red impact zone (mainly Düsseldorf and Bonn
