Clusters, see Fig. 9) and only sparsely connected to actors located in the
other parts of its impact zone. This is certainly also due to the fact that
Düsseldorf has a much sparser and larger impact zone than Basel. WWD
seems to account more for downstream connections and also holds re-
lations to actors outside the impact zone.
4.3.2. Comparison of impact zones with areas of competence
By comparing the impact zones of the two herbicides and the two
pharmaceuticals with the jurisdictions of public authorities responsible
for micropollutant regulation, one can quantify the transboundary
character of the water quality problem (Fig. 10). For both study sites
(Basel and Düsseldorf) it is obvious that the parts of the impact zones
that deliver, for example, 50% or 75% of the annual load cover a fairly
large number of states (meaning about 15 to 30 sub-national entities,
i.e. cantons or German Länder) and include 3 to 4 diﬀerent countries
(nation states; for more details, see Figure X in the Supplementary
material).
For both drinking water providers, the national borders imply a
marked change in the network. Only two countries, Switzerland and
Germany, belong to the impact zone of the water suppliers in Basel (A
in Fig. 11). The two Swiss suppliers are well connected to their re-
spective national network including a variety of public and private,
national, regional and local actors (blue part of the bar and blue box in
Fig. 11A). Unlike the supplier in Düsseldorf (B in Fig. 11) the Swiss
actors have more connections to actors outside their country (six dif-
ferent German actors in the green box, Fig. 11A); nevertheless, the
number of ties drops dramatically when going beyond the national
border.
The situation in Germany (B in Fig. 11) is slightly diﬀerent: still
considering only isoproturon (see Figure XI in the Supplementary ma-
terial for the other three substances), WWD is almost exclusively con-
nected to national and subnational actors from Germany which ac-
counts for more than 75% of the loads. Like Switzerland, the German
network of the water supplier is multi-level and cross-sectoral: it in-
cludes national, regional and local actors from diﬀerent public and
private sectors. WWD has no connection to French (red part of the bar
in Fig. 11B) actors and maintains only one single transboundary con-
nection with a Swiss scientiﬁc institute (blue part of the bar and blue
box in Fig. 11B).
When superposing the area of competence or action of the suppliers’
network connections (see Figs. 6 and 7) with the physical impact zones,
an overlap and a misﬁt between both can be identiﬁed. For instance, in
the Swiss case (Fig. 12) most of the physical impact zone (colored parts)
is also covered by actors’ action or competence area (grey parts) which
indicates a considerable overlap of the two areas. The 46 national actors
of the Basel network (management and regulation) cover almost the
entire impact zone. Most cantonal and local actors have their compe-
tence area near to the drinking water suppliers (darker area around
Basel). However, at the left side of the ﬁgure (Liechtenstein and Austria
in the East of Switzerland) no actor responsible for the regulation or
management of this part of the impact zone is involved in the networks.
This implies that the Swiss water suppliers in Basel and any other actor
of the regulation network lack connections to actors from these parts of
the impact zone. This white area on the left side (where micropollutants
occur which aﬀect water supply in Basel) is what we consider to be a
misﬁt between the physical extent of pollution and the area in which
this pollution is addressed through management and policy regulation.
Micropollutants stemming from the white area aﬀect water needed for
the water supply in Basel, but suppliers lack the means to reach out to
actors responsible for these sources of pollution.
This misﬁt is even more accentuated for the water supplier in
Düsseldorf (Fig. 13). The German part of the impact zone is covered by
37 national and 6 state actors (see also Appendix B in Supplementary
material, list B.2 and the level of competence indicated in the last row).
However, large parts of the impact zone on the left of Fig. 13, mainly
covering the French and Luxembourgian parts in the West, are white.
Fig. 8. Geo-referenced network of Basel (Base data: BAFU, 2012; BFS, 2009, 2011; Eawag, 2011; EEA, 2012; GEOSTAT, 2011; JRC, 2007, 2008; Swisstopo, 2007).
