This means that neither the water supplier in Düsseldorf nor any other
actor from the regulation network is linked to any actor having com-
petences or taking actions in this area although micropollutants from
this area spread into waters reaching Düsseldorf. As already seen above,
the connection to the Swiss actor, a research institute, further means
that WWD reaches out to only one actor in the most distant part of the
impact zone.
This analysis is complementary to the geo-referenced maps as out-
lined in sub-section 4.3.1, Figs. 8 and 9, highlighting that water sup-
pliers are connected to a considerable proportion of local and regional
actors. When now considering the competence areas of actors, as
highlighted in Figs. 12 and 13, one notices a large area with actors
active or responsible for the whole nation state, which equilibrates the
local and regional share. Transboundary competences are, again, rare
or even inexistent. This result has however to be nuanced: we also asked
representatives of the three water supply organizations to indicate
which regional, national and international water platforms and orga-
nizations they are members of. Appendix D (Table D.1) in Supple-
mentary material shows that suppliers are members of two or more,
mostly regional and national, basin-wide platforms. Such organizations
are supposed to account for transboundary water management, the
harmonization of measures such as emergency and alarm systems on
the international level and for contributions in national decision-
making processes.
5. Discussion
In the previous sections we have demonstrated that the impact
zones of the four selected model compounds (isoproturon, S-metola-
chlor, carbamazepine and sulfamethoxazole) cover substantial areas of
Fig. 9. Geo-referenced network of Düsseldorf (Base data: BAFU, 2012; BFS, 2009, 2011; Eawag, 2011; EEA, 2012; GEOSTAT, 2011; JRC, 2007, 2008; Swisstopo, 2007).
K. Ingold et al.
Global Environm
ental Change 48 (2018) 136–150
145
the upstream watersheds of the drinking water suppliers in Basel and
Düsseldorf. Due to the spatial distribution of the sources (see Fig. 2)
these impact zones also comprise many diﬀerent political jurisdictions,
rendering the issue of micropollutants a truly transboundary topic.
When choosing other compounds the resulting impact zones might
be diﬀerent in size and shape than those presented above. This could be
ﬁrst, because the respective sources could be distributed diﬀerently in
space; and secondly, because of a diﬀerent fate (“behavior”) of the
compounds in the aquatic environment.1 Regarding the ﬁrst argument,
one has to distinguish between urban (point) and agricultural (diﬀuse)
sources. For household chemicals like the pharmaceuticals included in
this study, the sources hardly vary in space as long as there are no
regional or national compound-speciﬁc diﬀerences in consumption or
distinct legal registration. Hence, source maps such as the one shown
for carbamazepine in Fig. 2 will hold true for many wastewater-born
household chemicals.
For agricultural compounds such as the herbicides, Fig. 2 clearly
reveals important diﬀerences in the spatial distribution of the relevant
crops. Nevertheless, the resulting impact zones are similar. Given the
spatial distribution of arable crops in the Rhine basin one can expect
similar impact zones for most pesticides applied to major crops in the
area.
From a regulation perspective, this similarity between the impact
zones of diﬀerent chemicals means that the area of physical impact, and
the area of regulation, are likely to be very similar to the ones we in-
vestigated, even if diﬀerent target and source groups might have to be
addressed by diﬀerent policies.
We observe an overlap or misﬁt when taking the area of physical
impact of micropollutants as an indicator of how the physical phe-
nomenon spreads in space and superposing the regulation network of
actors
involved
in
resource
management
and
policy
design.
