Others = other countries).
1 To model the impact zones we have assumed that the compounds do not undergo
degradation once they have entered the stream. If one had selected compounds that de-
grade, the spatial extent of the impact zones would have shrank. This is because the
longer the distance travelled the longer the residence time in the stream network and
hence the time available for dissipation. However, many micropollutants are rather
persistent and have a long-range impact in streams. Persistence holds true for compounds
discharged from WWTPs (Barber et al., 2013) where easily degradable compounds have
been reduced already during treatments well as for important herbicides (Ruﬀet al.,
2015). In summary, these arguments suggest that the impact zones that we have derived
have a more general relevance than just for the four model compounds and that we can
use these impact zones to evaluate the degree to which a spatial misﬁt between the
physical aﬀectedness of the drinking water suppliers in Basel and in Düsseldorf and their
regulatory embeddedness exists.
K. Ingold et al.
Global Environm
ental Change 48 (2018) 136–150
146
Conceptually, we thus deﬁne a misﬁt as a situation in which the re-
levance of an area as part of an impact zone is not reﬂected through
corresponding coverage by the respective regulation network. Figs. 12
and 13 actually demonstrate such a misﬁt: on the one hand, the French
part of the (yellow) impact zones for WWD in Düsseldorf are not cov-
ered by the respective network of the drinking water supplier; whilst,
on the other hand, areas that hardly contribute to the micropollutant
load in Düsseldorf (dark blue areas) are represented in the network to
the same degree as the parts of the basin delivering 75% (red, yellow,
and green).
Results further show that there is a spatial misﬁt when comparing
the impact zones and load contributions of diﬀerent parts in the wa-
tershed of drinking water providers with the number of actors of their
network that exert inﬂuence in these parts (Fig. 10). The results also
demonstrate that national boundaries play an important role in these
misﬁts. This observation is not necessarily surprising but points to the
fact that the regulation networks we have analyzed have not been de-
veloped for handling transboundary issues of micropollutants.
The observation of a spatial misﬁt leads to the question of whether
this also implies a functional misﬁt. There is a large body of literature
about social-ecological or spatial misﬁt in environmental governance
emphasizing that eﬀective management of natural resources strongly
depends upon the congruence between physical and societal dynamics
(Borowski et al., 2008; Folke et al., 2007; Guerrero et al., 2015;
Kotzebue, 2012). Hence, network embeddedness of water suppliers is
considered to be essential for resource management or, as expressed by
Crona and Bodin 2006); see also Alexander et al., 2015), network re-
lations among actors seem to be a “pre-requisite” for eﬀective co-
management of natural resources.
We therefore assessed water suppliers’ contacts and relations to-
wards
public
authorities,
industries,
NGOs
and
scientiﬁc
re-
presentatives in their daily business of service delivery (management
network) and in the larger regulation network designing policies to ad-
dress water quality issues such as micropollutants.
Our results showed that water suppliers in Basel and Düsseldorf are
rather well embedded in the management and regulation networks but
that they lack direct links towards the diﬀerent polluters accounting for
the problem.
Looking at this result more qualitatively, one could acknowledge
that indirect links to polluters are suﬃcient and that what counts would
rather be the direct links to state oﬃcials and public authorities. The
latter can be held accountable for issues related to water quality con-
cerns; moreover, it is their duty to manage potential conﬂicts among
diﬀerent water uses and users. We can even go one step further and
assert that water quality issues in particular should not (only) happen
through self-steering by third parties (such as water suppliers), through
explorative forms of public participation (Bruch, 2004) or in the
shadow of hierarchy (Bolleyer and Börzel, 2010); it is rather regulation
by democratically legitimated bodies or public authorities that is
needed when an environmental problem is aﬀecting large parts of the
population.
The missing link between water suppliers and polluters might not
only be the result of a state-centred approach in drinking water man-
agement. It could further mean that the sources and causes of micro-
pollutants are not yet well known to all actors involved. Even though
actors might want to reach out towards polluters, they may not know
whom to address. Even if potential cooperation and coordination
partners are known, established conﬂict lines (also emerging from do-
mains other than micropolluant management) might challenge the
creation of communication or collaboration relations. The question
persists whether conﬂicts and management deﬁcits would decrease if
further links between suppliers and polluters were established. Future
research should focus on the relation between conﬂict lines and steering
forms (see also Alexander et al., 2015; Singleton and Taylor, 1992). It
should particularly answer the question if direct contacts between users
and consumers of the resource water increase or decrease conﬂict po-
tential.
We further conclude that water suppliers in Basel and Düsseldorf are
both rather well embedded and at least indirectly (via one intermediary
actor) connected to all organizations identiﬁed as relevant in the
