management and regulation networks. Furthermore, the here-in-
vestigated river basin and adjacent countries dispose of water quality
regulation and basin wide treaties, water frameworks and river com-
missions. This situation might be very diﬀerent in other countries and
parts of the world and further research is needed to account for cases
aﬀected by a bigger institutional deﬁcit and by more serious policy
implementation barriers than in the cases investigated here.
6. Conclusions
In this article, we followed up on the idea that the identiﬁcation of
spatial misﬁts matters because good ﬁt might be a necessary and per-
haps even a suﬃcient condition for appropriate natural resource man-
agement (Österblom and Bodin, 2012; Treml et al., 2015; Sayles and
Baggio, 2017). A truly interdisciplinary approach allowed us to describe
and demonstrate such spatial misﬁt in the context of managing mi-
cropollutants which are relevant for drinking water providers.
An important question emerging from this observation is whether
the reduction of such a misﬁt would lead to more eﬀective and ap-
propriate policies and ﬁnally even to better water quality. At this stage
we can conclude that water quality issues in the Rhine River basin
beneﬁt from a large ﬁt, i.e. an overlap of the physical extent of mi-
cropollutants and the jurisdictions managing and regulating this type of
pollution. However, this ﬁt is mainly due to national authorities ad-
dressing the issue, mostly through environmental quality norms and
technical standards (Metz, 2017). It could thus be that this ﬁt might not
directly lead to eﬀective regulation and desired water qualities. For
instance, interviewed public authorities integrated in the regulation
