K. Ingold et al.
Global Environm
ental Change 48 (2018) 136–150
148
network (see again Metz, 2017) mentioned respecting the competence
areas of upstream and downstream riparians: as soon as a resource
transcends a border the jurisdiction across the border responsible for its
management takes over. Such interview statements suggest that au-
thorities are conscious about potential misﬁts, but do not necessarily
regard them as problematic. Without problem awareness, public au-
thorities are unlikely to discuss and reduce potential adverse eﬀects of
misﬁts in terms of biophysical outcomes. The contribution of science
here is to pinpoint the existence of misﬁts and trigger a political dis-
cussion about potential consequences of misﬁts.
We conclude that investigating ﬁt and misﬁt should take diﬀerent
levels (local, regional, national and international) as well as scales
(points, jurisdictions, etc.) into account. Furthermore, more research is
needed about the link and interdependencies between spatial and
functional misﬁt and policies on one side (see Satake et al., 2008) and
misﬁt and outcomes (e.g. observed water quality) on the other. This
paper and research constitutes one ﬁrst step into this direction and gave
one concrete and tangible example of how to design such an inter-
disciplinary endeavor. It laid out how to conceptualize and oper-
ationalize the misﬁt between the biophysical and the governance sys-
tems in diﬀerent ways (i.e. relational, point- or area-related misﬁt). It
further showed the demand for spatial visualization when bringing
results from at least two disciplines together and thus paved the way for
further misﬁt modelling.
