While integration and participative practices are key principles in IWRM (Newig et al., 2005), the appropriate scale
for integration is not easily deﬁned. Geographic scales may be deﬁned by catchment, sub-catchment, political, jurisdic-
tional or other (e.g. biome) boundaries. While water managers might tend to reduce the ‘space for water’ to the histor-
ical separation of water from land, hydrologists and ecologists tend to rely on hydrological catchment areas, while spatial
planners refer to the opportunities for ‘new forms of spatiality’ (Wiering and Immink, 2006: 435). ‘Functional spaces’ is
a conceptual framework that can underpin a new type of political steering in favor of integration and sustainability
(Nahrath and Varone, 2009). The ‘action areas’ of water development and infrastructure are, at best, slowly adaptive
to other domains and functions (Moss, 2003; Holzinger, 2000; see also Wyborn, 2015, for a similar discussion in
the domain of habitat conservation); functional integration is difﬁcult to realize. Some iteration in the choice of appro-
priate geographic scale and corresponding inclusion of water uses (and users) is needed (Hering and Ingold, 2012).
Beyond deﬁnitions of scales and spaces, there are important challenges regarding the design of multi-level and
transboundary water regulation (Green et al., 2013; Huntjens et al., 2011; Dombrowsky, 2007; Conca, 2005). Mullin
(2009) argues that claims for integrated water management, stakeholder participation and local governance contributed
to the fragmentation of US water policy regulation. Environmental policy integration (EPI) within the same and across
different ﬁelds is not easily achieved (Nilsson and Persson, 2012). EPI requires important institutional changes, but often
these ‘are mainly restricted to the dimensions of ‘discourse’ and ‘rules of the game’ (Wiering and Immink, 2006, p. 435).
Broadening the scope of analysis and decision-making provides potential beneﬁts by expanding the range of pos-
sible solutions. It also increases the costs related to the demands for data-intensive descriptions of systems and the
involvement of more actors in policy design and implementation (Hering et al., 2012; de Gispert, 2004). Inclusive-
ness can increase problem-solving capacity and the legitimacy of management structures, but may also lead to inef-
ﬁcient decision-making and conﬂict expansion. Though IWRM may be an accepted concept, it is too early to assess
its effectiveness (Hughes and Pincetl, 2013). Others argue that some sectors (typically agriculture) can only be
‘integrated’ in water management and sustainable water policy design if (1) agendas coincide and (2) there is high
likelihood of a win–win situation or monetary beneﬁts (Brouwer et al., 2013; Kuks, 2004).
Given these debates and challenges, it is important to ask which sectors and which actors should be integrated,
and at what scale and to what extent.
Rationale and Overview of the Special Issue
This collection of articles takes stock of the development in thinking on ‘integration’, looks at existing collaborative
practices and addresses two major gaps in the literature. First, we answer the questions of how to deﬁne integration
and how to develop collaborative management. As Theesfeld and Schleyer (2013), Borowski and Pahl-Wostl (2008)
and Brown (1997) suggest, further progress in the design of environmental integration is necessary; and IWRM
should be translated from discourse into practice. The second gap relates transboundary water management and
the deﬁnition of scales. We continue with a reﬂection on new ideas of spatiality (Keskitalo and Pettersson, 2012;
Huntjens et al., 2011). Overall, we wish to show how policy analysis theory and methods tackle current issues of
integrated water management conceptually and empirically (cf. Lubell and Edelenbos, 2013).
This collection is organized in two parts, each including three contributions. The ﬁrst part focuses on collabora-
tive management and environmental policy integration (i.e. functional integration between different watershed
functions). The second part addresses questions about scales and borders and how to transcend them (i.e. institu-
tional integration between geographical, hydrological and jurisdictional scales).
Collaborative Approaches to Decision-Making and Implementations in Water Politics
Water policy and regulation goes beyond environmental protection, and should reﬂect the interactions between var-
ious resources and the link between the use and protection of water (Jonch-Clausen, 2004). IWRM asks for policy
processes and designs that respect the principles of horizontal and vertical integration, e.g. that actors representing
diverse sectors and different decisional and implementation levels are involved. Collaborative governance takes this
complexity into account by integrating various stakeholders. The policy analysis and governance literature has
