developed conceptual tools to study such collaborative modes of governance across multiple levels. Horizontal inte-
gration reallocates political authority sideways, from state to non-state actors, often through non-hierarchical and
negotiated exchanges between different actors (Bolleyer and Börzel, 2010). All sectors related to the water sector
should be taken into account. The concept of ecosystem services allows for a deﬁnition of some equivalent economic
value, even in the absence of markets (Brauman et al., 2007). Yet, the identiﬁcation and implementation of trade-
offs is likely to be contentious when other natural resources and sectors (e.g. energy, agriculture) are seen as equally
or more important than water resources (Hering and Ingold, 2012).
The Collaborative Approaches portion of the special issue contributes to state of the art and future developments
in water policy research, with a special focus on collaborative governance modes, the participation of local commu-
nities, the integration of different policy sectors and sustainable resource management (Table 1).
de Boer et al. (2015) review the literature on conceptual models and the practical application of collaborative gov-
ernance. They highlight different models that explain, understand or evaluate multi-stakeholder participation and
the quality of multi-stakeholder processes. The ‘Governance Assessment Tool’ is introduced to identify the strengths
and weaknesses of a speciﬁc water governance context. It is useful for designing or supporting collaborative water
management in practice. They illustrate the tool’s usefulness for understanding the impacts of governance on col-
laboration by applications in ﬁve different countries.
Balsiger and Ingold (2015) relate the procedural aspects of collaborative governance to sustainable development,
arguing that the knowledge of how sustainability is interpreted differently by local communities impacts upon the
further design of collaborative management schemes in ﬂood prevention (for a comparable study focusing on
decentralized irrigation management in Turkey, see Kadirbeyoglu and Özertan, 2015). They use a combination of
survey-data and social network analysis to assess actors’ sustainability perceptions and their relational
embeddedness in decision-making and implementation.
Metz and Fischer (2015) also rely on social network analysis to study policy diffusion and the spread of policy
instruments across institutionally integrated countries in the Rhine river basin. They analyze whether the Swiss pol-
icy on the management of micropollutants, a new and complex issue on the water quality protection agenda, has
spread to other countries integrated into the collaborative governance scheme. Based on a rich set of qualitative
and quantitative evidence, the authors conclude that both transnational networks and the speciﬁcities of the original
policy can inﬂuence policy diffusion.
All three papers rely on multiple cases to highlight similarities, differences and diffusion mechanisms across dif-
ferent countries and regions. Metz and Fischer (2015; and later also Bréthaut, 2015, and McNeill, 2015) acknowledge
that a traditional comparative research design might be a suboptimal approach when countries or districts are inte-
grated in one larger water body or catchment. Two of the three papers apply social network analysis. This method
seems to be particularly appropriate when identifying the structural patterns of collaborative governance and
resource dependences (Crona and Bodin, 2006). All of the papers demonstrate the importance of researcher
