Politics of Scale in Basins and Catchments
A major challenge of collaborative governance is deciding on the appropriate scale, by deﬁning regulation boundaries
and identifying catchment areas and river basin dimensions (Thiel, 2014; Wiering et al., 2010; Bernauer, 2002).
These boundaries are permeable and indistinct in a number of ways. Pollution of surface waters can be
transboundary in nature, as the physical, biological and chemical extent of pollution may transcend jurisdictional bor-
ders. Socio-political networks of governance and management may have a network character rather than be conﬁned
to geographical/hydrological space. Institutional integration across different scales and levels is difﬁcult to achieve,
and depends on other matters, for example, the decentralization of the political system (Lubell and Edelenbos,
2013). This second section contains three contributions on cross-boundary water management, with speciﬁc focus
on multi-level decision analysis, the identiﬁcation of functional spaces, the design of innovative water policy instru-
ments and the construction and re-construction of scales in water resource governance and management (Table 2).
One added value of Jager’s (2015) contribution lies in the systematic assessment of factors and mechanisms that
contribute to the success of European transboundary water basin management schemes. Through qualitative com-
parative analysis, Jager ﬁnds that the economic interdependence of riparians, water quality and legal issues impact
upon successful cooperation in transboundary water settings.
Transboundary elements of water management are also prominent in Bréthaut’s (2015) research. He further
shows that the application of participatory principles, in practice, immediately raises the question of who should
be integrated, formally or informally, and when and where. The investigation of the Rhone river basin demonstrates
the difﬁculties in intersectoral and transboundary water management when institutions are fragmented and water
basin authorities absent.
Questions of institutional legitimacy are also addressed by McNeill (2015), who draws lessons from New
Zealand’s experiences with IWRM. He highlights legitimacy questions that arose when rescaling water manage-
ment based on hydrological catchments. Typically, the public and national government are uncomfortable with
declining fresh water quality and the need to address indigenous people’s resource claims to some rivers have led
to a range of innovative, but ad hoc, governance experiments. These redeﬁne participation constellations at national
and regional government levels.
The second part of this special issue shows that single national case studies (McNeill, 2015), can show results
similar to those of comparative or transnational investigations (Jager, 2015; Bréthaut, 2015). Belonging to the same
hydrological catchment may favor the creation of joint initiatives towards water management (Metz and Fischer,
2015), but context and issue related speciﬁcities, and legal or procedural devices, can further explain the facilitation
or obstruction of cooperative management across sectors and scales. Finally, McNeill (2015) and de Boer et al. (2015)
develop recommendations (and assessment tools) that impact the broader acceptance and concrete realization of
governance modes for integrated water management.
