Empirical Challenges, Lessons and Solutions
This special issue shows that research questions related to water management, politics and governance can be
answered by various methods, qualitative or quantitative data analysis and single, multiple or comparative case study
designs. Yet, all six contributions come to the general conclusion that context, case and country speciﬁcities matter.
Policy analysis emphasizes the contribution of three distinct but interrelated dimensions: policy, deﬁned as con-
tent and the concrete answer to social or environmental problems; politics, embracing the procedural elements of
policymaking, and polity, describing institutional arrangement and set-up (see also Knill and Lenschow, 2000; Knill
and Tosun, 2012). Only a combination of these three elements in research designs gives a full picture of governance
modes in water management and their strengths and weaknesses, and challenges and shortcomings, when deﬁning
new scales to solve water related problems.
