"E D I T O R I A L
Transboundary environmental governance: Emerging themes
and lessons from Southeast Asia
1
|
INTRODUCTION
A transboundary approach to governing the environment has never
been more germane or important. The commodification of nature
within an increasingly global system has produced resource connec-
tions and flows between places that routinely generate unintended
consequences, such as transboundary air and water pollution and
extreme weather events linked to anthropogenic climate change. This
inability of individual jurisdictions, sectors and groups of resource
users to contain and effectively mitigate the cascading effects of
human interventions into nature prompts a critical rethinking of the
role of borders in partitioning, fragmenting and enclosing resources
for their conservation or (un)sustainable development (Dalby, 2020;
Miller, 2020; Wong Villanueva et al., 2020). Scholars and policy-
makers concerned with addressing these transboundary challenges
have paid close attention to the linkages between different scales of
border crossings to understand how policy choices can, and often do,
shape spatially and temporally distant socioecological outcomes
(Haarstad, 2014; Ingold et al., 2016; Wyborn & Bixler, 2013).
This special issue is concerned with the transboundary dimensions
of governing common pool resources and formulating collective
responses to cross-border environmental threats and crises. Our theo-
retical and conceptual focus is on transboundary environmental gover-
nance, understood to mean the full range of actors and institutions
involved in shaping and implementing decisions about the environment
across jurisdictions and property boundaries within, as well as between,
nation-states (Miller et al., 2020). From a governance perspective, this
definition is useful in directing attention toward the cross-border net-
works and power relations that determine how resources are differen-
tially valued over time, what ecological knowledge becomes integrated
into formal policy choices and who is included or excluded from envi-
ronmental collaborations among spatially dispersed actors and institu-
tions. In taking this multi-sectoral, multi-scalar and cross-border
approach, we build upon the contributions of two earlier Environmental
Policy and Governance special issues on polycentric governance (Heikkila
et al., 2018) and water management across borders, scales and sectors
(Ingold et al., 2016). Like these thematically allied collections, we
explore the varied forms that transboundary environmental governance
takes in (re)distributing environmental benefits and costs while mediat-
ing risks across scales and borders of overlapping resource interests.
Our approach contributes to knowledge of the transboundary scale
of environmental governance in at least four ways. First, this special
issue builds upon, but is not limited to, work that locates the trans-
boundary scale at the intersection between political boundaries and
natural resource systems (Milman et al., 2020), which tends to focus on
transboundary resources that circulate through jurisdictionally divided
ecosystems (Salmoral et al., 2019). Transboundary resources certainly
have materialities that inform their governance, but the focus of this
collection is on how human systems and processes shape cross-border
flows and socioecological outcomes. Environmental governance, as a
human enterprise, always occurs in political spaces. While transbound-
ary resources such as air, water, birds and migratory fish have properties
of non-stationarity (Wolkovich et al., 2014) that shape their cross-
border governance, humans demarcate and delimit these as bordered
spaces for environmental action, which are sites of continual negotia-
tion, contestation and compromise.
Second, this special issue contributes to critical analysis of the
multiple scales at which transboundary environmental governance is
enacted and operationalized. There is a misleading tendency in the
literature on transboundary environmental governance to conflate
the transboundary and transnational scales of analysis. In large part,
this has to do with the substantial number of transboundary agree-
ments that seek to integrate bilateral or regional economic interests
with environmental protections in marine, coastal, riparian and
inland
areas
by
neutralizing
sections
of
national
borders
(Jager, 2016; Warner & Marsden, 2016). Unlike transnational gover-
nance, however, which limits the study of cross-border human
resource relations to country-level units of analysis, transboundary
environmental governance regimes encompass human resource con-
nections that traverse borders at all scales of governance. In struc-
tural terms, transboundary environmental networks and agreements
are horizontally enacted across (sub)national jurisdictions and public,
private and communal property boundaries. Vertically, too, trans-
boundary governance regimes encompass an extraordinary hetero-
geneity of human resource connections that range from the
integration of place-based ecological knowledge into formal policy
regimes through to connections between agrarian communities and
sustainable supply chains, and collaborations between transbound-
ary environmental publics and (inter or sub)national regulatory
bodies.
