The third contribution of this special issue is empirical, extrapolat-
ing broad lessons for transboundary environmental governance that
derive from specific contexts in Southeast Asia. A uniquely complex
region, Southeast Asia covers just 4%–5% of the world's land mass
but supports 18% of its biodiversity and 9% of its human population,
or almost 680 million people (ASEAN Post, 2019; Woodruff, 2010;
Worldometer, 2021). These factors, combined with Southeast Asia's
historical pattern of trade regionalization, globalization and mixed-use
resource regimes around often legally ambiguous ownership struc-
tures, have produced multiple forms of interconnectedness and
hybrid partnerships based on state–societal, public–private and
co-governance arrangements (Miller et al., 2020).
A recurring theme throughout this special issue, however, is that
Southeast Asia is as deeply divided as it is economically and ecologically
connected. Bordering practices exacerbate existing resource inequalities
and create new imbalances in wealth, access to technologies, education
and public services. These power asymmetries, which assume specific
socio-spatial forms, pose ongoing barriers to active participation in
transboundary environmental stewardship. Whereas the most physi-
cally proximate users of natural resources tend to bear the cumulative
burden of transboundary development pressures, actors at higher scales
of governance frequently exploit power geometries to their advantage
(Armitage et al., 2015). In Southeast Asia, unequal power relations are
commonly associated with predatory resource extraction methods,
reduced regulatory standards and unsustainable development activities.
These power relations challenge transboundary environmental gover-
nance regimes and amplify negative environmental externalities, such as
greenhouse gas emissions linked to deforestation and air and water pol-
lution (Dasgupta et al., 2005; Simpson, 2017).
The fourth and final contribution of this special issue is to our
understanding
of
how
transboundary
environmental
governance
regimes shift over time according to changing geopolitical realities and
ruptures or crises in human-natural resource connections. The progres-
sion of the COVID-19 pandemic has fundamentally altered the nature
of transboundary environmental governance arrangements throughout
Southeast Asia. Governments across the region, seeking to recover
from the pandemic-induced recession, have selectively reopened
national borders to resume flows of trade and finance while rolling back
or scaling down key cross-border environmental agreements and pro-
grams, with important implications for environmental (in)justice, democ-
racy and activist networks (Miller et al., 2022). Pandemic-related border
disruptions have similarly impacted Southeast Asia's substantial popula-
tion of migrant workers, contributing to a region-wide pattern of return
urban–rural migration that has significant implications for the depletion
of finite natural resources in rural and agrarian communities across the
region (Suhardiman et al., 2021). The post-pandemic rush to return to
business as usual threatens, in the medium to longer term, to further
displace development pressures from water to land-based livelihoods,
adding to existing ruptures in human-water connections on an ever-
expanding scale, which will further realign the governance systems
required to cope with such social-ecological transformations (Miller
et al., 2021).
