divide the collection into two substantive parts. The first part explores
the practices and processes of participation in transboundary net-
works that underpin, legitimize and operationalize cross-border envi-
ronmental collaborations (Table 1). The second part problematizes
hybrid governance arrangements among diverse interest groups
across scales of jurisdictions and varying types of property borders
(Table 2).
2.1
|
Cross-border participation in collective
environmental stewardship
Stakeholder participation is an integral component of transboundary
environmental governance. Broad-based inclusion in decision-making
about complex environmental issues can improve problem-solving
capacities, facilitate the formulation of contextually relevant policy
choices and build political legitimacy around the perceived co-benefits
of transboundary agreements and cross-border programs. Notwith-
standing these clear advantages, greater participation has also been
negatively associated with more veto points, policy blockages and
compromises on strategic environmental objectives, causing opera-
tional delays and/or the partial implementation of coordinated actions
(Koontz & Newig, 2014). Moreover, many governments have been
slow to integrate inclusivity mechanisms into decision-making institu-
tions and to engage in cross-border activities that risk ceding control
over key budgets or forfeiting strategic partnerships linked to compet-
itive funding opportunities (Klinke, 2012).
Despite these limitations, the contributors to this collection sub-
scribe to the prevailing viewpoint in recent environmental scholarship
and among international development circles that collaborative, inclu-
sive and coordinated forms of environmental stewardship are not only
desirable, but necessary, for governing complex environmental issues
that exceed the capacities of individual groups and sectors at and
between
different
scales (Ghosh
&
Wolf, 2021;
Kochskämper
et al., 2018; Wong Villanueva et al., 2020; Wyborn & Bixler, 2013).
Our authors remain mindful, however, that the “participatory para-
digm” (Bäckstrand, 2003, p. 24), like sustainable development and
environmental democracy, has been applied to the full spectrum of
governance regimes, including to over-simplify or misrepresent more
variegated realities. For this reason, the papers in this collection ask
critical
questions
about
who
is
included
and
excluded
from
transboundary decision-making bodies, what cross-border programs
are given priority, who reaps the benefits of collective environmental
activities, and, to what extent does participation actually optimize
social-ecological outcomes?
In her critique of the role of participation, Yong's (2022) contribu-
tion to this collection examines the spatial–temporal politics involved in
negotiating, creating and contesting spaces for mobilizing transbound-
ary environmental publics. She argues that it is important to challenge
assumptions about the inherent virtue of public participation. Taking
the example of transboundary hydropower governance in the Mekong
River Basin, she illustrates the tendency among powerful state and pri-
vate sector actors to deploy claims to social inclusiveness as a means of
obtaining legitimacy for less-than-democratic decision-making pro-
cesses, alongside mundane state practices that limit opportunities for
participation. Drawing from semi-structured interviews and event eth-
nography conducted in Thailand and Cambodia, Yong makes her case
by comparing and contrasting two types of transboundary environmen-
tal publics in the Mekong that entail divergent visions of what public
participation means through varying interpretations of publics, place,
scale, and temporality.
Addressing questions of scale, Suhardiman and Geheb (2022)
argue that institutional disjuncture in hydropower decision-making
impede meaningful participation in transboundary water governance.
Taking the case of the Pak Beng hydropower dam project in Laos, the
authors show how institutional disjuncture constitutes a form of sca-
lar disconnect that limits the ability of grassroots stakeholders to artic-
ulate their needs and aspirations for sustainable development through
formal channels. Unless efforts are made to overcome such institu-
tional disjuncture—that results from unequal power relations between
national government and private sector actors on the one hand, and
riparian communities and local governments on the other hand—then
the environmental costs of restricted hydropower decision-making
are likely to continue to accumulate to the detriment of the latter.
Marks and Miller (2022) are similarly concerned with scalar discon-
nects in addressing the transboundary problem of air pollution that orig-
inates inside Thailand and extends across its national borders. Using a
transboundary political ecology framework, the authors direct attention
toward the role of borders and bordering practices in creating barriers
to cross-border cooperation while driving flows of environmental
impacts. They argue that Thailand's incomplete decentralization, admin-
istrative fragmentation and prioritization of economic growth at the
