EDITORIAL
277
 17569338, 2022, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/eet.2012 by Cochrane Japan, Wiley Online Library on [18/02/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
expense of meaningful environmental sustainability measures have cre-
ated numerous blockages to transboundary governance of the air. To
provide redress for these problems, they propose legislative reforms to
open up opportunities for collaborative activities and to hold the worst
drivers of air pollution to account for their cross-border transgressions.
The degree of participation at different scales of transboundary
environmental governance regimes is influenced by whose ecological
knowledge is prioritized in policy choices and legislation. Tran and
Tortjada (2022) take the policy-practice interface as their entry point
for examining upstream-downstream disconnects in determining the
knowledge that is included in, and excluded from, policy documents
and decision-making processes in the Vietnamese Mekong Delta.
They show how the lack of ecological knowledge about the trans-
boundary impacts of hydropower dam projects in policy documenta-
tion not only compromises the ability of riverine communities to
adapt their own livelihoods to these hydrosocial transformations, but
also the capacity of the Vietnamese government to effectively plan
for inclusive forms of sustainable development and coordinate
resilience-building strategies at the national and regional scales.
2.2
|
Hybrid forms of transboundary
environmental governance
The bulk of recent governance research has been cautiously optimistic
about the innovative promise of hybrid models to address complex
environmental issues that cross state-market-societal divisions and
related state, private and communal property boundaries (Cecilia
et al., 2016; Ghosh & Wolf, 2021; Mert, 2014). In Southeast Asia
alone, a diversity of public-private, private-societal and co-governance
partnerships have reorganized the priorities and programs of environ-
mental governance regimes across the region. These partnerships
tend to be rooted in logics of market environmentalism and various
forms of green growth premised on the attainability of sustainable
development. In their most positive iteration, hybrid partnerships fill
gaps in formal governance regimes by reforming land use practices,
addressing regulatory gaps and promoting fairer systems of resource
access and distribution (Lambin et al., 2014). More negatively, how-
ever, power asymmetries can, and often do, undermine the collabora-
tive potential of hybrid governance in transboundary resource
regimes, such as when state or corporate actors conceal the opportu-
nities for capital accumulation in converting devalued or degraded
resources to the disadvantage of local community partners (Knuth
et al., 2019; Miller, 2022).
Varkkey (2022) explores how transboundary environmental pub-
lics can bridge gaps in formal governance regimes to deal with epi-
sodic and seasonal air pollution, known legally and colloquially in
Southeast Asia as “haze.” She deploys the concept of transboundary
environmental publics to examine the role played by three types of
non-state actors in influencing official policy directives aimed at miti-
gating haze pollution. Taking the case of the city-state of Singapore,
Varkkey shows how the active involvement of non-state actors in
supporting
official
haze
reduction
and
prevention
strategies
constitutes a form of “engaged non-indifference” that is emblematic
of the geopolitical culture of ASEAN (Association of Southeast Asian
Nations). As a microcosm of this regional culture, the Singapore expe-
rience thus contains important lessons about how transboundary
environmental publics might be productively mobilized and scaled up
to respond to cross-border environmental threats and crises in the
future.
Flows of natural resources, finances, technologies, people and
ecological knowledge across administrative borders inform hybrid
arrangements of transboundary environmental governance. Lamb and
Fung (2022) use a mobile political ecology framework to analyze flows
and fixes of transboundary sand commons along the Salween River at
the Myanmar-Thailand extraction frontier. Their study provides
important insights into two parallel and partly interconnected sets of
flows that generate transboundary governance regimes: (a) the sand
trade; and (b) out-migration corridors that hollow out villages along-
side, and in response to, the adverse environmental impacts of sand
extraction. The authors highlight the centrality of these flows to
understanding shifts in place-based practices in addition to the bor-
dering practices that inform transboundary resource organization
among dispersed groups of actors at the basin scale.
Using a networked approach to understanding the hybrid nature
of transboundary environmental cooperation, Cole's (2022) contribu-
tion to this collection explores the marginal role of smallholders in
agri-food supply chains. Drawing from the experience of upland
farmers in Laos, he examines what the rapid shift from subsistence to
commercial farming means for the transboundary governance of envi-
ronmental impacts linked to intensive modes of production. In asking
who ultimately bears responsibility for sustainability, he considers
how agribusinesses with headquarters in distant jurisdictions external-
ize land degradation impacts linked to debt cycles and unsustainable
land use change.
3
|
LESSONS AND LEARNING
OPPORTUNITIES
Taken together, the papers in this special issue emphasize the impor-
tance of developing more coordinated and collaborative approaches
to enhance the efficacy of transboundary environmental governance
arrangements. The diversity of transboundary contexts presented
here shows that while there is no one-size-fits-all model of best prac-
tice in cross-border environmental stewardship, a generalizable set of
interconnected lessons can be discerned to provide a foundation for
future policy research. These lessons, outlined below, are broadly
threefold.
First, power relations need to be taken into better account at dif-
ferent organizational scales of transboundary environmental gover-
nance. Power asymmetries inform ecological knowledge, policy choices
and who is included in activities that produce socially and spatially dif-
ferentiated outcomes. Greater attentiveness to bridging these inequal-
ities at the planning stage of cross-border environmental collaborations
could help to build more participatory mechanisms into the institutional
278
EDITORIAL
 17569338, 2022, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/eet.2012 by Cochrane Japan, Wiley Online Library on [18/02/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
design of transboundary agreements. For example, the creation of for-
mal channels to allow grassroots actors to articulate their aspirations
and provide critical feedback at different stages of targeted programs
could safeguard place-based knowledge and improve the regulation of
cross-border resource-sharing arrangements.
Second, and relatedly, hybrid forms of transboundary governance
that are prone to elite capture need to remain internally coherent
about the particular environmental good that brings people together
and to defend this collective interest against private individual inter-
ests. While organizational objectives may shift over time in response
to changing conditions and circumstances, institutional cohesiveness
is necessary to avoid scalar disconnects that can lead spatially dis-
persed actors to work at cross-purposes or implement programs in a
partial or piecemeal way. This risk of scalar disconnect and institu-
tional disjuncture is heightened in transboundary governance regimes
where the social, spatial and/or temporal distance is greater between
decision-makers and sites of policy implementation.
Finally, this collection contains valuable lessons about navigating
the governance of cross-border flows of natural resources, forms of
environmental harm, technologies, knowledge and capital. Each of the
transboundary contexts described in this special issue has applied pol-
icy relevance to specific peoples who negotiate cross-border spaces
and issues in Southeast Asia. By directing attention toward borders
and bordering practices as sites where environmental governance is
continually enacted and contested, our contributors individually and
collectively identify opportunities for sustaining vital linkages between
socially dispersed and ecologically connected systems.
