Trust and commitment among 
stakeholders 
Collaboration with parties directly affected by proposed actions is 
likely to be effective when natural resource managers perceive that 
the legitimacy of their organization is low. 
Collaboration with well-reputed actors is likely to happen when 
natural resource managers perceive a low legitimacy of their 
organization. 
The state collaborated with affected communities, action groups and 
non-governmental organizations to transform adversarial 
relationships. 
The Melamchi Water Supply project sought an opportunity to 
collaborate with non-governmental organizations to placate the 
protests. 
Miscommunication, controversy 
and conflict 
Collaborative governance is likely to be more effective when the 
perceived costs of controversy, contentions and litigations are higher 
than the costs involved in a collaboration. 
Natural resource managers are more likely to collaborate when they 
are worried about the loss of power and influence rather than joint 
gains. 
