1. Introduction
Ecological networks represent a conceptual approach for
increasing the connectivity of habitats, plant and animal popula-
tions and their communities. It is often discussed as a key strategy
for halting the global loss of biodiversity (EU-COM, 2006). The
notion of ecological networks is becoming ever more salient against
the backdrop of climate change, which forces many species to shift
their range towards the poles or to higher altitudes in order to
survive. Ecological networks can facilitate the type of large-scale
migration and dispersal which especially the more sensitive species
will have to perform in reaction to global warming (EU-COM, 2009,
p. 4; Hansen et al., 2003, p. 11; Kirby, 2003, p. 1; Opdam and
Wascher, 2004, p. 286; Warren et al., 2001, p. 68).
The deﬁnition of ‘ecological network’ in conservation biology
is straightforward: ‘a set of ecosystems of one type, linked into a
spatially coherent system through ﬂows of organisms, and inter-
