acting with the landscape matrix in which it is embedded’ (Opdam
et al., 2006, p. 324). In the most basic understanding, an ecological
network consists of core areas large enough to host a signiﬁcant
number of individuals of one or several species. The core areas are
surrounded by buffer zones and interlinked by linear corridors or
sporadic stepping stone areas (Bennett and Mulongoy, 2006, p. 4
f.; Jones-Walters, 2007, p. 263). Bennett (2008, p. 5) proposes to
surround these elements of an ecological network by sustainable
use areas, “where opportunities are exploited within the landscape
mosaic for the sustainable use of natural resources together with
the maintenance of most ecosystem services”. Many authors high-
light the need to adopt an integrative perspective on ecological
networks and the matrix (e.g., Schmiegelow, 2007, p. 259 f.). From
this point of view, ecological networks “may be regarded as a large-
scaled spatial structure required for the long-term conservation of
natural resources in multifunctional landscapes” (Opdam, 2006, p.
56).
A distinction is usually drawn between:
• structural connectivity or landscape connectedness, which is
determined by the spatial arrangement of different types of habi-
tats in the landscape, and
