• functional connectivity or habitat connectivity, which is con-
cerned with the behavioural response of individuals and species
to the physical structure of the landscape (Bennett, 2003, p. 9) (cf.
also Anderson and Jenkins, 2006, p. 2 f.; Jongman et al., 2004, p.
308 f.; Lindenmayer and Fischer, 2006, p. 121; Parker et al., 2008,
p. 47 f.).
In the frame of a series of research projects sponsored by the Ger-
man federal agency for nature conservation a method for designing
a national ecological network has been elaborated (Hänel, 2007,
p. XIII; cf. also Reck et al., 2008). This approach tries in a way to
integrate structural and functional aspects. Although it is primarily
based on GIS technology and on algorithms for analysing spatial
structures, it considers also the habitat needs of selected target
species. Due to limited ﬁeld data it can seldom be said whether
a particular stretch of land actually serves as habitat of a target
species, but the entire German territory was analysed in terms of
its suitability as potential habitat. In a second step it was analysed
how core areas in the sense of potential habitats of target species
can be most easily connected, given the current land-use patterns.
Of late there has been some debate in Europe about creating
a ‘green infrastructure’—a term which is mostly synonymous to
‘ecological network’. It is linked to an ongoing project that has
been commissioned by the European Commission. Some major
non-governmental organisations have also subscribed to the idea
of such a European ‘green infrastructure’ (cf. e.g., Hontelez and De
Pous, 2008).
Many international policy papers (e.g., EU-COM, 2006, p. 2) and
academic publications (e.g., Bonnin et al., 2007; Brunner, 2006;
Jongman et al., 2004, p. 317; Van den Belt, 2004, p. 324; Von Haaren
and Reich, 2006, p. 20) have called for better transboundary coor-
dination and management of ecological networks. Political and
administrative boundaries rarely reﬂect ecological connections and
systems. The effectiveness of ecological networks on a smaller scale
can therefore only be ensured if they transcend such boundaries.
Kettunen et al. (2007, p. 94) made a plea to integrate connectiv-
ity issues ‘into the mandate of existing cross-border/transnational
governance bodies’ or to design new mechanisms.
Transboundary cooperation in establishing ecological networks
is particularly important for a country like Germany located in the
centre of Europe and surrounded by a total of nine neighbouring
states. There are many transboundary bioregions and ecosystems
such as river basins, mountain ranges and wetlands. An expert
group under the auspices of the German federal agency for nature
conservation has identiﬁed 94 ‘international linkage sites of ecolog-
ical corridors’ along Germany’s external border (Finck et al., 2005,
p. 365).
The European Union and many European countries have
adopted legislation and policies on establishing ecological net-
works. A case in point is the European Habitats Directive in
combination with the European Birds Directive, which require
Member States to set up ‘a coherent European ecological network
of special areas of conservation [. . .] under the title Natura 2000’
(Habitats Directive, article 3 [1]), in which context ‘coherence’ is
not to be confused with ‘connectivity of sites’ (cf. Kettunen et al.,
2007, p. 15). Another example is the German Federal Nature Con-
servation Act, which stipulates in article 3 that the states of the
federation have to establish ‘a network of connected habitats (eco-
logical network) that covers at least 10% of the respective state
territory’.
None of these regulations include legally binding provisions on
the cross-border coordination of ecological networks. This means
that such cooperation is entirely voluntary. Nevertheless, some bi-
or multinational organisations for the coordination of ecological
networks in Central Europe do exist. For instance, Germany partic-
ipates in interministerial working groups for nature conservation
with several neighbouring countries such as Poland and the Czech
Republic. Government ofﬁcials and NGO representatives from Ger-
many, the Netherlands, Belgium, and Luxembourg also participate
in the annual ‘Kleve Meeting,’ ﬁrst held in 1999, and which has
evolved into an important informal communication platform on
transboundary nature conservation issues.
Some scholars have attempted to map either transboundary
cooperation of protected area administrations (e.g., Zbicz, 2003)
or ecological network projects around the world (e.g., Bennett
and Mulongoy, 2006). Neither type of study includes systematic
accounts of transboundary cooperation in establishing ecological
networks. Furthermore there are numerous case studies on individ-
ual cross-border conservation collaboratives (e.g., Chester, 2006;
Fall, 2005; King and Wilcox, 2008; Sierra, 2006; Smith et al., 2008;
Sneddon, 2006). However, little is known about the kind of trans-
boundary cooperation currently going on in establishing ecological
networks across European borders. The same applies to the factors
that facilitate or hamper such cooperation efforts.
The objective of the present research is threefold. First and fore-
most there is a descriptive interest in gaining an empirical overview
of transboundary cooperation in establishing ecological networks
on Germany’s external borders. The second issue is exploratory and
explanatory in nature, addressing the reasons why such coopera-
tion is launched and why some border regions seem to be more
active in this than others. Thirdly, from a prescriptive point of view,
shortfalls in current practice and potential remedies were analysed.
The article is structured as follows. In the ﬁrst two sections we
very brieﬂy summarize our theoretic framework and methodology.
Then we present our empirical ﬁndings before going on to dis-
cuss practical recommendations and possible directions for further
research.
2. Theoretic framework
Research can draw on the large body of literature on cross-
border cooperation in general and on theories of actor networks,
interorganisational coordination, and governance (for a recent
overview, cf. Klijn, 2008). Our theoretic framework consists of a
set of hypotheses grouped into ﬁve categories:
• the institutional context,
• structural and situational contexts,
• actors with their resources, orientations and interests,
• actor constellation, and
• ideas, symbols and discourses.
This classiﬁcation drives from broader, more generic frame-
works such as actor-centred institutionalism (Scharpf, 1997) and
the concept of policy arrangements (Van Tatenhove et al., 2000). A
brief overview of major issues follows.
Institutions are understood as ‘relatively stable collections of
communicative practices and rules deﬁning appropriate behaviour
for speciﬁc groups of actors in speciﬁc situations’ (Risse, 2002, p.
604). They comprise ‘not only formal legal rules that are sanctioned
by the court system and machinery of the state, but also social
norms that actors will generally respect and whose violation will
be sanctioned by loss of reputation, social disapproval, withdrawal
of cooperation and rewards’ (Scharpf, 1997, p. 38). A very strong
institutional factor in favour of cross-border cooperation is what
Thomas (Thomas, 2003, p. 258–261) called ‘the regulatory hammer’
with respect to the provisions of the US Endangered Species Act that
obliges conservation authorities to cooperate across the boundaries
of their individual jurisdictions. Otherwise they risk NGO litigation.
The transaction costs of cross-border cooperation are generally
higher than those of domestic cooperation owing to differences in
86
M. Leibenath et al. / Landscape and Urban Planning 94 (2010) 84–93
cultural and administrative systems. Transaction costs are lowered
and cross-border cooperation accordingly facilitated to the extend
that: (a) the languages, legal systems and administrative structures
of the participating countries are similar, (b) the actors know each
other, have experience in cross-border cooperation—regardless of
thematic ﬁelds—and trust each other (cf. Deppisch, 2008; Head,
2008, p. 740), (c) overall intergovernmental relations are good,
and (d) the network of existing transboundary institutions is well
developed (Leibenath and Knippschild, 2007, p. 143). Existing insti-
tutions shape the course of action and limit the scope of possible
outcomes, though not in a deterministic sense (Blatter, 2001, p.
100; Scharpf, 1997, p. 39–42). Many authors have pointed out that
in Europe both cross-border cooperation and the implementation
of ecological networks are much more strongly dominated by the
public sector and rely more heavily on external ﬁnancial incentives
than, for instance, in the US (Bennett and Mulongoy, 2006, pp. 28
and 44; Blatter, 2004, p. 541 f.). Hence the availability of exter-
nal funding schemes is another enabling aspect of the institutional
context.
Structural and situational contexts are non-institutional factors
that can hardly be inﬂuenced by local actors. Aspects of the
structural context are, for example, the geographic size of the coop-
eration area, the quality of transport systems and communication
infrastructures, and the level of economic development. The latter
affects the deployment of advanced information technologies and
consequently the availability of data. Situational contexts can be
shaped by disasters such as natural or technical hazards, by the
election of a new government, or by the sudden appearance or
reappearance of large carnivores in a region. Transboundary biore-
gions with strong ecological ties across borders and the prevalence
of ﬂagship species are also part of the structural context. There is
some debate about the importance of such functional interdepen-
dencies in explaining cross-border cooperation: from an ecological
perspective it seems clear that transboundary ecological ties are
the lynchpin of cross-border cooperation in establishing ecological
networks. Many organisation scientists also maintained that func-
tional interdependencies have a crucial inﬂuence on the level and
intensity of coordination (Chisholm, 1989, pp. 56 and 58; Thomas,
2003, pp. 3 and 24). By contrast, Blatter (2004, p. 531) held that
cross-border cooperation can seldom be explained as a mere reac-
tion to functional necessities. Instead, successful environmental
cross-border cooperation depends on the tasks of the agencies
involved and on the perceptions and priorities of relevant actors.
Actors as the subjects of transboundary cooperation are by def-
inition of crucial importance. They may or may not dispose of
personal characteristics conducive to cooperation, such as language
skills and intercultural competence or resources in terms of time,
knowledge, money or authority (Skelcher and Sullivan, 2008, p.
758). The position or role of actors seems to be crucial in explaining
whether a cooperation initiative develops at all and the direction
it takes. With regard to nature conservation, Thomas (2003, pp.
27–48) distinguished between line managers, professional staff,
and ﬁeld staff. This corresponds in part with Fürst’s (2000, p.
76) distinction between generalists, i.e., elected politicians, and
expert ofﬁcers. Line managers and generalists value transaction
costs relatively highly and are primarily interested in safeguarding
the stability and autonomy of their agency. Conversely, profes-
sional staff and expert ofﬁcers are chieﬂy interested in ﬁnding
satisfying technical solutions according to the standards of their
discipline. For instance, ecologists who work as professional staff
in a conservation authority have a ‘natural’ interest in transbound-
ary cooperation if ecosystems or the populations of endangered
species span social boundaries. Furthermore they seek autonomy
as a group.
This leads directly to the fourth category—‘actor constellation’.
Experts talking the same technical language and sharing the same
knowledge, but working in different contexts such as government
agencies, consultancies and NGOs can form powerful networks.
They can drive transboundary cooperation in establishing ecologi-
cal networks. Haas (1992, p. 3) spoke of ‘epistemic communities’
in this regard and deﬁnes them as ‘network[s] of professionals
with recognized expertise and competence in a particular domain
and an authoritative claim to policy-relevant knowledge within
that domain or issue-area’. The more decision makers depend
on speciﬁc information provided and interpreted by experts who
do not necessarily know one another, the more easily epistemic
communities can develop and exert power through their con-
trol of knowledge and information. Sabatier’s concept of advocacy
coalitions shares some features with that of epistemic communi-
ties, but places greater emphasis on the normative, value-based
aspects of actor networks and on ‘interest group leaders, [. . .]
agency ofﬁcials, legislators from multiple levels of government,
applied researchers, and perhaps even a few journalists’ (Sabatier,
1998, p. 103). The actor constellation in combination with prob-
lem structure determines the distribution of the costs and beneﬁts.
Cooperation is most likely to occur: (a) if total beneﬁts out-
weigh total costs, and (b) if costs and beneﬁts are distributed
evenly or if at least every participant’s beneﬁts are higher than
his costs. If the second condition is not met in the ﬁrst instance,
the partners can agree side payments or tie two problems in
order to achieve symmetry (Chisholm, 1989, pp. 147–149). For the
issue of transboundary cooperation in establishing ecological net-
works, immediate transaction costs are usually comparatively high
whereas monetary beneﬁts tend to be longer term, if they occur
at all. This underscores the importance of ideas and non-material
values.
Ideas, symbols and discourses deﬁne the ﬁfth category of
hypotheses. Many scholars of international relations highlight the
importance of the ideational dimension of cooperation (e.g., Alper,
2004, p. 80). Transboundary cooperation in establishing ecological
networks is facilitated by an attractive vision or concept with which
actors can identify. In the initial phase of cooperation, it is crucial to
frame the issue to tie in with overarching discourses ‘en vogue’, e.g.,
the sustainability discourse in the 1990s or the climate change dis-
course in recent years. Often the speciﬁc leitmotifs of cross-border
cooperation reﬂect discourses in other regions or at other levels
of policy making (Leibenath, 2007; Skelcher and Sullivan, 2008, p.
763). Commitment and identiﬁcation are also spurred by the use
of appealing names, images and symbols. For instance, describing
a situation by using the phrases ‘ecological network’ in conjunc-
tion with and in opposition to ‘landscape fragmentation’ almost
automatically implies a leap from description to prescription, i.e.,
to a plead for fragmentation to be remedied by integrating isolated
elements into a network (Hajer and Versteeg, 2005, p. 344; Stone,
1988, p. 118). Applying a name such as ‘Yellowstone to Yukon’
or ‘Green Belt along the Former Iron Curtain’ (or ‘European Green
Belt’) to an ecological network initiative ‘may be seen as a way of
reconnecting to a pre-modern, natural state of being and mean-
ing, undivided by nation-state imposed borders’ (Lorton Levesque,
2001, p. 128). Effective transboundary advocacy groups are usually
built not on abstract values, but on ‘shared discourses centred on
nature’ and ‘a sense of community and identity based on the idea
of place’ (Lorton Levesque, 2001, p. 134).
As a matter of fact, our set of hypotheses is not—and cannot
be—all-encompassing in that it does not include all potentially
relevant factors. What is more, the hypotheses are introduced
only brieﬂy and need further elaboration and clariﬁcation. They
are intended to guide empirical analysis. The theoretic framework
structures existing knowledge and gives some exploratory indica-
tions of what to expect in the ﬁeld. It is also the basis for eventually
identifying hypotheses with the highest explanatory value worth
testing in further in-depth studies.
M. Leibenath et al. / Landscape and Urban Planning 94 (2010) 84–93
87
3. Methodology
3.1. Our empirical inquiry rests on three pillars
• First a literature review on biological theories of ecological net-
works as well as on existing ecological network concepts and
planning strategies at the European and national (German) levels,
• second a comprehensive internet survey in combination with
exploratory expert interviews,
• third a series of semi-structured, open-ended, in-depth inter-
views.
The exploratory expert interviews and internet survey were
intended to provide a comprehensive overview of transbound-
ary cooperation projects in establishing ecological networks across
Germany’s external borders. The selection criteria for projects were
that they had to be operating within the period 2003–2007, they
had to be transboundary in nature in terms of partner structure
or project area, they had to be targeted on establishing functional
and spatial linkages in the sense of ecological networks, and project
titles or outlines had to make explicit reference to ecological net-
works or at least to broader aspects of nature conservation. Nota
bene: the focus was on projects, i.e., on activities, rather than
on organisations or territories. Hence the mere existence of large
protected areas or of transboundary committees for nature conser-
vation was not considered as relevant.
An internet survey allows quick access to a broad range of infor-
mation, though it carries the risk of systematic errors, e.g., because
relevant projects may not appear on any website or are not detected
by the search methods used. To make up for this risk, 26 relatively
short exploratory telephone interviews were held. The intervie-
wees included representatives:
• of state agencies for nature conservation from all ten German
states with an international border,
• of the German federal agency for nature conservation,
• from the national headquarters of all major environmental NGOs
in Germany.
In the case of some states additional interviews were held with
state government ofﬁcials and local conservation agencies (in Ger-
man: Naturschutzstationen). Interviewees were asked about forms
of cooperation and activities that met the above criteria.
The preliminary interviews were complemented by an exten-
sive internet survey. We searched the project databases of all
INTERREG III A and III B programmes in which Germany partici-
pated, of the EU programme LIFE Nature and of German Federal
Environmental Foundation (DBU), the largest funding facility for
environmental projects in the country. Furthermore we scrutinized
the websites of international environmental organisations such
as the International Commission for the Protection of the Rhine
(ICPR), of transboundary organisations such as the International
Conference of Lake Constance and of all large protected areas along
Germany’s external border.
For each potentially relevant project a standardized proﬁle was
elaborated in which the projects were described with regard to
duration, area, speciﬁc objectives, partners, institutionalisation and
organisation, funding sources, results, and contact persons.
From the total number of relevant projects, 10 were selected
for in-depth interviews. They were chosen to encompass differ-
ences in terms of participating countries, size of the cooperation
area, number and type of participating actors, and speciﬁc project
objectives. The in-depth interviews were also held by telephone,
based on a guideline with relevant thematic issues underpinned by
a set of questions. The issues and questions were derived from the
theoretic framework and covered all ﬁve categories of hypotheses.
Interviewees were informed about the thematic issues in advance.
The interviews were semi-structured, which means that while
the issues addressed were constant, the order of questions was
ﬂexibly adapted, depending on the course of the interview. In
some cases additional and more detailed questions were added
and in other cases some questions had to be omitted when they
proved irrelevant for the speciﬁc case or the interviewee would
clearly be unable to answer them. On average, interviews lasted
45 min. They were not taperecorded, but detailed minutes were
kept by an assistant who also ensured that all issues in the inter-
view guideline where addressed when the order of questions was
altered.
We adopted a German perspective, since our focus was on Ger-
many’s external borders and the experts interviewed were almost
exclusively from Germany. This restricted approach is due to lim-
ited research capacities, although potential biases—for example
with respect to the interpretation of transboundary cooperation
restrictions as expressed by interview partners—would have prob-
ably been mitigated by cooperation with research partners from
other countries.
4. Individual projects and regional clusters
What was there in the way of transboundary cooperation in
establishing ecological networks between 2003 and 2007 at Ger-
many’s external borders? The object of analysis proved difﬁcult to
capture empirically and to separate from other forms of coopera-
tion because the term ‘ecological network’ is applied ubiquitously
and has often become practically synonymous with ‘nature con-
servation’. The distinction between relevant and irrelevant cases
in terms of research design was hence not always easy to make.
Therefore, although we spent considerable effort on identifying
relevant projects, we may have overlooked or wrongly classiﬁed
others.
However, strict application of our criteria (cf. chapter ‘method-
ology) enabled us to identify 34 projects, 4 of which were
stand-alone projects. The other 30 could be reasonably grouped
into 7 regional clusters (see Table 1 and Fig. 1). Projects were assem-
bled into a cluster if they focused on the same territory or on
immediately adjacent areas, if they displayed signiﬁcant overlap
in actor constellation, or if verbal relations to one or more other
projects were established in an ofﬁcial project outline.
The list of projects (see Table 1) shows striking heterogene-
ity in the natural systems concerned and the funding sources.
Closer scrutiny reveals certain groupings: more cases are centred
exclusively or at least chieﬂy on structural connectivity than on
functional connectivity. The majority of projects involve the imple-
mentation of practical measures rather than conceptual work. Very
few address large transnational project areas and again only few
appear to be integrated into broader strategies for ecological net-
works. In fact, most projects are relatively limited in geographic
scope and would form only small regional clusters, if other crite-
ria beyond territorial location were considered. Many projects are
probably small because they have been launched in a bottom-up
fashion by actors with local constituencies and limited organisa-
tional capacities. The larger a project area is and the higher the
number of actors is, the more professional the organisational struc-
ture must be.
There is an imbalance between Germany’s western border to the
Netherlands, Belgium, Luxembourg, France and Switzerland and
the eastern border to Poland and the Czech Republic: 21 projects
at the western border compared with only 8 projects at the east-
ern border. The few cases at Germany’s eastern border also have
more characteristics in common than projects at the western bor-
der. They mainly involve conservation authorities and NGOs, but
