hardly any cities and communities or other actors from civil soci-
ety, and most are concerned with functional connectivity, based on
target species such as the aquatic warbler (Acrocephalus paludicola)
or the freshwater pearl mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera).
Although not all regional clusters could be examined in detail, it
became apparent from the interviews and additional print material
that many regional clusters are based on common discourses and
varying degrees of institutionalisation. Some clusters are not insti-
tutionalized at all and others only to the extent that the main actors
know each other and meet regularly for regional workshops, con-
ferences, etc. The latter applies to the cluster ‘Benelux-Germany’
whose main actors gather annually for the ‘Kleve Meeting’. The
cluster ‘Green Belt along the former Iron Curtain’ is more formalized
because there are bi-annual conferences, a multiannual programme
of work dating from 2004 and an organisational structure building
on regional coordinators as well as on national focal points in nearly
every country involved (cf. Riecken et al., 2006, p. 9).
Formalized conventions or international commissions represent
the strongest type of institutionalisation:
• The ‘Alpine Network’ cluster is based on the Alpine Convention—
an international treaty with an underlying organisational struc-
ture including a permanent secretariat and several committees,
• the ‘Migrating Fish in the Rhine’ cluster is tied closely to the ICPR,
and
• the cluster ‘Czech-Saxon-Bavarian border triangle’ has evolved in
the context of the Czech-German commission for transboundary
waters which dates back to the 1970s.
The latter example shows that the degree of institutionalisation
does not correlate with the size of the cooperation area. It is impor-
tant to note that all these formalized international institutions were
not designed in response to a perceived need for ecological net-
works. They were established for other reasons such as hazard
prevention (pollution and ﬂoods), adopting the issue of ecologi-
cal networks at a later date. Where ecological networks were the
thematic point of departures, only loosely formalized institutions
like the Kleve Meeting or the Green Belt initiative emerged.
