5. International institutions and strong NGOs as the main
enabling factors
Many factors that had been derived theoretically were borne
out by the interviews, e.g., relaxed intergovernmental relations,
legal requirements, long-standing personal contacts, skills in cross-
cultural communication including language proﬁciency, personal
leadership or the absence of substantial conﬂicts. Transboundary
ecological interdependencies and the availability of external fund-
ing were also scrutinized as expected potentially strong drivers
of transboundary ecological networking initiatives. However,
transboundary ecological interdependencies or the availability of
external funding cannot fully explain why there are so many more
transboundary cooperation projects in establishing ecological net-
works at Germany’s western border as opposed to the few at the
eastern border: potential international linkage sites of ecologi-
cal corridors are distributed more or less evenly along Germany’s
external borders (Finck et al., 2005, p. 365). Similarly, funding
schemes such as INTERREG, LIFE or DBU are equally available every-
where along the border, even if we keep in mind that the public
ﬁnancial situation in East Germany is worse than in the western
part of the country, making it more difﬁcult to secure the co-funding
that is usually a prerequisite for project grants.
Finally, two groups of hypotheses seem to be of speciﬁc explana-
tory value. The ﬁrst concerns the inﬂuence of NGOs and the second
international institutions and organisations.
The interviews showed that NGOs are the driving forces
behind many transboundary ecological network projects. Some-
times they directly play a leading role in the cooperation as does
the BUND—the German branch of Friends of the Earth—in the Green
Belt initiative (cluster I). In other cases it is NGOs that incite author-
ities to act as in the case of the Alpine Network (cluster III). And
sometimes the authorities even rely on them to prepare project
applications as in the case of the Ketelwald project (no. 29). Con-
versely, but in the same vein, several experts suggested that the
reason why there are so few ecological network projects at the
border between Poland and Germany is the lack of powerful NGOs.
This borderland is only sparsely populated and lags economically
behind. Environmental NGOs are present, but very few residents are
registered as members. The WWF ran a project called ‘Green Belt
Oder-Neiße’ (or ‘Green Belt Odra-Nysa’ respectively) from 1994
to 2000 in which many transboundary follow-up activities were
proposed. However, the initiative dried up by and large because
the actors did not dispose of sufﬁcient organisational and ﬁnancial
capacities.
The empirical analysis highlights two facilitating effects of
international institutions and organisations such as the Alpine Con-
vention, the ICPR or the structures around the ‘Green Belt along
the former Iron Curtain’ cooperation. First, they reduce transac-
tion costs by providing platforms where actors can meet, exchange
ideas and build project consortia. The second effect comes into play
when international organisations elaborate overarching ecological
network concepts which can function as frames of reference for
projects at the local and regional scale. Funding agencies can be
more easily persuaded to approve a project if it is presented as part
of a larger puzzle. Gap analyses, in particular, can motivate local
authorities to eliminate bottlenecks and gaps and channel funds to
places where they are needed most. That was the case, for example,
with ICPR projects, which prides itself in the removal of many dams
and sluices in recent years.
6. Discussion, recommendations and possible directions
for further research
Our initial statement that the ecological deﬁnition of ‘ecologi-
cal network’ was straightforward is at odds with the ﬁnding that
practitioners in transboundary cooperation use the term almost
ubiquitously. On the other hand, the observation is not entirely sur-
prising, because actors from different social contexts, with different
cultural and educational backgrounds can subscribe to a norma-
tive claim or to an issue of cooperation only if there is ample room
for divergent interpretations. From an ecological standpoint, this
vagueness may be deemed problematic as Boitani et al. (2007, pp.
1415–1419) have emphasized. On the other hand, terminological
openness can be considered a strength as it allows for integration
across social boundaries. Hence, beside the deﬁnitions in con-
servation biology, the notion of ‘ecological network’ can also be
approached as representing a ‘boundary object’ (Bowker and Star,
1999, p. 297) or a ‘multi-interpretable story line’ which can eas-
ily function as ‘the glue that hold[s] together the coalitions behind
transnational policy discourses’ (Hajer, 2000, p. 140; cf. also Van
der Windt and Swart, 2008).
Many projects do not seem to be too valuable from a pan-
European perspective and particularly with regard to the ecological
consequences of global warming because they deal with small areas
and are apparently not part of any overarching strategy. This is a
generic problem, especially with many INTERREG projects, because
generalists and budgeting experts play a key role in designing
and administering these programmes. These actors are not very
familiar with sector-speciﬁc strategies and have a predominant
interest in implementing the programmes properly according to
the accounting standards of the European Union. That means that
INTERREG programmes, other than, for example, the LIFE pro-
gramme, do not primarily follow any sector-speciﬁc logic, but
rather a logic of bureaucracy (cf. Gualini, 2003, p. 48; Perkmann,
1999, pp. 663–665).
However, not all transboundary projects aiming at establish-
ing ecological networks need cover large areas to be effective. For
species such as the freshwater pearl mussel (M. margaritifera) or
for habitat types such as sand dunes, it would be either extremely
difﬁcult or totally inappropriate to establish ecological networks
at the regional or even transnational scale. Instead it has to be
regarded a success if local subpopulations are connected and if
new funds can repeatedly be acquired for the continuous improve-
ment of the conservation status. Although our analysis addressed
only the period 2003–2007, we found many such temporal project
clusters consisting of project series. This applies not only to the
cluster ‘Czech-Saxon-Bavarian border triangle’ and the ‘French-
Swiss-German border triangle’ cluster, and here particularly to the
‘Regiobogen’ project (no. 18).
Nevertheless some forms of transboundary cooperation in
establishing ecological networks fall into the category of one-shot
activities with limited ecological effects. This could in part be due
to the fact that there are few ecological network strategies or con-
cepts to date that cover larger transnational areas and that are at
the same time precise enough to inform and to guide concrete
implementation measures on the ground. A positive exception is
the ‘Atlas Ecological Network Rhine’ that was prepared by the ICPR
and has a scale of 1:100,000 and covers the entire Rhine, whereas
the ‘Indicative map of the Pan-European Ecological Network in
Western Europe’ (Bouwma, Jongman & van Doorn, 2006), for exam-
ple, offers little usable information for Germany and neighbouring
countries. Thus it is highly advisable to elaborate more spatially
speciﬁc ecological network concepts at national, bi-national and
international or European levels. In recent years the German federal
agency for nature conservation has commissioned several research
projects on a potential national ecological network concept which
shall be presented soon.
Almost all respondents in the in-depth interviews stressed the
uniqueness of their speciﬁc projects. While this is of course true,
since no two projects are the same, it shows how little many grass-
roots activists know about similar projects in other regions. There
92
M. Leibenath et al. / Landscape and Urban Planning 94 (2010) 84–93
are few broader forums for the exchange of experiences with trans-
boundary ecological networks in particular or with transboundary
nature conservation in general. A practical recommendation would
therefore be to intensify the ﬂow of knowledge and information
between practitioners in this ﬁeld and to establish more institution-
alized forums which could be modelled on the already mentioned
‘Kleve meetings’.
The creation of ecological networks is often portrayed as a
complex coordination challenge which presupposes, inter alia, the
participation of actors from different policy sectors. With this in
mind, many of the projects we identiﬁed have been quite successful
because they involve representatives from:
• tourism (e.g., the project ‘Nat’Our’, no. 27),
• forestry (e.g., the project ‘Network of wet habitats Maas-
Schwalm-Nette’, no. 30),
• ﬁshery (e.g., the project ‘Revitalisation of Taubergießen—Old
Rhine with new dynamic’, no. 24) or
• hydrology (e.g., the project ‘Protection of the freshwater pearl
mussel (M. margaritifera) in the catchment area of Südliche Reg-
nitz, Zinnbach and others’, no. 11).
Yet project-based cooperation and the informal networks it
relies on require win-win situations or positive-sum games to keep
the different actors—and stakeholders respectively—involved. As
participation is voluntarily, an organisation will only make com-
mitments if it expects net beneﬁts. Substantial conﬂicts about the
use of natural resources can hardly be solved by informal coopera-
tion. They are much easier to tackle with statutory, legally binding
forms of planning and decision-making. As a matter of fact statutory
spatial planning, i.e., comprehensive spatial plans in combination
with landscape plans at the level of states and regions, represents
one of the most prominent instruments for safeguarding ecologi-
cal networks in Germany. All German states adjacent to Germany’s
external borders except for Lower Saxony do already dispose of
state-wide landscape planning documents that include spatially
speciﬁc stipulations for ecological networks or are currently in the
process of drafting such documents (Hänel, 2007, pp. 78–114).
Statutory spatial planning is a powerful instrument in Germany
with its highly codiﬁed and formalized planning system when it
comes to designating various stretches of land as parts of an ecolog-
ical network and to protecting them legally from being converted
into adverse uses. Statutory planning has its limits with regard to
the development of ecological networks in the sense of, for instance,
transforming an intensely used farm plot into a multifunctional
ecological corridor. However, spatial plans can provide direction
and guidance for informal instruments such as agri-environmental
schemes or the kind of project-based activities that dominate along
Germany’s external borders. Currently the issue of ecological net-
works is receiving growing attention from spatial planning agencies
in Germany, maybe because the concept can be helpful in achieving
goals such as a balanced spatial structure which spatial planning is
required to adhere to anyway.
Spatial plans have to be coordinated across national and inter-
national boundaries. There are a number of mostly informal
coordination mechanisms at work, often in the form of inter-
ministerial or interagency working groups. This applies, among
others, to Germany’s borders with the Netherlands, Switzerland,
the Czech Republic and Poland. However, statutory spatial plan-
ning and related cross-border coordination were not referred to
by either interviewees or on the project web pages. We therefore
recommend strengthening the ties between ecological network
planning and spatial planning in transboundary contexts. This holds
especially for the many conﬂicts between transboundary ecological
networks and transport infrastructure.
Some interviewees had experiences with transboundary com-
mittees
and
working
groups
for
nature
conservation.
One
representative of the state government of Rhineland-Palatinate
explained that the French-German working group of which he was
a member was making very slow progress over years because of
communication problems. Only when his authority hired an ofﬁcer
with good proﬁciency in French, issues such as ecological networks
could be discussed in detail.
The empirical analysis that has been presented here can be the
starting point for many different research endeavours in the future.
In our view, two directions are especially promising. The ﬁrst is to
investigate the reasons for the failure of transboundary cooperation
to establish ecological networks. In our research we came across
many initiatives that did not yield the intended results or that did
not evolve the stable cooperation over time that the initiators had
envisaged. By studying such ‘failed’ projects, a lot could be learned
about the conditions that are necessary to make projects successful
in the long term. Notwithstanding the need for further research,
one practical recommendation can already be given at this point:
in the process of approving the applications for funds, projects that
perpetuate already existing transboundary cooperation could be
assigned higher priority.
The vertical coordination mechanisms in large transnational
cooperation initiatives such as the Alpine Network (cluster III) and
the ‘Green Belt along the former Iron Curtain’ (cluster I) consti-
tute another interesting object of further study. Here the questions
are, e.g., how transnational priorities are brought into line with the
aspirations and interests of actors at the local and regional levels,
how local knowledge feeds bottom-up into transnational strategies
and, vice versa, how activities at the local level can be instigated
top-down.
