Situating water in peacebuilding: revisiting the Middle East peace
process
Karin Aggestam* and Anna Sundell-Eklund
Department of Political Science, Lund University, Lund, Sweden
(Received 16 October 2012; accepted 20 September 2013)
Few studies have conceptually discussed the linkage between hydropolitics and peace-
building. This article critically assesses the problematique of conflict and cooperation
in the Jordan River basin and explores the underlying and shared assumptions between
functionalist negotiation principles and liberal peacebuilding practices. It analyzes the
competing positions among Israelis and Palestinians and revisits the water negotiations
in the Middle East peace process. It reveals how technical framing of water coopera-
tion takes precedence, which tends to ignore power asymmetry and the politics of
water.
Keywords: Jordan River basin; hydropolitics; liberal peacebuilding; negotiation;
peace; Israel; Palestinians
Introduction
Fierce national competition over water resources has prompted fears that water issues contain
the seeds of violent conflict. . . . If all the world's peoples work together, a secure and
sustainable water future can be ours. (UN Secretary-General Kofi Annan, World Water Day
2002, quoted in Cosgrove, 2003, p. 1)
In recent years, water scarcity and its implications for cooperation and conflict have
been fiercely debated in academia. Based on neo-Malthusian and realist assumptions,
several scholars have warned that violent conflicts may erupt, since populations and
demands for fresh water are growing in water-stressed areas while the lack of trust
between states prevents them from seeking joint solutions to the management of
shared water resources (Amery, 2002; Chellaney, 2013). Furthermore, the zero-sum
game between conflicting states is exacerbated by an upstream␀downstream problem
in river basins (Lowi, 1995; Wollebaek, Gleditsch, & Hegre, 2000). In addition to
neo-Malthusian and geopolitical concerns, diminished water availability as a result of
global warming is often described as a potential source of conflict. Projections of
climatic changes in the Middle East include rising temperatures and decreased pre-
cipitation, which will increase evaporation of surface water, slow rejuvenation of
groundwater
and
add
to
aridity
in
the
whole
region
(Feitelson,
Tamimi,
&
Rosenthal, 2012; Gleditsch, 2012). Climate change is also assumed to have negative
effects on economic growth, social stability and food security due to diminished
production from local agriculture and higher global food prices. According to some
studies, unpredictable climate patterns may add to uncertainty and contribute to the
securitization of natural resources (Brown & Crawford, 2009; IPCC, 2007). In his
