widely cited book, Environment, Scarcity, and Violence (1999), Thomas Homer-Dixon
underlines that these challenges are expected to put pressure on institutions of water
resource management and that the capacity to deal with these changes will determine
the risk of future violence.
The water␀war linkage has been reinforced by statements and alarm calls by promi-
nent policy makers, such as the successive UN Secretaries-General Boutros Boutros-
Ghali, Kofi Annan and Ban Ki-moon (Amery, 2002; Scheumann & Schiffler, 1998;
UNESCO, 2009). These public calls have had a tremendous impact on international
policy discourses on water, as reflected in the numerous reports produced in recent
years on water and security (Brown & Crawford, 2009; ICA, 2012; WBGU, 2008;
WEF, 2011).
This article seeks to address and conceptually expose the interplay between
hydropolitics and peacebuilding practices by combining the literature on transbound-
ary1 water management with peacebuilding theory in the empirical case illustration of
Israeli␀Palestinian water negotiations. Hydropolitics refers to ␀conflicts and negotia-
tion processes between sovereign states on water allocation and distribution, particu-
larly in relation to transboundary rivers or aquifers␀ (Mollinga, 2008; see also
Sneddon & Fox, 2005; Trottier, 1999). The article has a threefold aim. First, it
seeks to critically assess the water␀war discourse and explore the problematique of
conflict and cooperation in order to highlight some basic assumptions that may be
traced to various theoretical traditions of international relations and peacebuilding
theory. Second, it strives to highlight the linkage between functionalist ideas of
negotiation and cooperation and the liberal peacebuilding paradigm. Third, it aims
to analyze the competing frames and negotiation positions held by the parties in the
Middle East peace process (MEPP). The article concludes by arguing that there is a
convergence between functional ideas about water negotiations and liberal peace-
building practices, which tend to depoliticize conflict while ignoring asymmetrical
power dynamics. It also suggests ways of advancing the research agenda on hydro-
politics and peacebuilding, particularly in relation to the roles played by international
actors.
Functionalism and water cooperation
The Middle East and the Jordan River basin are frequently mentioned in the water
literature in relation to conflict and violence (Amery, 2002; Gleick, 2000; Kliot, 1994;
Soffer, 1999). Particularly in the 1990s, urgent calls were made about the risk of
future water wars in the region. The animosity between the riparian parties of the
Jordan River, combined with droughts, population growth and increased consumption
habits, had resulted in prolonged over-utilization of both renewable and fossil water
resources, which in turn was considered a dangerous breeding ground for conflict
(Kliot, 1994). Yet, the worst doomsday predictions of coming water wars have been
met with academic objections due to their weak empirical underpinning. For instance,
the 1967 war is often mentioned as an example, suggesting that water and the
escalating competition over the Jordan River may have been the major cause behind
the conflict (Shemesh, 2004). Yet, upon closer scrutiny, it is apparent that water was
neither the cause of the war nor its main goal. However, it did have negative
consequences for future hydropolitical dynamics in the region. Hence, at the inter-state
level, violence arising out of water disputes has been rare, whereas water scarcity has
2
