been the cause of escalating violence at national and sub-national levels (Selby, 2003a;
Wolf, 2000).
Countering the more pessimistic water␀war thesis, the prospect of a peace resulting
from hydro-cooperation has surfaced as a rivalling counter-discourse, which has trig-
gered a discursive turn in academia as well as among policy makers and international
donors (Katz, 2011). Aaron Wolf (1994), in his pioneering historical study of inter-state
water disputes, shows that water conflicts have been surprisingly rare; cooperation
overwhelmingly dominates among state actors.2 Since then, a series of studies have
been presented which suggest that technological and diplomatic advances make coopera-
tion rather than conflict a more likely outcome of water scarcity (Alam, 2002; Stefano,
de Edwards, de Silvia, & Wolf, 2010). Other scholars have suggested that mutually
shared interests have resulted in and continue to foster cooperation in the Jordan River
basin (Abramson et al., 2010; Dolaytar & Gray, 2000). Data sharing, joint management
of the Yarmouk and Jordan Rivers by Israel and Jordan, and technical cooperation
between Palestinians and Israelis are mentioned as examples of collaboration between
the riparians (Amery & Wolf, 2000). The Israeli␀Jordanian peace treaty stipulates the
joint construction of a desalination plant to produce additional water to the basin (Treaty
of Peace, 1994, Annex II). In line with this, a shared desalination plant to supply Aqaba
in Jordan and Eilat in Israel with potable water has been suggested, even though the
exact location and funding of the project have still to be decided upon (Fischhendler,
2008). Large-scale water-transfer schemes have also been suggested, such as the Red
Sea␀Dead Sea Water Conveyance. This is a trilateral project between Israel, Jordan and
the Palestinian Authority, which was recently evaluated in a feasibility study commis-
sioned by the World Bank. In the 1990s, as part of the MEPP, less developed and
refined ideas were launched, such as the ␀Peace Pipeline␀ from Turkey to Israel, via
Syria, and the transfer of Nile water to Israel and the West Bank (Soffer, 1999).
A shared underlying assumption that is reflected in several analyses of water
cooperation and peace proposals originates from functionalist theory, which figures
prominently in international relations theory on peace and European integration.
Functionalism stresses the possibility of nurturing and developing cooperation between
disputants in areas of low politics, which ideally builds trust between parties and then
generates positive spill-over effects in other fields of high politics. In practice, it means
framing joint activities in the water sector in a technical and apolitical manner. The
theory also underlines the importance of scientific knowledge, expertise and technology
and how international organizations and institutions assist in the construction of common
frames, interests and needs between states and non-state actors (Haas, 2008; J␀gerskog,
2009; Sosland, 2008). In the specialized communities of hydrology and water engineer-
ing, these frames are frequently based on environmental universalism and rationality. As
such, they are less context sensitive and may therefore risk producing unintended
consequences and even counterproductive results (Stetter, Herschinger, Teichler, &
Albert, 2011; Trottier & Brooks, 2013).
Functionalist assumptions are also reflected in negotiation theory. The ␀principle of
gradualism␀underlines how a step-by-step approach, which preferably starts off in areas
of low rather than high politics, may widen and deepen negotiations. Small incremental
steps are framed as less controversial than trying to resolve core conflicting issues. As a
consequence, deep mistrust between adversaries may be transformed over time by
sequenced peacemaking strategies, making a sustainable peace agreement more likely
(Weiss, 2003).
