Situating water negotiations within the liberal peacebuilding paradigm
In parallel with the debates on water scarcity and conflict, the liberal peacebuilding
paradigm has evolved since the end of the Cold War as a global response to the challenges
posed by contemporary conflicts. As early as 1992, then UN Secretary-General Boutros
Boutros-Ghali launched his ambitious `Agenda for Peace', which stressed the responsi-
bility of the international community to prevent, manage and resolve conflicts so as to
reach durable and self-sustainable peace. Contemporary conflicts have often been
described as `new wars', triggered by state `failure' or `collapse' (Kaldor, 2012). By
gradually framing these conflicts in technical terms, for instance as complex emergencies,
the core problems have related to state structures and institutions and less to politics per
se. As a consequence, peacebuilding activities have expanded dramatically in recent
decades. In contrast to traditional peacekeeping, peacebuilding is to be understood as an
umbrella notion which covers a broad range of strategies and spheres ranging from peace
negotiations, through elections, state and institution building, and economic development,
to reconciliation (Ramsbotham, Woodhouse, & Miall, 2011).
Liberal peacebuilding has also become the favoured blueprint of the international
community, as is reflected in the growing number of handbooks and toolboxes on
peacebuilding produced in recent years. The underlying assumption of liberal peace-
building is democratic peace theory, which advances the thesis that liberally constituted
societies are more peaceful than illiberal states. Moreover, mature democracies do not go
to war with each other, because of institutional constraints upon their political leaders.
Economic interdependence and regional cooperation are also assumed to make these
states more peaceful and prosperous. Consequently, technical and judicial processes of
institutionalization, governance, neo-liberalization and economic development are prior-
itized and supported as part of peacebuilding efforts in conflict-ridden regions (Campbell,
Chandler, & Sabaratnam, 2011; Newman, Paris, & Richmond, 2009). At the same time,
the nature and impact of liberal peacebuilding practices have been debated and contested
in the academic and policy communities in recent years. Critiques point to, among other
things, the lack of local ownership and broader participation, and to cultural insensitivity,
and question more generally the suitability of exporting Western liberal political and
economic ideas to diverse conflict settings (MacGinty, 2011; Richmond, 2010).
Just as transboundary water cooperation has been inspired by functionalist assump-
tions and gradual technological strategies within a non-political context, so the blueprint
of liberal peacebuilding has similarly attempted to depoliticize and reframe conflicting
issues towards technical solutions (Aggestam & Str␀mbom, 2013). Yet, despite the fact
that there is an abundance of books and articles referring to peace, water and the Middle
East in general, there are surprisingly few studies that explicitly and conceptually explore
the interplay between water and liberal peacebuilding practices (Abitol, 2009; Kramer,
2008; Selby, 2003a; Weinthal, Troell, & Nakayama, 2011; Zeitoun, 2008).
Liberal peacebuilding and the framing of water negotiations in the Middle East
peace process
Resolution of the water conflict, it was thought, would only be achieved if a technical
solution brought more water to both parties. This approach carried over into the Oslo talks
when water issues, defined as ␀water resources␀were considered a technical issue and issues
of water quality separated off into separated talks on environment. It is this inappropriate
definition of the problem that, we believe, is now blocking resolution of water issues that
separate Israelis and Palestinians. (Brooks & Trottier, 2010, pp. 9␀10)
4
