The framing of conflict, cooperation and negotiation refers to dominant world-views and
discourse structures, which guide actors in their interpretations and construction of meanings
about water (Barnes & Alatout, 2012; Fr␀lich, 2012). For instance, geopolitical reasoning is
identified as a major impediment to cooperation in the Jordan River basin. Likewise, in
conflict-ridden societies water tends to be securitized, as it is perceived to be related to
existential concerns and uncertainties. In the Middle East, most of the available water
resources are allocated to irrigation in the agricultural sector, which is embedded in most
countries as an integral part of state and nation building (FAO, 2008; Stetter et al., 2011).
Negotiations obviously contain both cooperative as well as conflicting elements, because
without common interests there is nothing to negotiate for, and without conflicting interests
there is nothing to negotiate about (Ikl␀, 1964). The way conflict is understood has con-
sequences for how negotiations are pursued. In the empirical analysis below, the contrasting
framesofwaterconflictaswellasapproachesto negotiation are highlighted.Thediscrepancies
expose the extent of major disagreements that exist between the parties (Alatout, 2006).
From the outset of the peace process in 1991, water constituted an important area of the
standing working groups within the multilateral framework aiming to foster regional coopera-
tion and sustainable peace. In the bilateral and secret negotiations in Oslo during 1992␀1993, a
gradual approach was adopted as the only possible way to achieve an agreement between Israel
and the Palestine Liberation Organization (PLO). After eight months of intense negotiations in
Norway, the parties reached a Declaration of Principles (DOP), which was signed in Washington
in 1993 (Declaration of Principles on Interim Self-Government Arrangements, 1993).
The MEPP was from the outset framed according to the notion of a liberal peace,
despite the continuing Israeli occupation. The DOP was packaged and marketed with
economic cooperation, development and prosperity in sight. For instance, Israeli foreign
minister Shimon Peres, in his timely book The New Middle East (1993), outlined his ideas
of peace and economic interdependence, drawing heavily on the European experience of
functionalism and integration. Simultaneously, there was a great influx of international
actors and donors after the signing of the DOP.
As the water␀war discourse had been dominant among policy makers in the 1990s, a whole
range of hydro-cooperative projects between Israelis and Palestinians were initiated and received
generous donor funding, on the assumption that functional water cooperation would be easier to
establish because expertise and economic development would dominate it. Water was also seen
as a particularly ripe area for cooperation because of its emphasis on technological development
rather than politics. Many technical water projects were initiated and sponsored in an effort to
develop additional water supplies, particularly for the Palestinians. Most of these efforts were
centred on water cooperation in the Eastern Basin and the Gaza Strip (Selby, 2003a).
The DOP stipulates interim Palestinian self-rule, but does not address the major
contentious issues, such as the status of Jerusalem, borders, territories, Jewish settlements
and Palestinian refugees. It states that the final status negotiations should resolve all these
issues within a five-year period. The agreement mentions water as a potential area of
cooperation and highlights the need to establish a Palestinian Water Authority (PWA) in
order to promote economic growth. However, as the agreement concerns general princi-
ples, which do not specify agreed-upon interpretations, water rights and resource alloca-
tion are only vaguely and imprecisely referred to.
The second major interim agreement between Israel and the PLO was signed in 1995,
the so-called Oslo II (Israeli-Palestinian Interim Agreement on the West Bank and the
Gaza Strip, 1995).3 It stresses the need for cooperation over water resources and it thus
provides greater details about water management. Both parties agreed to develop addi-
tional water supply from the West Bank's underground aquifers to meet the needs of the
