Palestinians. According to Jan Selby (2003b), this provided a first attempt towards more
equitable water management. A Joint Water Committee was established with the aim of
coordinating, protecting and managing water resources and sewage systems. A system of
joint teams was set up for supervision and monitoring. However, it is important to note
that the Joint Water Committee has no authority to regulate or manage the existing
quantities of water currently under the control of the Israeli civil administration and the
Jewish settlements in the occupied territories (Schl␀tter, 2005). Thus, some scholars argue
that the ␀Oslo II negotiations did little more than to formalise and legitimate management
structures and relations, which were already very much in existence␀ (Selby, 2003b,
p. 127; see also Zeitoun, Mirumachi, & Warner, 2011).
Technical framing of water negotiations
Israel has been successful in coping with water scarcity due to the structural features of its
society, state control and massive investment in infrastructure. Water resources have been at
the core of the state-building project, as well as part of the Zionist ideology of settling the
land and making the desert ␀bloom␀ (Harris & Alatout, 2010). The Zionist tradition and
ideology of cultivating the land has deeply influenced policy making in the past. Apart from
the spiritual and cultural aspects of farming activities, food security and land claims have
been of importance when allocating water to agriculture (Lipchin, 2006; Trottier, 2007). As
a consequence, water policies are highly centralized and state controlled. Water irrigation is
also heavily subsidized, even though agriculture only contributes a few per cent to the GNP
(Borthwick, 2010; Selby, 2005). After the 1967 war and subsequent occupation of the West
Bank and Gaza Strip, Israel declared all water resources in the occupied territories to be state
controlled and under the jurisdiction of the Israeli military. The interdependence between
Israelis and the Palestinians on water resources is particularly interwoven because they share
two groundwater basins: the Coastal Aquifer, on the costal plain of Israel and the Gaza Strip,
and the Mountain Aquifer, which includes the Western Mountain basin, the Eastern
Mountain basin and the North-eastern Mountain basin (see Figure 1). The Mountain
Aquifer system provides nearly all the water consumed by the West Bank Palestinians4
and constitutes 40% of Israel's naturally renewable fresh water (Borthwick, 2010).
The Israeli approach to water negotiation and peacebuilding has been guided
primarily by technical strategies. The embedded technological discourse of water
management and development is dissociated from historical contexts and has attempted
to steer away from political aspects of water rights (Harris & Alatout, 2010). As a
consequence, Israel has strived to achieve agreements which address only the joint
management of existing resources, conservation, and joint development of new water
supplies (Weinthal & Marei, 2002). Claims and demands made by the Palestinians for
access to the Mountain Aquifer have been rejected. Israel argues that it has historical
rights to the water because the water previously appeared as springs within pre-1967
borders and Israel was the first to invest in and use the sources. Furthermore, the
contested aquifers provide water to the urban areas of Tel Aviv and Jerusalem (Soffer,
1999). Consequently, a depoliticized approach to water negotiations has been preferred.
Such an approach does not recognize the relevance of water rights and international
law. The underlying fear is that transfer of water rights and Israeli control would
ultimately undermine Israeli strategic interests (Weinthal & Marei, 2002). In line with
the dominant technical and water-engineering approach, ␀many Israeli water profes-
sionals have realized that manufacturing water will be much easier than negotiating it␀(Allan, 2002, p. 269).
