Rights-based framing of water negotiations
The Palestinian framing of water and negotiation must be viewed through the prism of
continued Israeli military occupation. Since the war in June of 1967, Palestinians have
been subject to severe water-use restrictions. Israel does not recognize the Palestinians'
basic water needs or past historical water management practices and prefers to distribute
water through its national water company, Mekorot. Furthermore, very few Palestinians
have received permission to drill wells. Consequently, Israel has become the main supplier
of water to the Palestinians (Selby, 2003a).
Consequently, the main Palestinian position on water negotiations originates from
water rights as well as from demands for a greater share of and access to water
resources (Fr␀lich, 2012). Palestinians claim the right to most of the water in the West
Bank, both the groundwater and the Jordan River, which are embedded within a
historical context and international law. The Palestinian approach has been guided
by principles of rights-based water allocation, compensation and the need to return to
the pre-occupation laws, which will enable their own body of water laws to develop in
the West Bank and the Gaza Strip (Weinthal & Marei, 2002). They frequently refer to
the Helsinki Rules (1966) and the 1997 Convention on the Law of the Non-naviga-
tional Uses of International Watercourses, which underscore the equitable sharing of
water and that no party should cause ␀significant harm␀ to another user. The
Palestinians have for a long time sought Israeli recognition of these water rights,
but to no avail. Also, international humanitarian law tends to be state-centric and
barely deals with the use of water resources between enemies or binding agreements
between states and non-state actors, which is the case between Israel and the PLO
(Schl␀tter, 2005).
Palestinians have few options for income other than agriculture, due to the various
restrictions that are imposed by the occupation.5 A relatively large part of the limited
water resources is consumed by the agriculture sector, despite shortages of drinking water.
However, overall the Palestinians consume much less than their neighbours (FAO, 2008).
Important to note, however, are the leakages due to dissolving infrastructure, which
generate water losses of around 25% in the West Bank and 40% in the Gaza Strip
(Schl␀tter, 2005). Military confrontation has further degraded the water situation.
Amnesty International (2009) claims that water and wastewater infrastructure worth
about USD 6 million was destroyed in the Gaza Strip during the Israeli military offensive
in December 2008.
As previously mentioned, Oslo II does not specify or define Palestinian water rights,
and Israel therefore continues to control the vast majority of supply lines and deep wells.
Israel also provides the settlers with highly subsidized rates on water, while withholding
information on extraction levels from wells located inside the Jewish settlements.
According to Selby (2003b), the Oslo II agreement therefore implicitly sanctions the
continuing use and access by Israel of Palestinian water resources during the interim
period.
Beyond the difficulties of coping with the ongoing Israeli occupation, the PWA has
had difficulties in directing and controlling the actions of local Palestinian interests,
municipalities and village councils. At the same time, the PWA is trying to accommodate
the criteria stipulated by international donors and private-sector contractors, since the
PWA has the overall responsibility to observe large internationally funded projects. There
have also been strong pressures by international actors and donors to have only one
coordinating body with overarching plans, which oftentimes contradicts the practical
8
K. Aggestam and A. Sundell-Eklund
