needs on the ground (Kramer, 2008; Selby, 2003a). This has at times generated conflicts
between water experts, NGOs and the local Palestinian population. In 2002, the
Palestinian Authority introduced its water law, stating that most springs should be con-
trolled under communal-property regimes, with the PWA as the regulator (Trottier &
Brooks, 2013). Yet, in the West Bank there is a great diversity of communal and informal
water institutions, which challenge the authority of the Palestinian Authority. Yet, over
time, these traditional communal forms of water management have increasingly been
disempowered. There also exists a rivalry between the PWA and the local Palestinian
NGOs for international funding of water projects, as both are highly dependent on funds
and aid from the international community (Selby, 2003a; Trottier, 2007; Trottier &
Brooks, 2013).
Conclusion
This article has addressed and analyzed the convergence of functionalist theory and
gradualist approaches to water negotiations within the wider context of the liberal
peacebuilding paradigm. The overarching design of the Middle East peace process
was based on the principle of gradualism in directed efforts of peacebuilding, particu-
larly in areas of economic development, technology and institutions. As such, it fell well
within the scope of the liberal peacebuilding paradigm; the water sector was particularly
ripe for cooperative projects, which consequently received generous funding from
international donors. Analysis of the Israeli␀Palestinian water negotiations reveals the
contrasting and divergent frames of the parties. The Israeli approach has emphasized
technology, whereas the Palestinian has favoured a basis in rights. In hindsight, we can
see that the technological approach has dominated, which consequently marginalizes the
politics of water management. The avoidance of politics and rights-based approaches has
furthermore led to a tacit acceptance of the power asymmetry between the parties and
the status quo of the conflict (continued Israeli occupation). As Zeitoun et al. (2011)
pointed out, Israel as the dominant hegemon has been successful not only in promoting
its own technical discourse of hydro-negotiation but also in engaging the international
donor community to assist, manage and yet not resolve the water conflict. Also, since
the collapse of the MEPP, Israel is pursuing what some scholars label ␀unilateral
environmentalism␀ (Fischhendler, Dinar, & Katz, 2011).
In sum, two concluding remarks are offered to advance the research agenda on
hydropolitics and liberal peacebuilding. First, there is a need of further research, which
focuses on the roles of third parties and international donors in water management and
negotiation. More studies in this field have the potential to generate important policy-
relevant theory and insights about governance mechanisms and institution building in
water management. International actors oftentimes play a doubled-edged role in water
conflict by mediating as well as financing water-development projects. At the same time,
international water experts are inclined to focus more narrowly and technically on water
issues, which at times may cause counterproductive results because the suggested solu-
tions lack context sensitivity. In addition, there is fierce competition for donor funds and
international aid between Palestinian agencies where some are empowered and others
disempowered by international donor assistance (Katz, 2011; Selby, 2003a).
Second, while inter-state water conflicts may be few, water scarcity and poor quality
may trigger the escalation of local conflicts into violence. Yet, most research focuses on
inter-state cooperation and negotiation, which tends to ignore local forms of manage-
ment and bottom-up indigenous processes. For instance, while Israel favours centralized,
