Notes
1.
In this article, transboundary water is defined as surface water or groundwater that crosses or
constitutes international borders (see also Cosgrove, 2003).
2.
Yet, it is important to note that the absence of water wars does not mean that water conflicts are
resolved. At the domestic level, violent and nonviolent conflicts are occurring between various
interest groups in different societies (Trottier, 2007). Zeitoun and Mirumachi (2008) also
highlight negative forms of transboundary water interactions, such as asymmetric and coercive
cooperation where soft power is used to influence outcomes.
3.
With the Oslo II agreement in 1995, the West Bank was divided into three areas. In Area A, the
Palestinians exercise full autonomy; in Area B, the Palestinians have limited autonomy; and in
Area C Israel has full control. To note, the Palestinian Authority controls only approximately
40% of the West Bank (Areas A and B), and these areas are not contiguous.
4.
Since the peace treaty between Jordan and Israel, the Jordan River is no longer available as a
water resource to the Palestinians (Schl␀tter, 2005).
5.
The agricultural sector often serves as a societal buffer zone, as it offers work opportunities for
many who have lost their jobs elsewhere in times of crisis (Butterfield, Isaac, Kubursi, &
Spencer, 2000).
