1. Introduction
Socioeconomic demands can exert pressure on water resources and
lead to a decline in ecosystem services by having adverse effects on
water quality or water distribution (Sharmina et al., 2016). An inte-
grated response to this problem becomes even more challenging
when water demands are competitive and local stakeholder priorities
diverge, suggesting that water management is an inherently political
task (Mollinga, 2008). Skewed power dynamics can result in resource
mismanagement and lack of accountability (Lyla and Bill, 2017;
Zinzani and Bichsel, 2018). This is observed at different scales, from
community marginalisation through to transboundary water hegemony
(Butler and Adamowski, 2015; Petersen-Perlman and Fischhendler,
2018).
In this context, institutional capacity and relationships become in-
creasingly important for sustainable water management. The term “in-
stitutions” is used broadly to describe government agencies, subject
relevant organisations and agents, while capacity refers to their ability
to jointly fulﬁl a purpose (McGill, 1996), in this case delivering Inte-
grated Water Resources Management (United Nations, 1992). In this re-
gard, water governance is essentially the processes and institutions
through which decisions are made related to water (Lautze et al.,
2011). The attempt to identify the principles by which good governance
adheres is amalgamated by Roger and Hall (Rogers and Hall, 2003).
These include some principles that are related to a “good process prac-
tice” (open, transparent, participative, accountable, communicative
and ethical) and others directly to the “water resources outcomes” (efﬁ-
cient, equitable, integrative, effective and sustainable) (Lautze et al.,
2011). In the rest of this paper, governance is always referred in the con-
text of water governance speciﬁcally.
Multilevel governance is planned, and statutory roles are assigned
such that the diffusion of policies and regulations is possible. However,
institutions rarely function exactly as intended, and are instead shaped
or formed through complex processes of adoption and adaptation of
practice (Cleaver, 2012; Merrey and Cook, 2012). In practice, they as-
sume certain responsibilities and duties in order to maintain a well-
functioning catchment (Papacharalampou et al., 2017). Institutions
performing their role successfully are subject to their technical exper-
tise, ﬁnancial capacity and strength of cross-organisational links. Institu-
tions without ties to other stakeholders may not have the necessary
capacity to perform their roles effectively (McNicholl et al., 2017).
Only few studies have investigated inter-organisational networks across
scales and how these enhance capacity for effective water management
(Azhoni et al., 2018). Kellogg and Samanta (2018) performed a network
governance analysis for the Chagrin River Catchment, Ohio, and found
that although a multitude of stakeholders is involved in water manage-
ment, the network largely depends on a central organisation for infor-
mation and resource management. They suggest that this hinders
adaptive management capacity.
With respect to water allocation speciﬁcally, market-based ap-
proaches exist but these can easily lead to cut-throat competition (Tu
et al., 2015). Examples of appropriative water allocation in the U.S.A
(i.e. based on seniority) have been criticised of making lower-rank
users more vulnerable to climate variability (Null and Prudencio,
2016). In practice, an administrative and market-based mechanism allo-
cation usually operate in conjunction and works optimally. Yan et al.
(2018) investigated how different allocation strategies can minimise
overall economic loss among users, but this results in “winners” and
“losers”. They identify, therefore, that this approach is not encouraged
and, rather compromises among users and improved water efﬁciency
are the only viable options (Ibid). For Southern Europe, Goetz et al.
(2017) demonstrate that rules based on social choice theory (collective
decision making) improve economic efﬁciency without introducing dis-
tributional inequality.
The impacts and management responses to socioeconomic and cli-
matic drivers can be evaluated through a system dynamics approach
(Kelly et al., 2019; Pluchinotta et al., 2018; Zomorodian et al., 2018).
Using this approach, institutions are able to design policies and imple-
ment them to respond adequately in a systemic manner (Verkerk
et al., 2015). System approaches allow multiple objectives to be co-
optimised. At the same time, trade-offs in water allocation are
recognised and managed accordingly (Nafarzadegan et al., 2018).
Pluchinotta et al. (2018) show how system dynamics modelling sup-
ports collective decision-making in irrigation water management.
Numerous recommendations often relying on simplistic ‘standard’
panaceas have been put forward for water governance reform without
testing of appropriateness in diverse contexts (Pahl-Wostl et al.,
2012). Instead, Everard et al. (2018) proposes context-speciﬁc
“engineered water resources governance”. The hypothesis of the pres-
ent article is that strong, context-speciﬁc institutional relationships are
key to delivering IWRM in environments with multiple stakeholders
and competitive water uses. The hypothesis is tested through the map-
ping and characterisation of such interactions and the assessment of
their “water resources outcomes” (Lautze et al., 2011). By extension,
the objective is to identify potential interventions at a technical or policy
level that require “better governance” (Rogers and Hall, 2003) in terms
of both “practice” and “outcomes” (Lautze et al., 2011).
By means of case study, this article combines a water budget analy-
sis, an egocentric network analysis (Crossley et al., 2015) and a DPSIR
analysis (OECD, 2001) to provide a mechanistic understanding of the
physical and socio-technical problem dimensions at the Axios Delta,
Greece. The methodology section below describes the study area and
analysis tools while the results and discussion sections present the ﬁnd-
ings and implications for the case study area as well as the approach's
transferability potential. The analysis is relevant to researchers, practi-
tioners, decision- and policy-makers interested in the design process
and the facilitation to sustainable transitions.
2. Methodology
2.1. Study area
As seen in Fig. 1, Axios River Delta is located on the northern coast of
Greece where the Axios River discharges into the Mediterranean Sea in
the Gulf of Thermaikos. Climate is transitional continental to Mediterra-
nean on the Koppen-Geiger classiﬁcation (Bear-ID Novatek, 2016;
Kotinis-Zambakas et al., 1984). With regard to interannual variation,
winter months tend to be wet and summers dry. The mean annual pre-
cipitation is 663 ± 151 mm (Chalastra Station, British Antarctic Survey)
(Kapetas et al., 2019).
The Delta is formed at the conﬂuence of four rivers: Aliakmon,
Loudias, Axios and Gallikos from west to east, respectively. Aliakmonas
contributes signiﬁcant ﬂows to the west delta, while Loudias is techni-
cally an artiﬁcial drainage channel. Gallikos river originates from
Kroussia Mountain and discharges into the Thermaikos Gulf. Its river
basin covers an area of 950 km2. Axios is a transboundary river shared
between Northern Macedonia and Greece. Its catchment has an area
of 24,437 km2, with only about 12% of this area in the Greek territory. Al-
though an agreement on river ﬂow management and coordination was
established between the two countries in 1959 (Global Water
Partnership, 2012), no formal agreement is practically active today.
Like Greece, North Macedonia makes abstractions from the river for ag-
ricultural use. This has resulted in uncertain ﬂows reaching across the
border depending on upstream abstractions requirements and cli-
matic/weather conditions. Within the Greek territory, most abstractions
take place at the mouth of the river as water intensive rice paddy ﬁelds
cover the largest part of the deltaic valley.
Groundwater abstractions on the eastern part of the delta take place
to cover urban water demands for the city of Thessaloniki. These
groundwater abstractions act primarily as a redundancy measure dur-
ing drought condition when the alternative major sources of water sup-
ply for the city are under pressure (EYATH, 2018). At the same time,
2
L. Kapetas et al. / Science of the Total Environment 695 (2019) 133831
quality of transboundary surface water and groundwater is moderate
due to nitrate, metal pollution, wastewater discharges and solid dump-
ing (Karageorgis et al., 2005; Milovanovic, 2007).
The Axios Delta also includes a National Park established in 2009
(Axios-Loudias-Aliakmonas Management Authority, 2018), has signiﬁ-
cant biodiversity value and offers extensive ecosystem services
(Hadjicharalambous et al., 2015). Maintaining the functions of this eco-
system depends on the supply of sufﬁcient water quantity of good qual-
ity. Hadjicharalambous et al. (2015) established the ecological ﬂow
characteristics for the Delta area which are required to support
favourable conditions for local species. A study by Karageorgis et al.
(2005) explored the interactions between Axios watershed and
Thermaikos Gulf highlighting the need for an integrated management
approach as pressures to both water bodies are tied.
Climate models show a change in spatiotemporal rainfall patterns
with more rainfall falling in fewer events. As a result, inﬁltration is
lower as well as the ability to distribute ﬂows equally over the year.
The reduction in the predicted Standardised Precipitation Index (SPI)
and the temperature increase shows the area is susceptible to droughts
(Hertig and Tramblay, 2017). This results in higher evapotranspiration
rates and therefore affects the required irrigation and abstraction
rates. The region's sea level rise is expected at the range of 9.8 and
25 cm by 2050 (Galassi and Spada, 2014). Such climatic impacts com-
prise pressures speciﬁc to the area and are considered in Section 3.1.
Kapetas et al. (2018, 2019) showed that the coastal aquifer is also
under risk of salinisation, particularly as climate change can alter river
ﬂow and rainfall patterns, and increase groundwater abstractions as a
response. Sea level rise is also expected to shift the fresh-salt water in-
terface. Thus, groundwater resources require attention within an Inte-
grated Water Resource Management Plan.
Within the Delta area operates the Wastewater Treatment Plant of
Thessaloniki. After treatment an amount of 180,000 m3 per day dis-
charges into the Thermaikos Gulf. Reclaimed water can be artiﬁcially
recharged via deep boreholes and also used for irrigation covering a
large part of irrigation needs in the study area (Spachos et al., 2012).
On a pilot level this has already been successfully performed.
2.2. Water budget analysis
A water budget analysis is carried out as a methodological step to es-
tablish what the key relevant hydrological processes are in the case
study area. To this end, a conceptual model for the Delta is built to quan-
tify ﬂuxes in/out its control volume. Primary data to calculate ﬂuxes
where obtained during the interview process with the relevant stake-
holders. A sound understanding of the hydrological processes and ﬂuxes
is necessary to (i) initially assess system performance, i.e. what the
water deﬁcit is and what the temporal variability in ﬂuxes is, and (ii)
understand whether all critical institutions are currently involved in
the governance process.
2.3. Egocentric network analysis
An institutional responsibilities analysis is carried out in the context of
Axios' deltaic system once a conceptual understanding of the hydrological
processes has been obtained. Information on institutional statutory roles,
responsibilities and inter-institutional interactions was collected via, ﬁrst,
document analysis (desk study) and, second, semi-structured interviews.
Interviewees were senior technical managers of the administrative and
advisory bodies. They did not hold a political position which could bias
the outcomes of the interviews. This qualitative approach allowed the in-
terviewers to gain a deeper understanding of the case via the inter-
viewees, as a semi-structured interview enables the researcher to guide
the discussion while allowing the respondent ﬂexibility in answering
(Bryman, 2016; Cohen and Crabtree, n.d.). This interactive method
allowed for free participant response, going into as much or as little detail
as they wanted about the water management system (Flick, 2015).
The discussion was facilitated by the presentation of the research
project and a map of the area to spatially situate the organisations' activ-
ities. The questionnaire included the following questions: 1. What is
your organisation's role? 2. With which other institutions and agencies
do you collaborate? 3. What sort of collaboration do you have? 4. What
is the nature of this collaboration? 5. In the context of data and informa-
tion exchange or action coordination, how strong and frequent would
you say this collaboration is? 6. What is your organisations' view of cli-
mate change and its impacts? What other challenges do you face? 7.
What is the priority for your organisations and for other organisations
in the context of Axios Delta? 8. What is your level of preparedness to
manage possible impacts? (self-assessment) 9. What is your organisa-
tions' plan for dealing with climate change? (existing plans, future ac-
tions, actions you have taken in drought conditions).
Survey questions were clearly deﬁned and, therefore, responses
were speciﬁc. Yet, an element of bias or subjective judgement is possible.
Fig. 1. Axios, Aliakmonas, Loudias and Gallikos River catchments and discharge location at Thermaikos Gulf where the Delta is formed. The study area is highlighted in red. (For
interpretation of the references to colour in this ﬁgure legend, the reader is referred to the web version of this article.)
3
L. Kapetas et al. / Science of the Total Environment 695 (2019) 133831
The accuracy of the responses was corroborated wherever possible by
examining the perception of the quality of the inter-institutional interac-
tion at both ends (i.e. between two system nodes as described below). A
time dimension is always present in this type of analysis as
(i) perceptions might change regarding organisational priorities (e.g.
as climate change is raised as priority in the political agenda, the impor-
tance of certain interactions will be seen as more critical by the stake-
holders) or (ii) as institutional roles might change, and by extension,
the need for certain interactions between stakeholders will change.
This information was used in the egocentric network analysis
(Crossley et al., 2015) that sheds light on missing or weak links among
stakeholders in the governance structure. Networks are made up of
‘nodes’ which represent stakeholders and government agencies at dif-
ferent levels. The nodes are then connected by ‘ties’ (Scott, 2017).
Once relationships have been established, the networks can be analysed
for quantitative properties, such us frequency of communication with
other nodes, or exchange of technology, resources and information
with them (McNicholl et al., 2017). In this study, a speciﬁc node is of
particular interest and, for this reason, the network immediately around
a node of interest. The central node is therefore considered as the ‘ego’
and is placed at the centre of the network, and the stakeholders that it
interacts are called ‘alters’ (McNicholl et al., 2017).
Power dynamics between the “alters” and the “ego” can be quanti-
ﬁed and classiﬁed in four tie types within the network: information,
skills, resources, and authority. Each tie type can contain subcategories
of different weights for distinguishing between stronger and weaker
ties. Section 3.4 presents the egocentric network analysis for Axios
Delta water resource management.
2.4. DPSIR analysis
The Drivers-Pressures-States-Impacts-Response (DPSIR) approach
was selected as a simple qualitative model to understand interactions
at a system's level. Socioeconomic and climatic Drivers give rise to pres-
sures (such as, for example, reduced precipitation) and “new” States for
water bodies (water-stress) that have a series of Impacts (e.g. reduced
crop yield and aquifer salinisation) to which societies and their agents
then provide a Response (e.g. adapting agricultural practices). There is a
breadth of studies in literature that have used this approach in a variety
of socio-environmental context (Expósito and Berbel, 2017; Jia et al.,
2018; Mattas and Soulios, 2011; Seydehmet et al., 2018; Vannevel,
2018; Wang et al., 2018), thus further details on the approach can be
found there. The model presented here (see Section 3.4) describes the
interactions between (i) the environment, (ii) agricultural activities
and (iii) urban water supply at the Axios Delta under climatic and socio-
economic drivers of change. A prerequisite for an accurate representa-
tion of interactions is a robust hydrological conceptual model (see
Sections 2.2 and 3.1). Once interactions are established, several climatic
and socio-environmental scenarios can be conceived and tested. These
scenarios include: (i) expected sea level rise between 9.8 and 25.6 cm
by 2050 (Galassi and Spada, 2014) increasing the risk of salinisation,
(ii) increase in drought severity with a reduction of Standardised Pre-
cipitation Index to −1 and an increase in intra-annual variability
(Hertig and Tramblay, 2017), (iii) reduction in sustained river ﬂow as
a result of the latter. Similarly, interventions can also be planned to
strengthen the systems' resilience by improving water allocation prac-
tices and protocols for the case study area. Interventions can therefore
represent (i) direct mitigation of a threat/pressure or (ii) support adap-
tation, i.e. via management at the impact level.
3. Results
3.1. Hydrological processes
Fig. 2 shows the three areas which receive water from river abstrac-
tions and are located at the East of Axios River. Areas on the west of the
Axios River are irrigated by Aliakmon abstractions, therefore, the focus
in this analysis is on the East Delta region. The areas of Agios Athanasios
and Chalastra, receive 97% of the abstractions, while 3% is transferred to
Nea Magnisia. Signiﬁcant surface-subsurface interactions take place
with water exchange between the underlying coastal aquifers and the
two rivers, namely Axios and Gallikos. Rice paddies are rainfed between
November and April, but between May and October, signiﬁcant trans-
fers take place from both Axios River (abstractions at the upstream di-
version dam of Agia Eleousa) and Aliakmon River.
Table 1 reports ﬂuxes in and out of the Delta area to highlight the
current water allocation practice. The surface water – groundwater in-
teraction observed in the study area are typical for most coastal aquifers
which are either rain or irrigation fed, are connected to rivers and sur-
face and groundwater abstractions take place. The attempt to draw an
annual water budget for the Delta is challenged by the lack of monitor-
ing information. Spatiotemporally distributed ﬂuxes would in fact pro-
vide a better guide for water allocation, yet this degree of detail is not
currently available. From the existing information, it becomes apparent
that current agricultural practices are water intensive; despite the rela-
tively abundant annual rainfall, signiﬁcant abstractions are required to
meet demands. In the summer when Axios River cannot meet the de-
mand and evapotranspiration is high (Lekakis et al., 2015), complemen-
tary trans-basin transfers from Aliakmon take place. It is noted that
when hydroelectricity is produced at the Aliakmon reservoir, the re-
leased water can support irrigation. However, the key priority is
hydro-electricity production and for this reason the amount of water re-
ceived is uncertain; agriculture cannot fully rely on these inﬂows. More-
over, groundwater abstractions for urban water supply can double
during droughts to compensate for the reduction in surface water avail-
ability. These typically comprise 20% of the total for a population of ap-
proximately 1 million people.
A critical difference in ﬂow management between Axios and
Aliakmon rivers is that the latter has a reservoir upstream of the
Delta: this allows the asset managers (DEI) to regulate ﬂows and supply
the environmental ﬂow throughout the year. In contrast, Axios ﬂow is
variable depending on storage management in Northern Macedonia
and abstractions in both countries. Most importantly, under non-
drought conditions, agriculture receives a ﬂat-rate of water without
consideration of river ﬂow variability and minimum environmental
ﬂow requirement.
In drought periods, the Emergency Plan (Ministry of Environment
and Energy, 2014) is implemented which bans irrigation and prioritises
urban water supply over ecosystem demands. The plan largely follows
the “Mediterranean Drought Preparedness and Mitigation Planning”
policy recommendations (European Commission - EuropeAid Co-
operation Ofﬁce, 2007).
3.2. Institutional responsibilities
As there are signiﬁcant competing water demands and the upstream
river basin area extends through multiple jurisdiction areas, a large
number of institutions and other stakeholders engage with water man-
agement in Axios Delta. The relevant institutions and stakeholders are
mentioned in this section, and their statutory authority or role is
discussed to allow a complete understanding of their interactions. Iden-
tiﬁcation of the relevant actors was carried out through (i) acquired
knowledge of statutory or consultative role, and (ii) semi-structured in-
terviews with all key stakeholders (14 interviews) in which they
expressed their views on water management as well as on their institu-
tional network.
At an international level, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MFA) has the
sole responsibility for communication and coordination with the re-
spective authority of Northern Macedonia with regard to water man-
agement. Unlike the EU transboundary water basins, technical experts
of both countries are not directly connected (respective technical capac-
ity in Northern Macedonia would be represented by the Ministry of
4
L. Kapetas et al. / Science of the Total Environment 695 (2019) 133831
Environment and Physical Planning, MOEPP). At a regional level, the
Water Directorate of Central Macedonia pertaining to the Ministry of the
Interior (YPES) is sitting within the Decentralized Administration of
Macedonia and Thrace (3rd level of decentralisation) and is responsible
for carrying out administrative acts in accordance to water policies, pri-
marily the Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC) which was sup-
plemented by the Groundwater Directive (2006/118/EC). Their role
includes the coordination and accounting of water allocation of Axios
Basin. In this capacity, several other institutions provide relevant infor-
mation to the Water Directorate. Monitoring is carried out by the De-
partment of Environment and Water Economy of the Region of Central
Macedonia (PKM, 2nd level of decentralisation). The General Land Recla-
mation Organisation (GOEV) is a private entity responsible for the agri-
cultural activity in the area, thus requesting and receiving a water
quota from both Axios and Aliakmonas, which is then distributed to
the Local Land Reclamation Organisation (TOEV). In practice, TOEV repre-
sents the Farmer's Unions.
Axios-Loudias-Aliakmonas Delta Management Authority is respon-
sible for the surveillance of the protected area, preservation of its ﬂora
and fauna, promotion of eco-tourism and sustainable development, as
well as raising awareness in the local community and visitors alike.
