The Authority is a legal entity of private law acting for the public beneﬁt
and is overseen by the Ministry of Environment and Energy (YPEΝ). The
Special Sectretariat for Water (EGY), which is a division of YPEΝ is re-
sponsible for the development and implementation of all programs re-
lated to the protection and management of the water resources of
Greece and the coordination of all competent authorities dealing with
the aquatic environment. The implementation of the Water Framework
Directive fall within the scope of the activities of the Secretariat. The
Secretariat, in collaboration with the Regional Water Authorities, for-
mulates and implements the River Basin Management Plans and the na-
tional monitoring program.
The National Agricultural Research Foundation (ELGO), pertaining to
the Ministry of Agriculture (MINAGRIC) advises government and stake-
holders about best practices on agricultural management and water re-
sources within this context. The Greek Biotope and Wetland Centre
(EKBY) is a research-focused institution providing the knowledge base
for policy implementation and environmental conformance. The Insti-
tute of Geology and Mineral Exploration (IGME) carries out monitoring
campaigns and reports on groundwater levels and quality by maintain-
ing the national database for groundwater information. Similarly, the
Aristotle University (AUTH) and the Alexander Technological Educational
Institute of Thessaloniki (TEITHE) carry out research activities in the area.
Thessaloniki's Water Supply and Drainage Company (EYATH) main-
tains abstraction wells for water supply and is the asset owner of the
Waste Water Treatment Plant in the area. The Municipal Water Supply
and Drainage Companies (DEYA) of Chalastra and Chalkidona abstract
groundwater within the Delta area.
Finally, the Public Power Corporation (DEI) which manages the
Aliakmon Dam for hydropower generation plays a key role in the delta's
water budget. After coordination with GOEV and EYATH, DEI meets
their demands during energy generation. Similarly, it is responsible for
releasing the environmental ﬂow of Aliakmon river which in the end
of its course discharges on the west of Axios Delta.
3.3. Stakeholder ties
On the basis of the statutory roles and responsibilities of stake-
holders and institutions discussed, Fig. 3 provides a map to illustrate
their position in the water governance structure, from international
through to local, as well as build a visual representation of their ties.
Fig. 3 breaks down the complex network of the actors discussed.
Government authorities exist in three levels, namely national, regional
and local. Notably, the Special Water Secretariat pertains to the Ministry
of Environment and Energy, while the Water Directorate to the Ministry
of Interior. This organisational structure can create inefﬁciencies in co-
ordination and policy implementations, and the structure is currently
under re-evaluation by the national government (interviewee's infor-
mation). These government institutions aim to coordinate water man-
agement from a large (river catchment) scale to local scale (delta
area). Parallel to this governance scheme, other agents are active includ-
ing not-for-proﬁt advisors, academic institutions, asset operators and
businesses such as the farmer's co-operatives.
Fig. 4 presents interactions among actors from the perspective of the
lead authority for river catchment management planning, namely the
Water Directorate. Egocentric Network Analysis is used to visualise
the presence or absence of interactions between the Water Directorate
and other stakeholders. The Water Directorate is, therefore, the “Ego” in
the egocentric network. Understanding these interactions provides an
insight into where the existing governance is effective (black lines),
and where improvements can be made (dashed lines). The assessment
is based on information from the “Ego” stakeholder as to what consti-
tutes a close interaction and has been corroborated through the inter-
views with the “Alter” (i.e. all other stakeholders). It is worth noting
that not all institutions in the network ought to be linked directly, yet
the absence of some links may cause inefﬁcient coordination with re-
spect to water management.
Communication with international partners is weak (nodes 7a and
7b) affecting thus the effectiveness of water management. The Axios
Management Authority (13), although government-formed, has a
weak institutional role. Its role as a specialist organisation with experi-
ence in the local environment requires further empowerment. Commu-
nication ties are strong with the asset owners of DEI and EYATH,
however, stronger ties are required with municipalities and their
water companies (19–20) who are local water users. Strengthening
these relationships would allow the water budgeting and spatiotempo-
ral distribution and demands to be better planned. Academia is not di-
rectly inﬂuencing water management, and hence not discussed in
detail here.
It is noted that if the same analysis is carried out for other stake-
holders as “Ego”, new ties can be produced; these connections consid-
ered together would create a more complex looped network. For
instance, reclamation of wastewater managed by the water company,
EYATH, can feed GOEV's distribution network (see interventions in
Section 4).
