3.4. Systems dynamics
The current water deﬁcit suggests there is a need for (i) a more
adaptive water allocation policy in the Delta area which will be able to
respond to the different competing demands in variable hydrological
conditions, and (ii) technical solutions that can augment the water bud-
get. Interventions and policies, identiﬁed by the stakeholder experts, are
more effective when their role within a system is better understood. For
this reason, an analysis of Drivers, Pressures, State, Impacts and Re-
sponses and their interactions/feedbacks is carried out.
The frequency of unfavorable climate conditions is expected to in-
crease (Risva et al., 2018) and agricultural activity to intensify, primarily
upstream of the Delta. As seen in Fig. 5, climate change, agricultural ac-
tivity and urban growth give rise to a series of pressures. Reduced rain-
fall and river ﬂow directly affect water balance, while sea level rise can
result in saline intrusion to the coastal aquifer. An increase in evapo-
transpiration is a combined effect of heat-waves, i.e. a climatic effect,
and irrigation practices as part of the agricultural economic activity.
Urban growth results in urban water demands that need to be met by
supply sources, including the Delta's boreholes. These pressures result
