in a State of water stressed Delta that is unable to meet competitive de-
mands between the environment and human activities. Socio-economic
activity losses are experienced through reduced crop productivity for
the farmers (of concern to TOEV) of the Delta, an increase in state-
covered insurance claims, loss in hydropower production for the opera-
tor (of concern to DEI) but also loss of a water supply source (on concern
to EYATH). Environmental impacts include the loss of biodiversity, in-
creased erosion, and seawater intrusion which are subjects under the
remit of the Axios Management Authority.
Although a water deﬁcit will be experienced more frequently, the
current response to this is reactive as the “Drought Emergency Plans”
focuses on emergency/crisis management, i.e. responses do not antici-
pate change and act once impacts are manifested. A series of interven-
tion and policies have been identiﬁed which form a set of proactive
“responses” that are currently not utilized. These are discussed in the
following section. The feedback of the proposed responses within the
system is shown by the corresponding arrows. Some responses are
aimed at mitigating the risk (increasing river ﬂow through transna-
tional cooperation; improving water budget through alternative
schemes, e.g. treated water reclamation), while other responses focus
on adapting to the pressures/impacts (adaptive agriculture; supporting
environmental ﬂows through adaptive water allocation).
While the DPSIR approach presented is conceptual and at a scoping
level, given sufﬁcient information, the analysis can be turned into a
causal loop – “stocks and ﬂows” diagram for fully quantitative system
dynamics (Zare et al., 2019).
4. Discussion
While policy and technical interventions have been identiﬁed via the
DSIR analysis, their successful implementation requires that a set of con-
ditions are met simultaneously. Fig. 6 classiﬁes these conditions at a
high level in three dimensions: (i) Authority, i.e. the political and ad-
ministrative power and control, (ii) Capacity, i.e. the technical expertise
and ﬁnancial ability to carry out an intervention, and (iii) Priority, i.e.
the multilevel alignment of goals and objectives.
If any of these three dimensions/criteria is not satisﬁed, then the pol-
icies and interventions will likely be ineffective. Priorities can differ be-
tween local level (e.g. farmer's unions) and higher levels of government,
and, therefore, the right mix of “top-down” and “bottom up” approaches
need to be identiﬁed during intervention design/implementation.
Even if stakeholders agree on the established priorities, it is critical
they have the necessary authority. It is only then that they can proceed
to intervention implementation. For instance, reuse of reclaimed water
requires a regulatory framework to be in place and a suitable adminis-
trative body supporting the intervention. Similarly, in cases where the
regulatory and political authority support an intervention, but no
funding streams are available or the technical capacity is lacking, then
implementation can suffer.
This simple system of analysis prompts asking questions that are
shaped by the speciﬁc study context. For the Axios case study, examples
of relevant questions were: What are the technical intervention options
available? Are there partnerships in place to enable their implementa-
tion? Is there sufﬁcient funding? Is it technically feasible under the skills
that can be sourced? Are there trade-offs arising from these options? Is
there broad public and stakeholder acceptance? Do beneﬁts accrue to
those who pay?
As with DPSIR, this model requires a dynamic perspective. If the
answer to some of the questions above is negative, then what further
technical/institutional capacity should be developed over time to
successfully introduce a particular intervention? How will an inter-
vention
perform
under
future
climatic
and
socioeconomic
conditions?
In this context, a high-level discussion on these interventions is pro-
vided in this section to explore their feasibility and prompt future dis-
cussion and studies. The interventions respond to existing priorities
for the area which, however, are currently not adequately met: ecolog-
ical health of the Delta, reliable agricultural production, energy genera-
tion at Aliakmon's reservoir and redundancy storage for urban water
supply. As seen in Table 2, these interventions can be assessed in
terms of impact, cost and ease of implementation, i.e. the organisational
capacity and cooperation. Regarding priority, these measures are not
mutually exclusive and the majority can be implemented in the me-
dium term.
An intervention guide based on Table 2 is provided along with quan-
titative information wherever this is possible
1. Improved monitoring is paramount for the integration of environ-
mental information which can be used for robust decision-making.
It is a prerequisite for the implementation of the following interven-
tions and requires multi-stakeholder collaboration with the advisory
bodies having a key role. Monitoring actions include: (i) declaration
of municipal abstractions, (ii) monitoring of the released environ-
mental ﬂow (stations upstream and downstream of the surface
water abstraction point for agriculture). (iii) a denser groundwater
monitoring network in the critical delta area to establish water levels
as well as physico-chemical characteristics (with focus on saltwater
intrusion) at regular periods, and (iv) an integrated view of ground-
water and surface water. Monitoring information can support the
production of integrated surface water – groundwater interaction as-
sessment tools of varying complexity, depending on the resolution
and quality of acquired information and the aimed use. The discrim-
ination of groundwater pollutants is also critical important for
groundwater monitoring (Busico et al., 2018). Vulnerability maps
form outputs of this type of integrated assessment tools that are
based on numerical modelling (Kapetas et al., 2019; Kazakis et al.,
2015, 2019; Patrikaki et al., 2012). Additionally, decision support sys-
tems can contribute to the management of groundwater and surface
water depended systems (Bournaris et al., 2015).
2. The adoption of alternative irrigation approaches or alternative crops
has been encouraged by the advisory organisation (ELGO) and man-
agement authority (TOEV/MINAGRIC) but have not taken further
over the last 10 years. Adapting agricultural practices, due to their so-
cioeconomic complexity, would require a longer-term strategic plan.
It is noted that the current agricultural practice, albeit water-
inefﬁcient, creates a hydraulic barrier through the diffuse recharge
of the ponding practice. Adaptation examples through crop selection
or efﬁcient water management practices, as for example “alternate
wet and dry” (Song et al., 2019; Sriphirom et al., 2019).
Fig. 6. Components (change to Capacity) affecting readiness for and effectiveness of
intervention implementation for improved water allocation.
