L. Kapetas et al. / Science of the Total Environment 695 (2019) 133831
3. Effective water allocation requires the engagement between national
stakeholders sharing information with the transboundary counter-
part, namely North Macedonia, where the source of the river is
found. The absence of a transboundary agreement on riverine ﬂows
increases the uncertainty in the delivery of the environmental ﬂow.
Conventions and soft law as instruments of international water law
serve as tools for water diplomacy (Honkonen and Lipponen,
2018). Several case-study examples in literature show how
transboundary agreements promote equitable and reasonable use
of transboundary waters and prevention of transboundary impact
(de los Cobos, 2018; Honkonen and Lipponen, 2018; McCracken
and Meyer, 2018). To this end, Mahon et al. (2017) have developed
a Transboundary Waters Governance Assessment Framework. More-
over, in contrast to the adjacent catchment of Aliakmon, the absence
of a reservoir upstream does not permit regulating discharge to meet
environmental ﬂow.
4. The application of treated efﬂuents of Thessaloniki's waste water
treatment plant can support the deltaic water budget. This efﬂuent
is a resource of 180,000 m3 on a daily basis which is equivalent to
1/3 of the Delta abstractions from Axios (EYATH, 2012). In practice,
the useful volume is that reclaimed during the irrigation period. Dur-
ing a previous drought (2007–2011), treated wastewater was mixed
with water from Axios River for rice paddy irrigation. Yet, the
approach was abandoned as the drought ended and transfers from
Aliakmon were established. The considerably high impact, average
cost and ease of implementation of the intervention suggest that it
is mature for implementation (see Table 2). Examples of successful
implementation of treated water reclamation in agriculture are
ample in literature. It forms national strategies for water efﬁciency
(Reznik et al., 2017) and is seen as a means for simultaneously
achieving climate adaptation and food security objectives (Sapkota,
2019). Despite the technical feasibility of the treatment processes
(Bailey et al., 2018), in many cases there is low public acceptance of
the technique. For this reason, Saliba et al. (2018) show that aware-
ness campaigns to inﬂuence risk perception are critical for the intro-
duction of reuse-oriented water policy in the Mediterranean region.
Ricart and Rico (2019) recommend that transparency, information
and participation form integral parts of the solution.
5. Currently, farming and urban water supply are the ﬁrst served under
typical and drought conditions respectively. This leaves the environ-
ment at the lowest priority level. Prioritising the environment as a
user can have positive contribution to environmental health during
non-drought conditions. Adaptive water allocation for supporting
continuous environmental ﬂow requires changing the priority user
from the agricultural sector (represented by TOEV) to the River (rep-
resented by the Axios Management Authority). It is evident that
