while the direct cost of the policy/intervention is low and its poten-
tial impact is high, there is signiﬁcant difﬁculty of implementation
due to institutional/political barriers. Presently, authority and prior-
ity are not aligned (see Fig. 6). In a Spanish case-study similar to
Axios, Jorda-Capdevila et al. (2016) developed an integrative model-
ling approach for linking environmental ﬂow management, ecosys-
tem service provision and inter-stakeholder conﬂict which can
serve a guide for the case of Axios. Pereau et al. (2019) demonstrate
through a hydro-economic model how environmental ﬂow can be
satisﬁed in La Mancha, Spain, through an optimal control approach
that can be pursued by the water regulating agencies and inform
management policies.
In summary, the study provided an insight into how strong coordi-
nated multi-level institutional relationships can help design water allo-
cation policies and improve water management practices, in this case
for deltaic ecosystem management. It was shown that for the case
study area of Axios Delta, environmental ﬂows are unmet due to the ab-
sence of ICWM approach in Axios Delta. This happens even during pe-
riods of minor water scarcity, and despite water transfers from a
neighboring catchment. The present allocation method does not take
into consideration the variability in river ﬂow and, by extension, hinders
the delivery of the environmental ﬂow, i.e. the protected area is the low-
est priority recipient.
A series of interviews with stakeholders showed an overall lack of
awareness about the positive value of coordination in water resources
management despite their expertise in their respective work areas.
Analysis of the Water Directorate “Ego” network identiﬁed opportuni-
ties for strengthening speciﬁc relationships that might address these co-
ordination challenges.
Although climate change was recognised as a potential risk by inter-
viewees, no action plan has been developed to prepare for its potential
impacts, including biodiversity loss and coastal aquifer protection. The
current drought mitigation emergency response plan (Ministry of
Environment and Energy, 2014) prioritises water supply to the urban
population, thus further increasing risk to the protected area and its
subsurface water resource. Recovery from such potential damages can
be irreversible or too costly to reverse in the future.
5. Conclusions
This study intended to break down the complexity of the water allo-
cation system and highlight the role that strong institutional interac-
tions can play in enhancing sustainability. The study showed how a
combined stakeholder network analysis and DPSIR approach in a
given hydrological setting can be used to identify suitable water man-
agement interventions to improve water allocation practices. Once in-
terventions
are
identiﬁed,
their
impact
and
maturity
for
implementation can be established by examining whether authority,
priority and capacity converge.
While the effectiveness of a certain intervention might be evident,
its implementation might not necessarily be supported. This can hap-
pen as decision-making is inﬂuenced by individual (or institution)
values that are not captured (Daron and Colenbrander, 2015). The
stakeholders' ability to set the agenda and procedure, as well as process
equity, are controlling factors in developing a shared vision (Koontz,
2014). This work raises awareness on systems interactions and stake-
holders can acquire a “cognitive knowledge gain” which contributes
to strengthening their ties and to their ability to co-design
interventions.
