A B S T R A C T   
The last decade has witnessed rapid progress in energy cooperation between the countries of the BBIN 
(Bangladesh, Bhutan, India and Nepal) sub-region. Cooperation has been bilateral, with each of the countries 
entering into separate energy development and trade agreement with India, broadly similar to the water sector 
where national governments engage bilaterally on transboundary cooperation and dispute resolution. A more 
recent wave of electrical grid interconnections and hydro-energy cooperation has emerged with governments 
increasingly shifting from bilateral to multilateral energy-sharing agreements. This trend holds considerable 
potential for regional transboundary water governance. Based on documentary and media analysis along with 
interviews of key BBIN policy-makers, we identify and examine in this paper four factors for future progress: 1) 
technical cooperation can be extended to information-sharing for policies and institutions to regulate and 
manage water resources; 2) India must seize the opportunities and benefits of enhanced regional leadership in 
the region; 3) simultaneous informal discussion and diplomatic negotiation of water, energy and their nexus can 
provide BBIN countries the opportunity to highlight potential gains of cooperation and interstate inter­
dependencies; and 4) regional cooperation can give a strong impetus to nations for advancing structural reforms, 
building institutions and capacity, developing a shared knowledge base, bridging infrastructural gaps, attracting 
private sector participation, and addressing poverty alleviation goals including job creation.   
1. Introduction 
In recent years, the development of regional cooperation among 
Bangladesh, Bhutan, India and Nepal (the BBIN countries) has assumed 
greater significance as governments attempt to deal with common so­
cial, economic and environmental problems. Sub-regional cooperation 
within the SAARC (South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation) 
framework is relatively new, with the idea being mooted at SAARC 
Summits in Male and Colombo (SAARC 1997 and 1998). Furthermore, 
SAARC’s failure to adopt important resolutions, such as agreement of 
land transport during the Kathmandu Summit in 2014 led the BBIN 
countries to seek cooperation in other areas like water and energy 
(Haran, 2018). As seen in Fig. 1, the BBIN countries share several 
transboundary rivers that are part of the Ganga-Brahmaputra-Meghna 
(GBM) basin (Mukherji et al., 2018), home to over 10% of the world’s 
population. A large portion of people living in this region face chronic 
energy shortages, erratic and unreliable water supply of questionable 
quality, and rising impacts of climate-change disasters such as floods 
and droughts and hence, the very real and imminent need for co­
operation (Scott et al., 2019). 
Energy-water trade-offs, synergies and interdependencies1 are in­
timately tied to the potential of finding comprehensive solutions that 
are founded on economies of scope (Farid et al., 2016). Yet, water co­
operation and energy cooperation in the BBIN region have followed 
different trajectories, primarily due to a traditional silo-ed approach in 
development that focusses on water, energy and food as individual 
sectors, rather than taking a nexus approach to resource assessment and 
governance (Rasul & Sharma, 2016; Keskinen et al., 2016). Water co­
operation in South Asia dates back to colonial and pre-colonial times 
when the countries and territories shared the waters of the Ganga and 
