Brahmaputra – but these linkages were abruptly disrupted after Parti­
tion of India in 1947. Since then, there have been a number of in­
itiatives that have failed at forging successful bilateral or multilateral 
water cooperation in the Ganga-Brahmaputra-Meghna (GBM) river 
basins. As compared to the water sector, the history of regional energy 
cooperation is still embryonic, with the idea taking concrete shape only 
from the early 2000s (Lama, 2003; SARI/E, 2000). Variants include the 
Bay of Bengal region (Devare, 2008), the Bangladesh-India-Bhutan grid 
(Bisht, 2012a), and the BBIN countries (a regional grouping first named 
as such in the field of public health, viz, Mittal et al., 2004) – the focus 
of the present paper. The U.S. Agency for International Development 
(USAID) initiated the South Asia Regional Initiative for Energy In­
tegration (SARI/EI) program in 2000, to address cross-border energy 
trade, energy market formation, and clean energy development in and 
among Afghanistan, Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, The Maldives, Nepal, 
Pakistan and Sri Lanka (SARI/EI, n.d.). The energy trade and market 
components have had greater success and are of particular salience for 
the BBIN countries, as we examine in this paper. 
Cooperation in the form of official water treaties has often produced 
outcomes that have led to greater controversy, anxieties and political 
disputes between the countries (Hanasz, 2014; Ranjan, 2016; Swain, 
2018). Against this chequered backdrop, the BBIN countries have em­
barked on energy cooperation which has intensified over the last 
decade through several commercial and diplomatic interventions. Hy­
dropower development has been central to the discussions on regional 
energy trade, given the tremendous combined hydropower potential, 
particularly of Himalayan rivers (see Table 1 for all commercial energy 
resources of BBIN countries). Several bilateral energy cooperation 
agreements have been signed successfully. In late 2019, discussions of 
Nepal and Bangladesh trading power through India’s transmission 
network successfully concluded with the finalizing of the Power 
Purchase Agreement (PPA) between the Bangladesh government and 
GMR, the Indian private developer of the 900-MW Upper Karnali Hydro 
Electric Project in Western Nepal (The Himalayan Times., 2019). This 
has led to mounting optimism that prospects for more multilateral en­
ergy projects may help identify factors that influence cooperation and 
the lessons can be generalised to other sectors such as water that lack 
joint settlement and cooperation. 
Various studies in the past have documented and assessed status and 
future prospects for transboundary water cooperation (Swain, 1993;  
Verghese & Iyer, 1994; Uprety & Salman, 1999; Salman & Uprety, 2002;  
Rasul, 2014). A similar breadth of literature exists on energy trading in 
South Asia (Wijayatunga & Fernando, 2013; Huda & McDonald, 2016;  
Haran, 2018; Nag, 2019). The large majority of scholars have laid 
emphasis on the opportunities for, and challenges obstructing regional 
cooperation; however, to our knowledge, none of the previous studies 
have comparatively reviewed the trajectories of water and energy co­
operation agreements in the BBIN region. Water and energy coopera­
tion are often impacted by similar impediments, notably bureaucratic 
inefficiency, lack of transparency, infrastructural bottlenecks, lack of 
finance (which adversely impacts institutional development), political 
challenges, and lack of trust (Nicol et al., 2002; Chanda, 2009; Gippner, 
2010; Araral & Wang, 2013). And yet, the experience of BBIN shows 
that the cooperation trajectories for water and energy have been starkly 
different. The objective of this paper is to analyse the similarities and 
differences in water and energy cooperation trajectories and distil les­
sons for future cooperation. 
We draw on a review of scholarly literature, project reports, news­
paper articles, official documents, as well as a case-example of Nepal, 
based on interviews held between September 2019 and November 2019 
in Kathmandu and in February 2020 in New Delhi (see list of institu­
tions in Appendix A) to examine the major shifts in policies, governance 
and institutional structures, and their implications for energy and water 
cooperation. Respondents included government officials, private hy­
dropower producers, policy researchers, representatives of regional 
institutions and multilateral development banks who were recruited 
through the snowballing sampling technique given the restricted access 
to elite policymakers and executives (Mendez, 2020). The fieldwork 
also includes notes from the two-day Power Summit ‘19 conference held 
