We argue that bilateral and multilateral negotiations over energy – 
in relation to both generation and trading – have achieved some level of 
success in the BBIN region, as visible through multiple cross-border 
investments in hydropower projects and related infrastructure such as 
transmission lines. Following the experience of the energy sector, an 
emphasis on mutual interests and benefits can generate positive out­
comes in transboundary water cooperation. To demonstrate this, we 
begin by introducing the political context, and existing water and en­
ergy cooperation arrangements among the BBIN countries, followed by 
a comparative analysis of the common attributes and differences be­
tween water and energy cooperation. The conclusion section draws 
from an assessment of the regional experience in energy cooperation 
and offers lessons for forging regional water cooperative agreements to 
forestall potential water conflicts. 
2. Political relations between BBIN countries 
Resource conflict between countries can have broader political and 
geographical implications. Conversely, hostile interstate relations have 
the potential to stall regional economic integration. Rivers are closely 
linked to regional politics, owning to their potential role in the national 
economy through economic and energy development opportunities. 
Distinct historical and political conditions within a region or basin are 
important determinants of interstate water relations (Giordano et al., 
2002). Therefore, before undertaking sector-specific analysis to un­
derstand what determines the success of cooperation efforts in water 
and energy, we present a general overview of political relations be­
tween individual countries in the BBIN region (Table 2). China is in­
cluded in this analysis as a result of its position as a riparian country in 
the GBM basin and its growing economic and geopolitical influence in 
the region (Chakma, 2019). 
Interstate relations in the BBIN region are mostly positive, barring 
some tensions between India and its neighbours. India prefers to deal 
with these countries in a bilateral, ad-hoc manner, instead of using a 
comprehensive negotiation framework which could be more conducive 
to regional integration and political bargaining by smaller countries 
(Muni, 2003; Behuria et al., 2012; Vij et al., 2020). Furthermore, the 
oft-cited Sino-India rivalry, inherent power differentials, and unsettled 
political issues such as border disputes and differences over sharing 
rivers have failed to dispel fears of Indian dominance and subjugation 
of the interests and concerns of its smaller neighbours (Muni, 2003). 
3. Water Cooperation 
The eastern Himalayan transboundary river system is primarily di­
vided into two river basins – the Ganga (shared between Nepal, India, 
and Bangladesh, with minor headwaters areas in China) and the 
Brahmaputra (shared between China, India, Bhutan and Bangladesh). 
We briefly discuss the current status of water cooperation between ri­
parian countries, which has mostly been bilateral with India at the 
center of all cooperation. 
3.1. India-Bhutan water cooperation 
India and Bhutan are co-riparian states of the GBM basin comprising 
three major Himalayan rivers that play a significant role in shaping the 
economy, culture and social lives of the people of the region. Bhutan’s 
long-standing political relationship with India and growing socio-eco­
nomic developmental needs, largely met through Indian loans and grants, 
have been critical factors guiding bilateral cooperation between these two 
neighbours. Five key bilateral agreements provide the overall framework 
for cooperation. The first two are friendship treaties in 1949, amended in 
2007, that provides the basis for free trade and economic cooperation 
