a The India-Bhutan political arrangement is complex and remains ambiguous. Indiaʼs influence in Bhutan was marked by the historic India-Bhutan Friendship Treaty (1949) which was amended in 2007, just before 
Bhutan adopted a constitutional democratic system of governance. The amended clause gave Bhutan full autonomy in handling its defence and foreign policy while being cognizant of India’s strategic interests in the 
region (Tobgye, 2019; Saklani & Tortajada, 2019). The Doklam crisis in 2017 between Bhutan and China highlighted the complexity of the India-Bhutan relations when the Indian military crossed over to Bhutanese 
territory to defend the disputed area, claiming to act on Thimpu’s behalf.  
