24
between the two countries. Other agreements include cooperation on 
hydroelectric power (2006), increasing the export of electricity from 
Bhutan to India from 5,000 MWh to 10,000 MWh by 2020 (Protocol 
Agreement, 2008), and developing Joint Venture Hydropower Projects 
through the public sector undertakings of the two Governments (Inter- 
Governmental Agreement, 2014). 
While no water-specific bilateral treaty exists between India and 
Bhutan, all issues of bilateral interest are resolved following the prin­
ciples of the Treaty of Friendship and Cooperation (1949 and its 2007 
amendment) which acts as the basic framework for cooperation. 
However, India’s strategic interests in the region and high degree of 
political influence in Bhutan reinforce the extreme power asymmetries 
between the two countries, which sometimes reflects in bilateral ne­
gotiations (Sauvagerd, 2018). 
The India-Bhutan bilateral cooperation in water began in 1955 with 
the establishment of numerous rain gauges and river flow stations in 
Bhutan sponsored by the Indian Ministry of External Affairs for the 
purpose of improving flood warning measures (Chadha & Pandya, 
2019). At the bilateral level, schemes were initiated in 19552 and 19793 
for the establishment of hydro-meteorological and flood forecasting 
networks on rivers common to India and Bhutan (Kibler et al., 2014). 
Data generated from this network is used by India to forecast its floods 
(Ministry of Jal Shakti., n.d.) and serves as an exemplary case of in­
formation and resource-sharing between developing countries (Varis 
et al., 2008). A Joint Group of Experts (JGE) on Flood Management has 
also been meeting almost annually since 2004 to discuss the issue of 
recurring floods and erosion in the southern foothills of Bhutan and 
adjoining plains in India and to assess mutually acceptable remedial 
measures (Table 3 and 4). 
Bhutan’s approach to water is guided by its National Water Policy, 
first drafted in 2002 and updated in 2007. All transboundary water 
issues are dealt with in accordance with international laws and con­
ventions to which Bhutan is a signatory. Bhutan recognizes the need for 
national, regional and global cooperation in information sharing and 
exchange, appropriate technology in water resources development and 
management, flood warning, and disaster management. Management of 
water resources in Bhutan is handled at the river basin level, similar to 
the Indian approach where the basin is treated as the basic hydrologic 
unit (Ministry of Water Resources., 2012). 
3.2. India-Bangladesh water cooperation 
India and Bangladesh (known as East Pakistan until 1971) share 54 
transboundary rivers and have a long history of cooperation, predating 
independence from British colonial rule. As a lower riparian state, 
Bangladesh negotiated with India to sign the 1996 India-Bangladesh 
Treaty on Sharing of the Ganges Waters, a thirty-year treaty valid until 
2026 which is hailed as a successful foreign policy initiative and is 
aimed at enhancing cooperative water allocation between the two 
neighbours. On the ground, the treaty has achieved some level of suc­
cess in sharing water in the lean season (Pandey, 2014; Haran, 2018), 
although other provisions such as augmenting water availability in the 
Ganga at Farakka (where the Hoogly distributary to Kolkata emanates) 
remain unfulfilled. The focus of the treaty remains solely on water 
sharing, instead of taking a holistic view on the value and uses of the 
river (Hanasz, 2015). The framework agreement for greater 
cooperation in water management signed by the prime ministers of 
India and Bangladesh in 2011 remains largely on paper (Rashid, 2012). 
Disagreements have also emerged in relation to India’s resistance to­
wards information-sharing and concerns over ecological impacts in 
Bangladesh due to upstream water withdrawals (Prasai & Surie, 2013;  
Kawser & Samad, 2016; Rahman & Rahaman, 2018). Despite a long 
history of contestation, negotiation and institutional arrangements such 
as the Joint River Commission (formed in 1972), bilateral dialogues 
have been relegated to diplomatic agreements without substantive 
changes in infrastructure and operations on the ground. 
In recent times, political and diplomatic efforts have sought to re­
solve pending issues on sharing of the Teesta and Feni rivers. While 
some success for the Feni has been achieved through the recent signing 
of an MoU between India and Bangladesh (Marathe, 2019), the Teesta 
remains a distant ambition due to objections raised in the Indian state 
of West Bengal bordering Bangladesh. Unlike these water-sharing 
challenges, India and Bangladesh have managed to successfully co­
operate on inland navigation and trade connectivity of the Brahmaputra 
under the Protocol on Inland Water Transit and Trade, 2015, although 
the tremendous potential to jointly develop river ways to enhance 
economic and people-to-people links remains severely limited due to 
extreme political sensitivity around sharing river water. 
3.3. India-Nepal water cooperation 
Water from the Himalayan mountain ranges in Nepal flowing into 
the Ganga in India has connected the two countries from time im­
memorial, not just physically but also socio-culturally. Many Hindu 
religious sites, such as Pashupatinath and Devighat in Nepal and 
Rishikesh and Haridwar in India, located along the Ganga and its tri­
butaries, are tirthas (auspicious pilgrimage destinations) with the ages- 
old free flow of people and commerce between the countries (Dhungel 
and Pun, 2009; Scott, 1989). However, formal water cooperation be­
tween the two countries started only in 1874. The current framework 
for water cooperation is based on three treaties signed on major rivers 
(Mahakali, Gandaki and Koshi) that flow from Nepal to India and are 
primarily centred on the construction of barrages in Nepal to divert 
water for irrigation, electricity generation, and flood control. All three 
treaties specify the roles of countries in water benefits along with vo­
lumes of water that each country will utilize as well as the construction 
of infrastructure (i.e. canals and hydropower plants). While all the 
barrages mentioned in the treaties have already been constructed, the 
Pancheshwar multipurpose project is the only project yet to be devel­
oped, pending details to be mutually agreed between the two countries. 
Nepal’s internal water governance is based on its Water Resource 
Strategy of 2002 that aims to utilize water resources in order to con­
tribute to the broader national goal of improving quality of life of 
Nepalese people. The actions for this strategy was followed up by 
National Water Plan of 2005 that laid out the short term, middle-term 
and long-term plan for water sector development. Nepal’s energy 
sector, is primarily guided by the Hydropower Development Policy of 
1992, which was updated in 2001 but has had limited impact due to the 
absence of any legal backing. With the promulgation of the new 
Constitution in 2016, procedures are ongoing for revising the National 
Water Resource Strategy and Electricity Act based on the principles 
enshrined in the Constitution to facilitate the development of genera­
tion, transmission and trade of hydro electricity in Nepal. 
3.4. India-China water cooperation 
Diplomatic negotiations between India and China on the 
Brahmaputra have resulted in marginal gains (Feng et al., 2019). At the 
bilateral level, China and India have renewed a memorandum of 
agreement in 2018 (Ghosh et al., 2020), after its expiry in 2007, to 
strengthen communication and strategic trust by sharing hydrological 
information during the flood season on the Yarlung Zangbo/ 
