Brahmaputra River by China to India (Ranjan, 2013; Lovelle, 2016;  
Barua et al., 2019). However, implementation of the agreement remains 
challenging as information sharing is limited to the flood season. The 
scope of agreement remains narrow, addressing only localized aspects 
of the broad issue of integrated water management, and does not in­
clude dispute-resolution mechanisms, even for data sharing (Barua 
et al., 2019; Ghosh et al., 2020). In 2017, China refused to share its data 
with India leading to accusations that the damage from Assam floods 
could have been lowered had China abided by its bilateral commitment 
(Ghosh, 2017). Despite the existence of an Expert Level Mechanism 
(ELM) on Trans-Border Rivers between the two countries, there remains 
much to be desired with respect to bilateral cooperation on water is­
sues. 
3.5. Trajectory of water cooperation in BBIN: From bilateral to 
multilateral? 
While a move towards multilateral agreements on water coopera­
tion has yet to gain momentum, current signs are promising, e.g., the 
formation of a trilateral cooperation mechanism for the Ganga basin 
among Nepal, India and Bangladesh (NIB), as well as in the 
Brahmaputra basin among Bangladesh, India and Bhutan (BIB) 
(Pattanaik, 2016). Water management in the region continues to be 
treated in a narrow and piecemeal manner, with an almost-exclusive 
focus on existing treaties (whether these are functional or not) and on 
water/river sharing rather than sharing of other benefits of and from 
the river (Sadoff & Grey, 2002). After decades-long negotiations, even if 
bilateral treaties are signed, commitments remain unfulfilled due to the 
actual or perceived difference in distribution of gains among riparian 
states (Sadoff & Grey, 2002), for instance, in the case of the Mahakali 
treaty between India and Nepal. The perception of fairness is an im­
portant criteria in the fulfilment of treaty objectives. As a result, there is 
little incentive for governments to invest resources and energies in the 
shared management of rivers. 
Some discussions are pushing the envelope for water cooperation 
beyond mere water sharing – through discussions around inland and 
coastal navigation (Padmaja, 2016). Another encouraging aspect of 
water cooperation in the region is the active involvement of civil so­
ciety in transboundary discourses – through projects such as the Brah­
maputra Dialogue initially led by SACIWaters (supported by the Asia 
Foundation) and from 2014, incorporated into the World Bank’s South 
Asia Water Initiative (SAWI) project, or the Transboundary Rivers of 
South Asia (TROSA), a Swedish-funded project focusing on trans­
boundary dialogue and capacity building around disasters in the GBM 
countries through multi-stakeholder engagement (Henshaw, 2018;  
Barua et al., 2019; SAWI, 2019). Yet, viewing water in isolation and as a 
zero-sum game remains the dominant paradigm; country-specific ap­
proaches result in different national-level policies and plans for water 
management, leading to a trust deficit which makes multilateral co­
operation over water seem like an elusive goal. Most water negotiations 
are limited to Track 1 (formal) diplomacy involving traditional state 
actors such as government officials and policymakers which makes 
transboundary interactions a complex, state-driven process with an 
inherently political nature (Barua et al., 2019). 
