3.6. The role of China 
Besides being an upper riparian country in the Brahmaputra river 
basin, China has been expanding its investment portfolio, especially for 
hydropower development around the world. After its rapid in­
dustrialization limited natural resources to exploit within China, it 
adopted a ‘going out strategy’ (Urban et al., 2013) by financing hy­
dropower projects in other countries. Though Chinese investments have 
grown rapidly in places like the Greater Mekong sub-region (Matthews 
& Motta, 2015; Urban et al., 2013), Africa (Hensengerth, 2017), and 
South America (Gerlak, et al., 2020), among others, its presence in the 
GBM countries is limited mostly to Nepal, which has seen some in­
creasing investment in hydropower generation. There is a growing 
pressure from the Bhutanese private sector, including the Bhutan 
Chambers of Commerce, on the Government of Bhutan to establish 
economic relations with China (Bisht, 2012b; Brassard, 2012), India’s 
hegemonic role in Bhutan’s hydropower development has restricted the 
entry of Chinese investment (France-Presse, 2018; Kumar, 2019;  
Mishra, 2019). 
Though Chinese investments have emerged as an alternative to 
multilateral banks, they have been criticized around the world for 
disregarding global social and environmental norms in hydropower 
development. In South America, hydropower projects with Chinese 
investment have experienced tensions in the form of protests from civil 
society and environmental groups due to absence of regionally-shared 
standards for environmental assessments and conflict mitigation (Casey 
& Krauss, 2018; Carvalho, 2019; Gerlak et al., 2020; Wu, 2020). Simi­
larly, in the Greater Mekong sub-region, Chinese investments in hy­
dropower have created significant negative impacts on livelihood, food 
security and the environment (Biba, 2012, 2018; Matthews & Motta, 
2015; Middleton & Allouche, 2016). However, in Nepal, due to the 
early stage of its hydropower development, Chinese investments have 
been considered a major financial source to advance projects for na­
tional development, described as “a handshake across the Himalayas” 
by the Nepalese elites (Murton et al., 2016). 
It is believed that part of what is driving Indian investments (in­
cluding grants, loans and commercial financing for infrastructural 
projects) in the neighbourhood is its geostrategic rivalry and economic 
competition with China (Barua, 2018; Stephens, 2019). A peripheral 
player in South Asian regional politics for many years, China has in­
creasingly assumed a position of importance as a trade and investment 
partner in the last two decades (Bhandari & Jindal, 2018; Singdel, 2018;  
Anwar, 2019). While the democratic character of Indian polity and 
close cultural and historical ties with the neighbours has been an at­
tractive feature of its engagement, China’s strong economic might and 
relative non-interference in domestic politics of its partners (although a 
lack of transparency in intent, processes and approach in Chinese en­
gagement raises considerable doubt and suspicion) has helped expand 
its footprint in Nepal and Bangladesh. Chinese investment supports the 
region’s massive infrastructure and connectivity requirements while 
balancing India’s dominating position in the region. 
4. Energy Cooperation 
There is great complementarity in seasonal energy demand and 
supply in the BBIN region that justifies regional cooperation. High 
variability in snow and monsoon-fed rivers in Nepal and Bhutan results 
in energy production that surpasses domestic demand in summer, 
which is also the most energy-thirsty time for India and Bangladesh 
