" high demands for irrigation and domestic cooling. Conversely, 
winter energy demand in Nepal and Bhutan surpasses domestic energy 
supply due to low availability of water for hydropower generation and 
higher need for heating and lighting, a gap that can be met through 
energy exports from India (Vaidya et al., 2019). Increasing seasonal 
variability under climate change resulting in reductions in dry-season 
flows of Himalayan rivers (Mukherji et al., 2018) is bound to increase 
energy interdependencies in the future. 
BBIN inter-country power trade in 2017 was estimated to be about 
2300 MW (less than 1% of the total installed capacity of 342 gigawatts 
(GW)) (South Asia Regional Initiative for Energy Integration (SARI/EI, 
2018), even though evidence clearly points towards the potentially 
substantial benefits that could be reaped through cross-border power 
trade. According to Toman & Timilsina (2016), cost savings of about $9 
billion per year could be generated as a result of increased regional 
electricity integration and trade with benefits reaching as high $100 
billion (at a social discount rate of 5%) between 2015 and 2040. Im­
ports of hydropower would also help India offset its carbon emissions 
by replacing some part of its domestic demand currently met through 
fossil fuels. Therefore, there is a strong rationale for expanded elec­
tricity cooperation and trade from both an economic and environmental 
standpoint. 
Recent steps in power sector cooperation (summarized in Table 4 
and explained in detail below) indicate that mutual interests can serve 
as a basis for continued progress. The stakes are especially high for 
Nepal and Bhutan, with numerous major hydropower projects in var­
ious stages of planning and implementation. An integrated BBIN-wide 
trade scenario, compared to separate bilateral trades, as reported in  
SARI/EI (2018) indicate that India’s net electricity import would in­
crease, making it more reliant on Nepal and Bhutan. Under this sce­
nario, Nepal’s exports to India would significantly increase in aggregate 
terms. Thus, the centrepiece of future BBIN energy trades hinges on the 
Nepal-India relationship within the regional geopolitical mix. 
4.1. India-Bhutan energy cooperation 
Given Bhutan’s status as an upper riparian country, the water re­
sources of the GBM basin serve as a potential source of hydropower 
development, both for domestic use as well as revenue generation 
through cross-border trading. Bhutan’s Water Policy clearly recognizes 
the need to harness its 30,000 MW hydropower potential, of which 
23,760 MW has been declared as economically feasible. India, with its 
lower riparian status and rapidly expanding energy needs, serves as an 
optimal market for Bhutan’s energy which is in effect subsidized due to 
a tariff that is less than half the current average rate within India, a 
sticky issue that often creates disaffection for India in Bhutan (Lamsang, 
2017; Tortajada & Saklani, 2018). 
The Indo-Bhutan energy cooperation began in 1987-88 with the 
completion of the 336 MW Chhukha hydropower project wholly fi­
nanced by India. This was followed by the 60 MW Kurichhu hydro­
electric plant (built on the Chhukha model) and the 1020 MW Tala 
hydroelectric plant (built on 60% grant and 40% loan by India). In 
2007, bilateral energy cooperation between India and Bhutan entered a 
new phase with Bhutan seeking to invite private and other foreign in­
vestment. However, in 2009, India and Bhutan agreed to jointly pursue 
new power generation targets by 2020 (the deadline has been post­
poned due to project delays in hydropower construction), effectively 
forestalling participation by other investors. The current phase of bi­
lateral energy cooperation also significantly modifies some of the ear­
lier financial provisions, with India moving away from a 60:40 debt to 
equity model to a 70:30 ratio for all hydropower projects under the 
2009 agreement barring the Punatsangchu-I, which has raised ques­
tions in Bhutan over the rapid hydro-power investment through heavy 
borrowing (Premkumar, 2017; Tortajada & Saklani, 2018). 
In 2019, India and Bhutan commissioned their fourth joint project, 
the 720 MW Mangdechu project (on a 30% grant and 70% loan) and are 
awaiting the completion of two more projects in the pipeline – 
1200 MW Punatsangchu-I and 1020 MW Punatsangchu-II. The con­
struction of four more projects (the 600 MW Kholongchhu, 180 MW 
Bunakha, 570 MW Wangchu and 770 MW Chamkarchu) under a Joint 
Venture (JV) model has been under discussion. In the current period, 
hydropower has been estimated to account for half of Bhutan’s GDP and 
three-quarters of government revenues, although most of the know-how 
