and much of the labour for project construction continues to be im­
ported from India. Bhutan’s increasing domestic demand for power 
coupled with climate change impacts on hydropower generation and 
mounting evidence of social and environmental costs (Ahlers et al., 
2015) could limit future power exports in the region and diminish the 
importance of hydropower as a revenue earner for the country (Bisht, 
2012a; Seth, 2015). However, given Bhutan’s narrow industrial base, 
low economic diversification, and low value and productivity of the 
Micro Small and Medium (MSME) sector (Wangchuk, 2017), it is likely 
that hydropower will remain a key driver for economic growth for 
Bhutan and a central pillar for bilateral cooperation between India and 
Bhutan, at least in the near future (Dorji, 2015). 
4.2. India-Bangladesh energy cooperation 
In the case of India and Bangladesh, the power sector has often been 
cited as the hallmark of regional cooperation (The Independent., 2019). 
The first interconnection between the two countries began in 2013 with 
the construction of a high voltage DC (direct current) line to facilitate 
transmission of electricity from India to power-deficient Bangladesh. 
Today, Bangladesh imports 1160 MWh of energy from India. New in­
terconnections that are under implementation could raise electricity 
transfers from India to Bangladesh by up to 1540 MW h (Ministry of 
Power., n.d.) Agreements have been signed for generation, supply and/ 
or financing of more than 3600 MWh electricity between Indian and 
Bangladeshi public/private companies (Ministry of External Affairs., 
2019). The setting up of a joint venture between Bangladesh and India 
for the 1320 MW coal-fired power plant in Khulna, Bangladesh and the 
130-kilometre India-Bangladesh Friendship diesel pipeline project 
supported with Indian grant assistance (The Hindu, 2018; Ministry of 
External Affairs., 2019) are meant to diversify energy cooperation. 
Recently, India and Bangladesh have welcomed the possibility of a 
‘power corridor’ that would allow India to use Bangladeshi territory to 
connect India’s north-eastern states to its central territory lying to the 
west of Bangladesh. India has also, in principle, agreed to allow Nepal 
and Bangladesh to use Indian transmission lines for the export of 
500 MWh of energy. 
4.3. Nepal-India energy cooperation 
The Koshi project (built in 1954) laid the foundation for Nepal and 
India’s energy exchange. Since then, there have been numerous 
agreements between the two countries such as the umbrella Electric 
Power Trade Agreement (EPTA) in 1996, Electricity Power Trade, 
Cross-border Transmission Interconnection and Grid Connectivity 
agreement in 2014 and several Ministerial, Secretary and Joint 
Secretary level meetings on bilateral issues of water and energy. 
Currently, there are 21 power exchange points through 11 kV, 33 kV, 
and 132 kV transmission lines between Nepal and India (Rahman et al., 
2012). In addition, completion of 400 kV Muzaffarpur (India)–Dhalk­
ebar (Nepal) double circuit transmission line in 2018 has facilitated 
transfer of high volume of electricity between two countries. The 
modality of construction of a second 400 kV transmission line between 
Gorakhpur (India) – New Butwal (Nepal) is being finalized. 
Nepal’s hydropower policy prioritizes the fulfillment of domestic 
consumption demands of electricity and envisions export of excess 
energy to neighbouring countries. In 2018-19, Nepal was still a net 
energy importer with the total annual consumption of electricity as 
6394.38 GW h; of this, domestic generation contributed 62.75% of the 
energy and the remaining 37.25% was imported from India. As the 209 
hydropower plants under construction, totalling 7,952 MW4 of installed 
capacity and additional projects, comes into operation, Nepal hopes to 
export its electricity to India and Bangladesh in the future. The 900 MW 
Arun 3 hydropower project was awarded to Sutlej Jal Vidyut Nigam (an 
Indian public sector company) through competitive international bid­
ding. The company has started constructing the hydropower plant to 
evacuate generated electricity to India through a dedicated transmis­
sion line, after giving a portion of the pledged free electricity (21.9% or 
197 MW) to Nepal. Similarly, the 900 MW Upper Karnali Hydropower 
project, promoted by GMR (an Indian private company), is planning to 
generate electricity in Nepal and sell it to Bangladesh via India. An 
electricity tariff agreement has already been reached between GMR and 
the Government of Bangladesh. In addition to cooperation in hydro­
power, Nepal and India entered a landmark deal in 2019 facilitating 
cross-border petroleum product trading from Motihari in Bihar to 
Amlekhgunj in Nepal through the 69 km-long pipeline (The Hindu 
Business Line., 2019). 
4.4. Nepal-Bangladesh energy cooperation 
After developing energy cooperation agreements with India and 
China, Nepal turned towards Bangladesh to jointly harness Nepal’s 
tremendous hydropower potential to serve the needs of an energy- 
hungry Bangladesh, which primarily relies on natural gas (53.7% of its 
fuel generation mix) followed by heavy fuel oil (PWC, 2018). In De­
cember 2018, the first meeting of the Joint Steering Committee on 
Bangladesh-Nepal Cooperation in the field of Power Sector (JSC) was 
convened to discuss the possibility of power trading, investment in 
hydropower development, construction of cross border interconnection, 
and scaling up of renewable energy generation and usage through co­
operation in the field of solar home systems (MOEWRI, 2018). Another 
institutional set-up known as the Joint Working Group on Bangladesh- 
Nepal Cooperation in the field of Power Sector (JWG) deliberated over 
whether the power trading mechanism may be direct between entities 
in Nepal and Bangladesh or through Indian entities based on back-to- 
back power purchase agreements (MOEWRI, 2019). Given the intrinsic 
dependence on Indian transmission facilities for power trading, two 
policies drafted by India are critical for Nepal-Bangladesh energy co­
operation: (1) the 2018 Guidelines for Import/Export (Cross Border) of 
Electricity issued by the Indian Ministry of Power, and (2) the 2019 
Cross Border Trade of Electricity Regulations issued by the Central 
Electricity Regulatory Commission of India. The finalization of a power 
tariff agreement for the import of 500 MWh hydropower from the 
900 MW Upper Karnali hydropower project in Nepal is the first concrete 
step aimed at fulfilling Bangladesh’s goal of importing 9000 MWh of 
power from Nepal in 10 years (Begum, 2019). The JWG also discussed 
the possibility of Bangladeshi officials sharing experiences from the 
power sector with their Nepalese counterparts through capacity 
building programmes on energy efficiency, conservation and reforms 
(MOEWRI, 2019). 
5. Energy and water cooperation: A comparative view 
Transboundary water cooperation is beset by challenges that are 
historical, institutional, political, and hydrologic in nature. Water re­
sources have to be managed across the region during the four to five 
months of monsoon season due to problems of flooding, landslide, se­
dimentation, soil erosion, cloud outburst floods, and glacial lake out­
burst flood (GLOF) in Nepal, Bhutan and India, and inundation in the 
southern plains of Nepal, northern India, and Bangladesh. Similarly, the 
dry season is characterized by water shortages for irrigation, drinking 
water, and generation of hydroelectricity across the BBIN countries, 
with severe contamination of ground water, primarily arsenic con­
tamination in Bangladesh. 
The opportunities for water cooperation are considerable, both in 
terms of outcomes, such as improved water management practices, and 
as a result of the process itself, leading to enhanced trust between 
countries (UNECE, 2015). However, shared river basins can also give 
