rise to strife due to conflicting demands and management preferences 
(Sadoff & Grey, 2002). In some cases, cooperative efforts have not re­
sulted in satisfactory outcomes. For example, the Koshi, Gandak and 
Mahakali treaties between India and Nepal have been diplomatic in­
itiatives purely at the national political level, but they remain wholly 
unsatisfactory to basin and local level stakeholders. Similarly, the 
Ganges Treaty between India and Bangladesh continues to pose major 
implementation hurdles. 
In the energy sector, there are many good examples of cooperation 
reaping mutual benefits. Although South Asia lags behind many regions 
such as Southern Africa, Central America, and Nordic countries in intra- 
regional electricity cooperation and trading (Vaidya et al., 2019), there 
are a considerable number of electricity interconnections, and power 
generation and trading projects such as hydropower plants, petroleum 
transport and thermal power plants, that are being jointly developed by 
governments in the BBIN region. Additionally, new initiatives such as 
‘energy banking’ aimed at addressing seasonal variability in energy 
production and energy consumption between India and Nepal, with a 
possibility of being replicated in other countries, shows that govern­
ments recognize energy interdependencies and are motivated to explore 
new possibilities in energy trade. 
In the following part of the paper, we discuss the differences and 
similarities between negotiations and outcomes pertaining to energy 
and water cooperation based on four key factors: domestic politics and 
diplomacy; India’s hegemonic role; institutional frameworks for co­
operation; and perceptions. 
5.1. Domestic politics and diplomatic approach 
Contemporary academic research highlights the primacy of politics 
in constraining shared development of resources between countries in 
South Asia (Iyer and Iyer, 2003; Biswas, 2011; Huda & McDonald, 
2016) and the role of diplomacy in facilitating shared understanding 
and mutual trust (Salmoral et al., 2019). Rangachari & Verghese (2001) 
argue that the Indo-Nepalese water cooperation has failed due to trust 
deficit and the ‘small-country-big-country syndrome’, where security 
concerns play a critical role in agenda-setting and outcomes. Similarly,  
Iyer and Iyer (2003) highlight a high level of suspicion between India 
and Bangladesh due to pending political issues such as illegal migration 
that reduce the policy space to resolve other relevant issues such as 
water-sharing. Politics and diplomatic styles and mindset are also key 
factors that cements successful cooperation between countries. Biswas 
(2011) credits ‘enlightened leadership’ and ‘sustained political will’ as 
key attributes contributing to success over three decades of hydropower 
cooperation between India and Bhutan. Muni (2003) blames India’s 
colonial mindset and domineering diplomatic approach for its inability 
to forge long-term reliable political allies in the neighbourhood. 
Therefore, politics and diplomatic efforts, within the hubris of re­
lationships, associations and tensions, determine the existence of co­
operation or conflict between countries. 
The management of rivers is complicated by the fact that they cross 
international and domestic borders which makes resource allocation 
and sharing contentious. Most efforts in transboundary water co­
operation till date have largely focused on formal government-level 
diplomacy (Track 1) that is dominated by bureaucratic constraints 
(Barua & Vij, 2018; Barua et al., 2019) and is ignorant of the highly 
complex, political and plural nature of river basin politics, especially 
between countries with different size, polity, and governance structures 
(Salmoral et al., 2019). Additionally, national governments focus on 
finding technical solutions through water projects while ignoring im­
portant political goals such as promotion of international cooperation 
and peace building both within and between states (Pohl et al., 2014). 
India, being central to water politics in GBM river basins, is not only 
impacted by the interests of riparian countries, but also water politics at 
the sub-national level that diverts the attention for transboundary 
governance (Hanasz, 2015). Water is a state subject in India which 
gives state governments primary jurisdiction over the management of 
water resources, unlike the case of Bangladesh or Bhutan where na­
tional governments hold the primary responsibility for managing rivers 
(Barua et al., 2019). In recent years, the number of interstate river 
conflicts in India have risen significantly due to increasing water de­
mand, degradation of rivers, climate change impacts reducing river 
flows, and a populist form of politics that reflects the growing im­
portance of water, but also, a heightened state of insecurity around 
river-sharing (Tortajada et al., 2017). Subnational governments have 
resorted to extreme measures such as defying orders of interstate tri­
bunals and the Supreme Court with regard to water-sharing arrange­
ments with neighbouring states. Such tactics have sometimes interfered 
with negotiations at the transboundary level with the case of the West 
Bengal government’s interference in the India-Bangladesh water co­
operation over the Teesta river serving as a notable example5 . Simi­
larly, the state government of Sikkim was able to aggressively intervene 
in issues regarding the Teesta river-sharing between India and Ban­
gladesh (Prasai & Surie, 2013). As a result, bilateral discussions over the 
sharing of other transboundary rivers between India and Bangladesh 
(such as the Feni, Manu, Muhuri, etc.) have been slow. Therefore, re­
gional water cooperation is forestalled not only as a result of complex 
foreign relations, but also due to the way India’s federal structure de­
fines its jurisdiction over water (Tortajada et al., 2017). 
Similar to India, the fragmentation of domestic water governance in 
other BBIN countries has added to ambiguity and chaos over water 
allocation and usage, besides opening up avenues for future conflicts 
with respect to domestic and international waters. In Nepal, the as­
piration to become the region’s ‘energy basket’ has led to confining the 
nation’s water resources to the development of hydropower, followed 
by irrigation (Upadhyay & Gaudel, 2018). Internal disputes have arisen 
with regard to the equitable use of water resources among users of 
water for drinking, irrigation, and industry, particularly those that are 
dependent on the river such as white water rafting and ecotourism 
(Bhushal, 2019). Weakening of institutions such as the Water and En­
ergy Commission, which has the mandate to plan for the development 
of water and energy resources in Nepal, along with the historical 
burden of past negotiations with India has led to deep suspicion in the 
mind of Nepalis regarding the ability and intention of their political 
leaders to successfully lead negotiations on sharing of water and water- 
related benefits in the interest of Nepal (Upadhyay & Gaudel, 2018). 
Informal dialogue processes (known as ‘multi-track water diplomacy’) 
could allow open discussions among multiple stakeholders, address 
local and community-based conflicts, and help identify critical inter­
ventions points for building confidence and political support on water 
projects, but in their absence, disputes continue to intensify without a 
resolution (Huntjens & Man, 2017). 
In contrast to the water sector, there is greater consensus on energy 
cooperation, built by Track II diplomatic channels6 involving third 
party advocates such as the USAID, the World Bank, and the Asian 
Development Bank (ADB). The SARI/EI initiative has been quite 
