successful in bringing together key stakeholders of each of the BBIN 
countries, helping them identify issues and priorities, harmonize po­
licies and legislations, develop technical standards to facilitate trans­
mission connections, and create market rules for South Asia Regional 
Power Exchange through a participative approach (IRADe., 2017b). 
Similarly, the ADB has been consistently pushing for electricity sector 
expansion and regional energy cooperation by generating evidence on 
electricity-related potential and benefits (Rahman et al. 2012;  
Wijayatunga & Fernando, 2013). Energy development and trading is 
not seen as a zero sum game where individual countries are adversely 
impacted by the benefits attained by another country. Countries like 
Nepal and Bhutan recognize their economic self-interest in producing 
more electricity for domestic consumption and for sale (Dhungel, 2008; 
Tshering & Tamang, 2020 n.d.). India and Bangladesh, with their large 
appetite for energy, have the capacity to absorb excess energy from 
their neighbours. As a result, even though power is a concurrent subject 
in India, which gives equal right to the federal and state governments to 
formulate laws, policies and regulations on the subject, protest or in­
terference at the sub-national level has generally been limited in mat­
ters of transboundary energy cooperation. Similarly, in Nepal, the na­
tional discourse, driven in part by the Nepali private hydropower 
developers7, strongly endorses the essential nature of energy trade and 
cooperation to tackle its energy crisis and developmental challenges 
(Hydro Nepal., 2017), despite some export-centric projects drawing flak 
from some stakeholders (Dixit & Gyawali, 2010; Rest, 2012). Therefore, 
even though transboundary issues are generally viewed through the 
lens of national interest and perspective (Rasul, 2014), regional elec­
tricity trade is seen as a matter of comparative advantages and win-win 
opportunities (Singh, 2013; IRADe, 2018). 
5.2. India’s hegemonic role 
India is seen as a regional hegemon that follows different standards 
in its upstream and downstream negotiations with its neighbours, and 
hence, prefers bilateral dialogues and treaties, rather than a regional 
approach towards transboundary water management (Regmee, 2016). 
For Nepal and Bhutan, India is a downstream riparian country, usually 
seen as a weaker player, while in the case of Bangladesh, India lies 
upstream. In both scenarios, co-riparians have accused India of enga­
ging in inequitable and unjustified utilization of river water through 
measures such as entering agreements that gave India an unfair ad­
vantage (Gyawali & Dixit, 1999), or constructing hydropower projects 
or barrages depriving the lower riparian its fair share of water in the 
river basin (Hanasz, 2014, 2015; Hassan, 2019). Vij et al. (2020) point 
to India’s use of material resources (such as its geographical position, 
economic growth and military strength) and ideational tools (such as 
internal security, securitization narratives and domestic hydropower 
conflicts), to avoid discussion on relevant issues and maintain the status 
quo with Bangladesh on the sharing of the Brahmaputra river. The 
common perception that India continues to have an inordinate influ­
ence on agenda-setting, dialogue, and implementation of bilateral 
water agreements and treaties (Hassan, 2019) has given rise to mistrust, 
suspicion, and reluctance in the region (Swain, 2018), making it diffi­
cult for countries to engage on water cooperation. 
Not unlike its hegemonic role in water management, India’s ap­
proach to energy cooperation is established mostly due to its geo­
graphical and political centrality, and greater economic might. India is 
seen as a key player in discussions on energy co-operation, given its 
bilateral energy engagements with the other three BBIN countries, in 
addition to its potential role in facilitating a regional consortium 
(IRADe, 2018) to attract regional and sub-regional investment partners 
for energy cooperation (Lama, 2016). Several policy analysts 
(Wijayatunga & Fernando, 2013; IRADe., 2017a, 2018, Singh, 2013) 
point to the benefits of regional electricity trade in the region, including 
the potential to diminish the mismatch between energy demands and 
energy generation among the countries, and reduce fossil fuel reliance 
in India and Bangladesh while encouraging Bhutan and Nepal to exploit 
abundant water resources for hydropower, in effect, reducing overall 
carbon emission. Regional energy cooperation is also expected to en­
hance energy security for countries as energy from diverse sources can 
be mixed from interconnected electricity grids, besides ushering in 
opportunities for public-private partnerships. For India, an additional 
benefit is the opportunity to power its north-eastern region by way of a 
power corridor through Bangladesh. Given these incentives, India’s 
enthusiasm in pursuing these opportunities is not surprising. There is 
almost unanimous consensus that it would take India’s wholehearted 
involvement for the success of any regional energy cooperation in the 
BBIN region (Haran, 2018; Vaidya et al., 2019). 
5.3. Institutional frameworks for cooperation 
Absence of effective legal and institutional mechanisms that 
promptly address the issue of water management at the river basin level 
is a major reason for the lack of regional cooperation in water in South 
Asia (Bandyopadhyay, 2010; Prasai & Surie, 2013; Barua et al., 2018). 
Wherever joint institutional mechanisms exist, as in the case of India- 
Nepal and India-Bangadesh, important stakeholders, including other 
riparian countries and sub-national level players, are effectively ex­
cluded from negotiations (Scott et al., 2019). Additionally, problems 
are escalated due to country-specific management options (Salehin 
et al., 2011). The absence of a holistic, institutional approach in tran­
boundary water governance not only prevents the consideration of so­
cial and ecological dimesions, but also disregards the interests, in­
centives and stakes of relevant stakeholders, thereby increasing the 
potential for conflict. A case in point is the exclusion of the Indian states 
of Bihar and Uttar Pradesh from the Ganges river-sharing negotiations 
between India and Bangladesh. Even though the then-Indian Minister of 
Irrigation admitted that both the Indian states would be impacted by 
the treaty during the lean-flow season, Bihar and Uttar Pradesh were 
completely kept out of the bilateral discussions which lead to strong 
criticism and resentment, particularly in Bihar (The Daily Star., 2011;  
Hanasz, 2015). 
Wilder et al. (forthcoming) propose a stronger role for science in 
diplomatic processes around water and a need to combine water di­
plomacy with science diplomacy (termed as ‘hydrodiplomacy’), in­
cluding a shared understanding of vulnerability in complex trans­
boundary settings. This is particularly relevant in the context of the 
increasing complexity and uncertainty of climate and environmental 
change, the impacts of which will be felt unequally across the region 
and are likely to generate greater conflicts. Amongst the BBIN coun­
tries, institutional mechanisms are missing even with respect to basic 
data and information sharing at the transboundary and subnational 
level. Despite a few early flood warning systems between countries in 
Bhutan and India, Nepal and India and India and Bangladesh (Salehin 
et al., 2011), equitable distribution of water among riparian states in 
India and among the co-riparian countries is sometimes hindered by the 
lack of exchange of data on relevant aspects of hydrology, morphology, 
water pollution, and ecology, and other technical information required 
for dispute resolution (Tyagi et al., 2015). Despite the opportunities for 
use of information in transboundary water governance, right to in­
formation laws, absence of transparency, and securitized discourses on 
water have all affected access to information (Prasai & Surie, 2015). 
There are also challenges in achieving regional energy trade. Vaidya 
et al. (2019) mentions the absence of policies, institutions and grid 
interconnections as major obstacles in achieving regional energy co­
operation in BBIN region. Wijayatunga and Fernando (2013) point to­
wards the lack of availability of energy supply, energy trade 
