transmission infrastructures as key impediments to regional energy 
trade. Though some of these challenges were mentioned for the South 
Asia region in general, these are also applicable in particular for the 
BBIN countries. 
While the regional approach to water governance is almost non- 
existent, a strong basis for regional energy cooperation is provided by 
the SAARC Framework Agreement for Energy Cooperation in the 
electricity sector signed in 2014 during the 18th SAARC Summit in 
Kathmandu. Until recent times, no significant strides were made to­
wards regional energy cooperation due to intermingling of politics and 
economics in symbolically-important power trading guidelines issued 
by India (Haran, 2018). Released in December 2016, the guideline on 
cross-border electricity trade mentioned electricity as a commodity of 
‘strategic’ importance (Ministry of Power, 2016; Pillai & Prasai, 2018). 
A clause in the guideline providing preferential one-time approval for 
all entities with 51% or more Indian ownership wishing to export 
electricity from other countries to India became a point of controversy 
(Pun, 2018). These guidelines were later revised to accommodate 
concerns voiced by neighbouring countries (Ministry of Power, 2018). 
The release of technical guidelines was seen as a welcome first step 
towards formulating rules and regulations for the operation of a power 
exchange in the region, and making way for other legal, regulatory, 
operational and institutional frameworks facilitating an open access 
power regime. More recently, there has also been some movement to­
wards entering increasingly complex multilateral trading arrangements 
with India agreeing to facilitate transfer of power from Nepal to Ban­
gladesh, and Bhutan to Bangladesh through its energy grid (Rijal, 
2019). Such regulatory and oversight mechanisms and institutions are 
either entirely absent from transboundary water governance, or prac­
tically non-functional. 
5.4. Perceptions: Water as national security, and energy as a commodity 
The discourse on water in South Asia is often centred around a 
perception of crisis, with its shortage seen as an existential threat to 
national security. These fears are further elevated due to the actual 
reduction in surface water flows, groundwater levels and surface water 
quality, and expanding water needs in the region corresponding to a 
rapidly growing population, with climate change acting as a threat 
multiplier (Gomez, 2018). This makes it easier to mobilize the popular 
imagination towards a narrow and highly politicized view of water. 
Water is seen as a zero-sum game where one beneficiary can benefit 
only through the loss of another (Hanasz, 2015; Bandyopadhyay et al., 
2020). Such an approach invariably makes partners feel vulnerable to 
exploitation making cooperation harder to achieve. Securitisation of the 
water discourse and knowledge on the Brahmaputra is serious to the 
extent that there have been no bilateral treaties so far (Prasai & Surie, 
2015; Vij et al., 2020). In some cases, the threat perception is so sig­
nificant that no transboundary cooperation or dialogue is sought, 
leading to a violation, in principle, of international practice and norms 
in utilization of international rivers. For instance, India’s National River 
Linking Project (NRLP), a gigantic water transfer programme, is meant 
to link the eastern Himalayan rivers (i.e. the Ganges and the Brahma­
putra) and the Peninsular rivers through construction of large storage 
projects and canals. This project is fundamentally conceptualized on the 
idea of scarcity. India’s plan to transfer ‘surplus’ water in the north and 
northeast parts of the country to the water ‘scarce’ areas of western and 
southern India has greatly heightened the anxiety of Nepal and Ban­
gladesh due to a threat of unilateral water diversion by India leading to 
environmental, economic, and other losses for riparian countries (Joshi, 
2013; Ghosh, 2019). 
The energy narrative in the region is also securitized and plays a 
role in informing policy choices in the BBIN countries; however, the 
framing and degree of securitization varies substantially. First, there is 
a shared commitment by national governments to transition towards 
renewable and clean energy sources, commonly seen as a solution to the 
challenge of climate change, which itself is closely linked to national 
security. Hydropower has emerged as a favourite option for energy 
trading between BBIN countries as it meets the stated objectives of 
furthering energy security, besides compensating for the intermittency 
of renewables like solar and wind. Second, technical vulnerabilities of 
national-level power sectors, characterized by weak and inadequate 
power grid, poor power generation, underinvestment and delays in 
sectoral reforms, and lack of electricity transmission and distribution 
systems, have given rise to a specific conception of energy security that 
is centred around the idea of exchange, interdependence, diversifica­
tion, and abundance. As a result, electricity is seen as a commodity for 
sale, use and exchange, rather than as a source of security risk. 
Additionally, source diversification (multiple forms of electricity gen­
eration and transmission pathways) offers flexibility and positive re­
dundancy for energy that is less evident for water cooperation. 
6. The way forward and key messages 
Regional energy cooperation in the BBIN region, while fraught with 
