"standing successes, breakthroughs, and failures in the formulation 
and adoption of technical guidelines, policies and institutions for reg­
ulation and management of water resources, including maintaining and 
extending information-sharing networks (Scott et al., 2019). Merging 
the traditional silos of science and diplomacy (Wilder et al., forth­
coming), and following a participative approach that is founded on 
strong institutions and policies and involves relevant stakeholders, in­
cluding previously-neglected riparian countries and sub-national 
players, will help overcome mutual suspicion that has been generated 
through past events. Stepping up cooperation in areas such as flood 
control, disaster mitigation, irrigation and navigation through a multi- 
track water diplomacy framework while following a scientific, adaptive 
approach to learning (Varady et al., 2016) can help integrate the 
multiple dimensions and interests of different stakeholders, and im­
prove the trust deficit amongst countries (The Hague Institute for 
Global Justice., 2019). 
Second, India must recognize the opportunities and benefits of 
playing a more constructive and demonstrated leadership role in the 
region, given its geographical and geopolitical centrality. Consistent 
engagement through negotiations and a strict policy on executing 
projects and fulfilling commitments could have a ‘demonstration effect’ 
on partner countries while serving to enhance India’s credibility. 
Notwithstanding progress made in energy cooperation and incipient 
efforts in transboundary water governance, India appears to be bent on 
deepening the trust deficit through current policies such as the 
Citizenship Amendment Act (CAA), 20198, which it has declared to be 
an ‘internal matter’, despite various objections raised at regional and 
global levels (Tripathi, 2020; Haidar & Bhattacharjee, 2020). Such 
measures are built on a traditional, heavily ‘securitized’ idea of borders 
and legal/illegal migrants, without taking into account the fluid inter­
connections of natural resources, cultures, ethnicities, societies, trade 
and commerce, tourism, technology, roads, and communication that 
exist across the border regions (Lama, 2016). In a heavily politicized 
8 The controversial Indian Act promises to fast-track citizenship for un­
documented migrants belonging to six faiths excluding Islam and Judaism, who 
fled from religious persecution in Bangladesh, Afghanistan and Pakistan before 
December 21, 2014. 
U. Saklani, et al.   
Environm
ental Science and Policy 114 (2020) 22–34
31
environment, water discourse and policies are likely to be crafted and 
perceived through a jaundiced lens. For cross-border linkages to be 
successfully established and functional, there is a need to give due ac­
knowledgement to shared interests and development potential, while 
addressing concerns and anxieties of India’s neighbours. 
While there is little empirical evidence on the effect of water-energy 
issue linkage (Poast, 2013), a simultaneous discussion of water and 
energy issues, either informally during diplomatic negotiations, or 
formally embedded in treaties and agreements, can provide an oppor­
tunity to BBIN countries to highlight potential gains of cooperation and 
interstate interdependencies, and increase the probability of states to 
reach, and stay committed to, a negotiated agreement. The water-en­
ergy-food nexus approach that focuses on trade-offs and synergies si­
milarly aims to encourage greater policy coherence and coordination, 
besides enhancing resource use efficiency (Rasul & Sharma, 2016). 
Regional cooperation can give a strong impetus to nations for ad­
vancing structural reforms, building institutions and capacity, devel­
oping a shared knowledge base, bridging infrastructural gaps, attracting 
private sector participation, and addressing poverty alleviation goals 
including job creation. Rather than being seen as ends in themselves, 
regional cooperation in energy and water and the attendant unlocking 
of investments must be viewed as means to broader, more ambitious 
end goals focusing on human development. 
